KANT_TR['wiener/guyer'] = {"wiener-s01-001":["Kant's by a society of auditors",[]],"wiener-s01-002":["Table of contents",[]],"wiener-s01-003":["*Prolegomena*",[]],"wiener-s01-004":["790",[]],"wiener-s01-005":["History of logic",[]],"wiener-s01-006":["796",[]],"wiener-s01-007":["*Tractatio Logices*",[]],"wiener-s01-008":["805- 940",[]],"wiener-s01-009":["On cognition",[]],"wiener-s01-010":["805",[]],"wiener-s01-011":["On assent and the withholding of assent",[]],"wiener-s01-012":["859",[]],"wiener-s01-013":["On probable cognition",[]],"wiener-s01-014":["879",[]],"wiener-s01-015":["On concepts",[]],"wiener-s01-016":["904",[]],"wiener-s01-017":["Short repetition of the foregoing",[]],"wiener-s01-018":["908",[]],"wiener-s01-019":["Division of all definitions",[]],"wiener-s01-020":["913",[]],"wiener-s01-021":["On logical division",[]],"wiener-s01-022":["925",[]],"wiener-s01-023":["On judgments",[]],"wiener-s01-024":["928",[]],"wiener-s01-025":["On inferences of the understanding",[]],"wiener-s01-026":["938",[]],"wiener-s01-027":["On inferences of reason",[]],"wiener-s01-028":["939",[]],"wiener-s01-030":["[Wiener Logik]",[]],"wiener-s02-001":["Everything in the world happens according to rules, just as we perceive this in corporeal nature, so we find it even in the exercise of our own powers, although we are not once conscious of the rules. One arrives at this exercise through mere attempts, and precisely from these attempts, e.g., to teach someone to speak or to walk, one can gather the multitude of rules. In this way we can learn many rules from ourselves. Grammar consists of infinite rules. For this reason, too, some have held language to be divine inspiration. Meanwhile so much is certain, that all languages can be brought together, according to their first principles, into one grammar. Otherwise grammar is of course a doctrine of the understanding. For as our soul combines concepts: so must the words also be combined. It is too abstract for the capacity of the school. Just take the abstract rules of *genere Substantivo* etc. These rules, according to which our powers act, let us investigate more closely. The understanding is the faculty of rules itself, and the understanding alone can examine these rules. According to which rules does it do this? This is difficult to find for this reason, because it, when it itself errs, cannot state the correctness of the rules according to which it proceeds.",[]],"wiener-s02-002":["All rules of the use of our powers are either absolutely necessary, or conditionally necessary. Without the first no use of the understanding at all takes place, without the contingent ones the use of the understanding will cease in a certain respect. The necessary ones must be so constituted that they hold, without distinction of the objects, of the understanding. They must concern merely the form of the understanding.",[]],"wiener-s02-003":["Remark. In all thinking there is matter and form. Matter concerns the object, and form the manner of treatment. Thus physiology and psychology are distinguished according to matter. According to form they can, if they are treated empirically, be the same. Our senses give the cognition to the matter. In the understanding lies [erg. nicht] the intuition. Words are the matter, but grammar the form of language. A science, therefore, that occupies itself with the form of the understanding is called logic.",[]],"wiener-s02-004":["Our understanding has various objects of cognition and of science, such as history, mathematics – but general logic abstracts from all this content, from all difference of cognition, and considers in everything only the form of concepts, of judgments and of inferences. In short, it is one of the sciences that prepare us for others.",[]],"wiener-s02-005":["Προπαιδευτική (*propositiones*). Every human being observes the rule before he can bring it into formulas. Gradually, however, he attends to what he does. The sum total of all these rules is called *Logica naturalis*. The science that expounds the same rules systematically, *artificialis*. It is affected to apply the name of a science to this natural propensity. Thus one could also have a natural optics, mechanics –. One divides, as was said above, logic into *naturalis* and *artificialis*. This division is bad for this reason, because logic is supposed to be the sum total of the rules of the understanding which we exercise without being conscious of it. But since we do not know these rules: it cannot be a science. Consequently this is a *contradiction*. *Logica naturalis* is the knowledge of the rules of the understanding *in concreto*; *Logica artificialis in abstracto*.",[]],"wiener-s02-006":["With us, therefore, only *Logica artificialis* is always called logic. We can communicate the laws of our understanding in the following way",[]],"wiener-s02-007":["1. Rules of how we think.",[]],"wiener-s02-008":["2. Rules of how we ought to think.",[]],"wiener-s02-009":["Sometimes we think entirely wrongly. This use can never agree with the rules. This is the misuse of the understanding, and is excluded here. Logic teaches the latter, namely to use the objective rules of our understanding. Logic has this particular feature, that the subjective laws are also the objective rules, because the universal rules are the sole condition of our thinking. One sometimes gives assent to a thing out of habit or inclination. This is no universal law. Consequently the subjective laws of willing cannot be objective laws of the understanding. Some logicians presuppose psychology in logic. Since this is an empirical science: there would arise from this a science of how we think under the various hindrances, not how we ought to think. They would be nothing but contingent and natural laws. But that is not what we are asking about. Logical rules must be taken from the necessary use of the understanding. In a grammar we consider the universal rules without which no thing at all can exist.",[]],"wiener-s02-010":["There is a twofold cognition. One *a priori*, which is independent of experience; and one *a posteriori*, which is grounded on empirical principles. Now since experience teaches us nothing but contingent things, but general logic abstracts from all content, and consequently rests on *principiis a priori*: it is counted among the *scientiis rationalibus*. Logi c is so called on a twofold ground:",[]],"wiener-s02-011":["1.) It is drawn from reason.",[]],"wiener-s02-012":["2.) It has reason as its object.",[]],"wiener-s02-013":["But into the *definition* there properly belongs only that it is rational according to the object.",[]],"wiener-s02-014":["The end in every science is either to extend our cognitions, or to clarify them. Logic only clarifies, because it considers the form, which does not let itself be extended. A universal prescription resting on grounds *a priori* is called *Canon*. Logic is so called because it rests on rules *a priori*. It is a canon of all thinking, morals a canon of all willing. – [ Textlücke: AA: Die ] Logic is a rational [ Textlücke: AA: -e Wissenschaft] a canon for the understanding. Just as grammar is solely for the judging of language according to form. Words are the matter. *Organon* is the name of a science that extends our knowledge. Logic cannot be so called, because it abstracts from all content. Just as little can it be an [ Textlücke: AA: Kunst ], since this is an organon of a knowledge. Some object the art of making definitions. But these must still always be very much examined, and taste [ Textlücke: AA: zu ] judge according to laws of the senses has no canon, because it derives *a posteriori*. Logic and aesthetics are therefore distinguished through the difference of the objects.",[]],"wiener-s02-015":["Definition. *Logica est scientia regularum universalium usus intellectus*. Now if logic is supposed to be a science of the universal laws of the understanding; then these must be necessary rules, because they are supposed to have relation to everything without distinction of the objects, and without them nothing at all can be thought. All necessary rules must be derived *a priori*. Rules of experience are *a posteriori*. Heaviness is to be met with in the whole world. But I will never have insight that all bodies are heavy until I have insight into the necessity of heaviness *a priori*. A science that rests on *principiis a priori* is called a demonstrated science. Experience cannot be demonstrated, because we must first prove its necessity.",[]],"wiener-s02-016":["Logic is therefore a demonstrated science. A science that can be taught from *principiis a priori* is called *Doctrina*, consequently logic too. If the rule of judgment precedes the judging: then it is called *Doctrina*. If the judging precedes, critique. H Hence critique [ Textlücke ] is called *Doctrina*, according as the rule or judging precedes. For if I now once have the rule of experience: then I can also judge it. We now ask: what is logic, a critique or doctrine? Because it is the touchstone, and is supposed to go before our understanding: it is a doctrine. Critical judging always presupposes a doctrine. Logic is therefore not a critique at all. But it serves for it. –",[]],"wiener-s02-017":["One divides logic into 2. parts.",[]],"wiener-s02-018":["1.) Analytic,",[]],"wiener-s02-019":["2.) Dialectic.",[]],"wiener-s02-020":["1.) Analytic or logic as critique of cognition with regard to truth is that part which through the dissection of our understanding presents the universal rule of the form of our understanding, hence the necessary rules of all truth. It is consequently the mere canon of the judging of the understanding.",[]],"wiener-s02-021":["2.) Dialectic is the misuse of this canon, when we make it into an organon, and want to cognize truth from it. Analytic is the art of separating with regard to our judgments. It has a great illusion that we, through the canon of the judging of the understanding, since it [ Textlücke ] is correct according to form, can cognize truth. But since the analytic abstracts from all content, and considers nothing further than the form: it can be no organon of new truths. The dialecticians were among the ancients those who disputed *pro et contra*. They were sophists, and sought to deceive. They assumed false propositions, accommodated everything to the formal laws of the understanding, and consequently gave themselves a great illusion of truth. It is possible to speak in the logical form of a thing of which one understands nothing, namely through the heaping of inferences upon inferences, and the like – and thereby the hearer is deceived. Thus logic is misused as art, and becomes organon, but not of truth. As long as this art flourished, logic was nothing other than a cultivation of dialectic as art. Still a century ago the professor was the best who expounded dialectic as art. We call dialectic a means by which one can cognize that something is contrary to the formal laws of the understanding. It is consequently only a means of purification. We are subjected through the senses to illusion in our judgments, inferences, etc. Further there are certain means of truth which one can imitate without otherwise having anything in common with truth. E.g., an author who imitates the form of thoroughness, but otherwise expounds untruths. Through these skills we are often deceived when we are ignorant of the content. Agreement with the logical rules is of course *conditio sine qua non*. But this is not sufficient to find truth. How dialectic arises one can see in schools. A school *chria* is full of [ Textlücke ] words without content.",[]],"wiener-s02-022":["Our author believes dialectic is logic of probability. But this is a judgment about truth according to correct but insufficient grounds. But if its grounds are correct: then it belongs to the analytic. Dialectic is the logic of illusion, where it is taken for empty. The application of logic can happen only to objects. But general logic deals without distinction of the objects. Consequently the canon must be theoretical. But general logic cannot be applied also for this reason, because practical logic presupposes knowledge and sciences, but general logic can presuppose no sciences, because it is a propaedeutic.",[]],"wiener-s02-023":["For a practical part, as our author thinks, there is not in logic. Otherwise dialectic would be a pure illusion. For a practical logic, as thus of the art of education, there cannot be. For this already presupposes study [der ] all [ Textlücke: AA: -gemeinen Logic ]. But all sciences are *praxis* of logic, because without logic no thing can make progress. Logic must therefore be divided not into the theoretical and practical part, but into the dogmatic and technical part. The dogmatic is the canon . The technical is the prescription of the rules of the school. General logic is supposed to consider the form of the understanding. Hence it abstracts from all speculation, and considers the logic of universal human reason. Here it departs entirely from sciences. Logic is divided, like all sciences that have their concepts *a priori*, into the logical doctrine of elements and doctrine of method. The doctrine of elements is the *Dialectica* or analytic of reason, or theoretical logic. For it is propaedeutic.",[]],"wiener-s02-024":["The doctrine of method is the logic of the form of a system of cognitions. One can draft a particular doctrine of method for one or another science. And this is then the technical logic, or the organon. This organon can come only at the end of a science, because only then do I know the nature of the science. It is the completion of the rules to their perfection, which contains all *terminos technicos* by which we distinguish the logical directions in critique. It is what in oratory the chapter on metaphors, metonymies, etc. is. It considers not so much the form of the understanding as rather the form of systems, and is for the most part of dialectical content. It speaks only of different kinds of the form of the system . In art one uses manner, in science method. In the former I act according to examples, in the latter according to grounds. It contains the sum total of all formal rules of the use of the understanding.",[]],"wiener-s02-025":["One has a logic of common reason, and a logic of speculative reason. We can have the faculty of cognizing rules *in concreto*, but not all *in abstracto*. The faculty of cognition of rules *in concreto* is the common understanding, the healthy understanding, insofar as it is correct. The cognition of rules *in concreto*, or when we judge according to an experience, according to an example, is the go-cart of the immature. It is necessary to know rules *in abstracto*, and to cognize how far these rules extend. The healthy understanding serves, a nd to think healthily and correctly. If this limits itself only to what is grounded on experience: then it will never overreach itself. But if one wants to have not only a healthy but also a perfect understanding: then one must possess a speculative understanding. For there is a great multitude of concepts that are too high for the healthy understanding, but which it nevertheless needs. The logic of speculative reason is therefore the faculty of having insight into rules *in abstracto*.",[]],"wiener-s02-026":["The exposition in a science looks not so much to the intelligibility of the object as rather to the activity of the subject. This can be twofold in logic.",[]],"wiener-s02-027":["1.) Scholastic. When it is appropriate to the desire for knowledge for the sake of cultivation de re r, who want to treat this knowledge as science. A science requires proofs, explanations, etc.",[]],"wiener-s02-028":["2.) Popular for the benefit of those who do not make a science of it, but only want to tidy up their understanding through it, this is very beneficial for the common weal, but requires much genius, because popular logic belongs among the descending kinds of knowledge. The ancients had more popularity in their exposition, because they spoke before the people. Among the moderns the French have brought it the furthest in this. Yet the scholastic presentation is the foundation of the popular, since it is the foundation of all sciences, and the latter is only as it were an ornament of it.",[]],"wiener-s03-001":["Logic comes fromλόγος (*sermo*), and has the meaning of reason. Epicurus called it *Canonicon* a science of the cautious and correct use of the understanding. Aristotle can be regarded as the father of logic. Yet his logic is too scholastic, full of subtleties, and has at bottom not been of much use to the human understanding. It is a dialectic, and an organon of the art of disputation. In his organon there is much acuteness. From him are all our logical terminologies. Otherwise it points to mythology and hair-splitting, and is banished from the schools. Otherwise one has still retained the main ideas from it, and that comes from this, that logic occupies itself with no object, and hence it is soon to be exhausted. His logic flourished for many centuries in all schools, until Petrus Ramus first wrote a logic in 2 parts, of which",[]],"wiener-s03-002":["1. *de Inventione*,",[]],"wiener-s03-003":["2. *de Iudicio* deals.",[]],"wiener-s03-004":["Through his refutations of Aristotle he drew upon himself a deadly hatred. After them come Malebranche and Locke. The latter wrote a treatise *de intellectu humano*. But both writings deal not so much with the form of the understanding as with the content. They are preliminary exercises for metaphysics. Among the moderns Leibniz and Wolff are to be noted. Wolff's logic is the best that one meets with. It was afterwards concentrated by Baumgarten, and this was extended again by Meier. After them Reusch and Knutzen wrote logics. Reusch is a Jena philosopher. At just this time there appeared in France *Récherches de la vérité*. The logic of Crusius is stuffed full of things that are drawn from other sciences, and contains metaphysical and theological principles. Lambert wrote an organon of pure reason. It is remarkable that of a science, even if it has almost come to its perfection, one can give no *praecise definition*. The cause is because our reason can develop its ends on account of their truth only late. But in the end a science thereby receives its right perfection. Since logic is a propaedeutic of all philosophical sciences: the concept of philosophy must be fixed here – Cognitions of reason from concepts are philosophical cognitions. Cognitions of reason from the construction of concepts are mathematical cognitions. The system of the former is called philosophy, of the latter mathematics. Cognition of reason is opposed to historical cognition. A cognition is historical according to form if it is a cognition *ex datis*. Cognition of reason is a cognition *ex principiis*, which has been drawn from grounds *a priori*.",[]],"wiener-s03-005":["A cognition can have arisen from reason. But the way in which I cognize it is nevertheless historical, if namely I only acquire it as it was given to me. E.g., the polyhistor who studies the philosophy of the ancients. Here the cognition is *objective* a cognition of reason, but *subjective* historical. It is two different things, to learn philosophy, and to learn to philosophize. It is one of the greatest errors in instruction if one has systems of philosophy of an author learned by heart without letting one judge about the author. It is therefore necessary to use more reason in the method of reason. Many philosophers therefore boast of nothing but the imitation of another reason.",[]],"wiener-s03-006":["One commonly believes mathematics and philosophy have two different objects, philosophy is a cognition of quality, and mathematics a cognition of quantity. We assert that philosophy and mathematics, in particular the first, go to all objects. In philosophy magnitudes are spoken of just as much as in mathematics. The specific difference of these two sciences is that philosophy is a cognition of reason from concepts, mathematics a cognition of reason from the *construction* of concepts. Construction from concepts is when I present a concept *a priori* in intuition. Through philosophy from concepts one can have insight into nothing in mathematics. But with intuition one has insight *ex principiis* into the *construction* of this or that proposition. Mathematics therefore has a great advantage over philosophy, since all its cognitions are intuitive, those of philosophy on the other hand",[]],"wiener-s03-007":["discursive. The cause why in mathematics we always consider only magnitudes is because magnitudes alone can be constructed. Qualities cannot be constructed, but only cognized from concepts. The difference of mathematics therefore rests not so much on the objects as on the form. Philosophy is the system of philosophical cognition. Here I leave out speculative, for cognition of reason under a system must be thoroughly speculative. For system is from *principiis a priori*. This is philosophy in the scholastic sense. But one also has a philosophy according to a *conceptu cosmico*, and then it is a science of the final ultimate ends of human reason. Speculative cognition of reason is distinguished from common by this: the former is cognition of reason according to rules *in abstracto*, the latter according to rules *in concreto*. Cognition of reason according to rules *in concreto* is the popular manner of exposition, which is appropriate to the common man. If it is correct: then it is healthy reason. Philosophical cognition is speculative reason -5 °cognition from concepts. Mathematical are cognitions of reason from the *construction* of concepts. A concept I can document, that is, I can give an intuition of a true concept, if I document it *a posteriori*. But the mathematician documents his concepts *a priori*. E.g., if I have a circle that is not quite correct: then I can nevertheless demonstrate. For the representation already lies in me. Scholastic philosophy is an instruction in skill, the true one a doctrine of wisdom, which must be the highest good of our striving. It is the legislation of reason. One can distinguish artifices of reason from the legislation of reason. The artist of reason, or the philodox, as Socrates calls him, is he who arranges reason for all arbitrary ends. The mathematician is an artist of reason, likewise the logician and the physicist. In the end one nevertheless sees that philosophy as doctrine of wisdom is necessary. Philosophy is therefore an idea of the most perfect legislation of the human understanding, and the philosopher the one versed in the laws of human reason. Now since this title is so sublime: no one can well with right let himself be called a philosopher. The cause why one esteems highly someone who arranges his actions according to the strictest laws of morality, and never leaves the straight path, is perhaps because in the end morals is nevertheless always the end toward which all speculations go out. It constitutes a unity of the entire cognition of reason, and he who follows its rules can alone be called philosopher. Where does the name philosophy come from? It was first ascribed to Pythagoras. Not as teacher of wisdom, but as the high concept which he had of the wisdom of the sublime God.",[]],"wiener-s03-008":["To philosophy *in sensu scholastico* belong two things. 1.) a sufficient store of cognitions of reason. 2.) a correct interconnection of them, or a system. For a system is the interconnection of many cognitions according to an idea. Our historical knowledge is of use for this, that our reason can make a purposive use of it. Since the ends are subordinated to one another: there must be higher ends, and thus there arises among these ends a unity, or a system of ends. The true worth of our use of reason can be determined only through the interconnection which this knowledge has with the final ends. It is therefore a science of wisdom. If we call the inner *principium* of the choice of the different ends maxims: then we can say that philosophy *in sensu cosmico* is a science of the highest maxims of the use of our reason. And here the philosopher marks himself more through the maxim of his way of thinking than through the interconnection of his cognitions. Philosophy in the school concept (philodoxy) is an organon of skill, and the philodox is related to the philosopher as he who carries on a trade in the state to the legislator. If the philosopher is to cognize the interconnection of all cognitions of reason with the final ends, he must determine",[]],"wiener-s03-009":["1. the sources of human knowledge,",[]],"wiener-s03-010":["2. the beginning of their use,",[]],"wiener-s03-011":["3. their boundaries. This is one of the most difficult, but also of the most sublime things in philosophy, which at present few have yet attained.",[]],"wiener-s03-012":["And philosophy *in sensu cosmico* is for this reason also called philosophy *in sensu eminenti*. Philosophy cannot be learned, because every philosopher erects his own building on the ruins of another, and if a system were actually given to me that was so clear that it also always contained incontrovertible propositions: I would nevertheless be no philosopher if I learned all its propositions by heart. I would then not learn to philosophize, but possess a historical cognition, without knowing the sources from which it was drawn. One can also say here just what one can say in jurisprudence: *Legis peritus* of the supreme laws of reason is the true philosopher. *Leguleii* of the supreme laws of reason are sophists and dialecticians, who give themselves a certain illusion of wisdom, and with this seek to push through certain ends by force.",[]],"wiener-s03-013":["The human being needs two pieces in philosophy:",[]],"wiener-s03-014":["1. A cultivation of our skill. This is necessary for this reason, because for all ends we need a facility in the use of all means to these ends. Without knowledge one can just as little be a philosopher as one becomes a philosopher through mere knowledge. The best *principien* of our way of thinking have no durability if science of wisdom does not secure them. Innocence is lovable, but not secured against seduction, unless a teaching about all that can lure, and about the origin of the errors into which it can fall, is added. He who has a hatred against all sciences, and *praetendirt* that wisdom alone ought to be highly esteemed, is called a misologist. Epicurus is blamed that he wanted to bring forth a doctrinal concept of wisdom whereby all sciences would be given up, and misology introduced. Misology commonly arises from this, that someone feels himself empty of all such skills. But there is also a misology among those who have extensive knowledge, and here it arises from this, because this knowledge had not been able to satisfy them enough. Philosophy alone can procure this inner satisfaction. It closes the circle, and then it sees how all knowledge hangs together in one edifice in a regular way toward such ends as are appropriate to humanity.",[]],"wiener-s03-015":["2. In that we consider philosophy as skill, we will look more to its method than to the aim toward which it is directed. To the supreme maxims we will of course also direct our gaze. But since we are guided through the method as to how we ought to learn to philosophize, and consequently philosophy sets the human mind in the greatest freedom: it deserves the greatest attention. No people in the world began to philosophize earlier than the Greeks, since no people thought through concepts, but all through images. They first began to study rules *in abstracto*. Which people has ever investigated the concepts of virtue, of the highest good? The Egyptian",[]],"wiener-s03-016":["wisdom is nothing but child's play compared with the Greek, and more recent authors have proved that the Egyptians had many surveyors, who measured out the field according to a certain assumed measure, but understood nothing of mathematics. The Greeks were the first originators of mathematics, who demonstrated it from the first grounds and elements. They are the kernel of the human race and its benefactors. Inthe Greek history it is obscure where philosophy arose. The Thracians seem to be the most insightful and oldest. The fragmentists among them are Orpheus and Mus aeus. The wise men in Greece had nothing but sentences and moral sayings, which were long since known among the people. Sentences are thoughts summed up in a familiar expression, in order to impress them more conveniently on memory. One among them is remarkable, and from him all philosophy reckons itself, Thales, who on account of his knowledge of natural science is also called *Physicus*. He is the originator of the Ionian sect. His pupils are Anaximander and Anaximenes. It is remarkable that the language of poets was the first in which things that are objects of reason were expressed, so that philosophy is much hindered by poetry. Pherecydes was the first prosaic philosopher, and soon after him Heraclitus. Their writings seemed very obscure to everyone, because at that time philosophy was still entirely new. The language of poets is astonishingly rich in ideas. That is proved by the works of Homer, Hesiod. But at that time the ideas were not yet made free of the corporeal secondary meanings. After these followed the Eleatic sect. Its founder was Xenophanes, and after him Parmenides. They taught in the senses is nothing but deception, and only in the understanding is truth. Here philosophy already clears up very much, and frees itself entirely from the poets. Zeno, a man of great acuteness, belongs to this sect. – Under the name dialectic was at that time understood the pure use of the understanding. Hence there are among the ancients great eulogies of dialectic. One counted among it the doctrine of the highest good, of the being thinking in us, etc. If the understanding frees itself from the guiding thread of the senses: then it runs the danger of losing its way in labyrinths. Hence it is comprehensible how such a use of the understanding could have fallen into nothing but subtleties. From these arose the present name of dialectic, which the pedants know well how to use. At the time of the Ionian sect Pythagoras set up in *magna Graecia* the Italic school. He is among all philosophers the only one who had something of his own. He was a great *Mathematicus*, and knew how to bring music under mathematical laws. He founded a society of philosophers who were bound to one another through secrecy. Geometrical doctrines he expounded only to his friends. This philosophy spread through the whole land. Their essential pieces that they taught were probably to give more moderate concepts of the gods than the people had, and to set something against the machinations of the princes. It was in fact a kind of freemasonry. Finally they came into such repute that all cities requested their regents from Pythagoras, who lived in Crotona, from among his pupils. – He believed the transmigration of souls, yet one cannot say much about it, because he had nothing but secrets. – Played with numbers. At this time a man arose in Greece who opened among the speculative minds an entirely new course, how one should indeed attain to the highest good, Socrates, whom Xenophon describes as one who comes nearest to the idea of a wise man. He possessed a great skill in driving the dialecticians into a corner through questions and answers, this one calls the irony of Socrates. His pupil was Plato, and his pupil Aristotle. The former cultivated the practical part of the Socratic philosophy, and the latter brought speculative philosophy to greater perfection. Now follow the moral philosophers. The Epicureans placed all virtue in an ever cheerful heart. The Stoics placed virtue in a renunciation of all enjoyment of life, and in the loftiness of the soul. Although the Epicureans taught many erroneous things: they were nevertheless the best natural philosophers among all the Greeks.",[]],"wiener-s03-017":["The most eminent schools6 * of the Greeks had particular names. – The Academy of Plato was not a single building, but an open place under the open sky near the most excellent buildings. It divides into 3 parts.",[]],"wiener-s03-018":["1. the Academy of Speusippus, who still remained a faithful pupil of Plato, and taught entirely dogmatically.",[]],"wiener-s03-019":["2: ......... Arcesilaus. This one was a mere doubter. Hence it comes that the *Academici* are also called *Sceptici*. Plato expounded his doctrine dialogically, and in such a way that he then did not decide, but let disputing occur on both sides. Now the method easily finds assent which suspends the matter, and makes it probable that it could be otherwise.",[]],"wiener-s03-020":["3. ....... of Carneades, who likewise was an extremely subtle and dialectical doubter.",[]],"wiener-s03-021":["*Lycaeum* or *Gymnasium* was the school of Aristotle, and because he commonly walked about with his pupils in the place, they are also called *peripatetici*. He had no famous pupils. Theophrastus and Demetrius Phalereus wrote no books, and also did not extend Aristotle at all. *Porticus* was a covered walk, a picture gallery, and the school of the sect of the Stoics. These were in speculative philosophy for the most part dialectical, but in morals very practical. Their founder was Zeno of Citium. His most famous pupils are Cleanthes and Chrysippus. It laid the ground for the most magnanimous principles in the world, although their doctrines are idealistic, and too high for practice.",[]],"wiener-s03-022":["*Horti*, the school of Epicurus. These disputed with the Stoics, and were finally displaced by them, because everyone after all esteemed such lofty principles highly. Lucretius's book *de natura rerum* is the only one that is written according to their principles. Besides the *Scepticis academicis* the skepticism of Pyrrho is also noteworthy. ( H — —) The *Dogmatici* asserted truth could be proved exactly. This one asserted that philosophy consists in the equilibrium of our judgment, and in the skill of being able to uncover all false illusion, without otherwise being able to prove anything decisive. But since human beings are more anxious than they have desire to tear down: this sect found little approval. Sextus Empiricus is noteworthy because he collected all these doubts.",[]],"wiener-s03-023":["When the philosophy of the Greeks came to the Romans: it did not increase in the least. Cicero adheres in speculative philosophy to Plato, in practical to the *Porticus*. In natural science Plinius *secundus* alone accomplished something excellent.",[]],"wiener-s03-024":["When the Roman Empire had been overthrown by the barbarians: all culture vanished, until the Arabs spread in the 10th *Seculo* in the Occident, and began to cultivate the sciences. All philosophy was taken from Aristotle, whom one followed in a slavish way. In the 10th *Seculo* the scholastic school arose; which made nothing but commentaries on Aristotle. Since Aristotle, as is well known, worked on nothing but speculative reason: one dwelt solely on the astonishing subtleties in logic and metaphysics. Around the time of the Reformation the remnant of the scholastics was swept out. Immediately after the scholastics there arose as *Sceptici*: Huetius, Bayle, Hume, whom one called *antilogicos*. Then *eclectici* came up, i.e., philosophers who adhered to no sect in particular. This improvement is to be ascribed to no circumstance more than to the *Studio* of nature, with which one combined mathematics. Hereby order in thinking was furthered. To this the Lord Chancellor Bacon of Verulam contributed in his *Organon* of the sciences, in that he drew attention to the method in physics, namely to observations and experiments. Cartesius was the first who took this path. Guidance has been given to speculative reason by Leibniz in Germany, and Locke in England, in that they sought to purify it of all scholasticism, and to bring everything to distinct concepts. – No philosopher probably ever had such an extensive skill to philosophize dogmatically as Leibniz. It is dangerous for this reason, because in our dogmatic propositions there is much that is false on account of the illusion of experience. Hence it is better to criticize the truth. – Locke treats philosophy psychologically, i.e., as an anatomist of the human cognitive powers. From his time one began in our fatherland to study the human soul. In our times natural philosophy flourishes to the highest degree. In morals we are not yet much further than the ancients. Some posit it in the will, others in self-love etc. etc. as with the ancients. As regards metaphysics, we are taken aback, after we have gone through all the parts, and there sets in among us a kind of indifferentism toward this study on account of the extensive difficulties. *Status anceps*. This is the age of critique for this study, and the point in time is near when its edifice will be torn down, and an entirely new one will be erected on the ruins of the old. Otherwise metaphysics alone is the true philosophy, and in it lie the genuine sources from which the understanding takes its use of reason.",[]],"wiener-s04-001":["All our cognitions can be considered in a twofold relation.",[]],"wiener-s04-002":["1. In relation to the object. That is the representation.",[]],"wiener-s04-003":["2. In relation to the subject. That is the consciousness of the representation.",[]],"wiener-s04-004":["Representation cannot be defined, because for that one always needs new representations. All representation is either sensation, or cognition. It is something which has a relation to something in us. Sensations do affect, but they also soon fly away, because they are not cognitions. For when I sense: then I cognize nothing. Cognition is twofold, either intuition or concept. The former is singular, the latter universal. For the concept belongs to all.",[]],"wiener-s04-005":["In all cognition matter and form are distinguished. Matter signifies the object, form, the way of cognizing the object. With the latter it comes down to consciousness. A cognition of which I am conscious is called clear. If I am not conscious of it: it is called obscure. Consciousness is the authoritative condition of all logical form of our cognitions. Hence obscure cognitions are no object of logic, because no logical rule that is obscure to us can help us.",[]],"wiener-s04-006":["All our clear representations can be logically distinguished into distinct and indistinct representations. The indistinct representation is the consciousness of a representation as a whole, without however distinguishing the manifold which is contained in the whole. Distinctness is the clarity that also extends to the parts. All Wolffians call an indistinct representation confused. – But the opposite of confusion is not distinctness, but order. Admittedly indistinctness is an effect of confusion, not confusion itself. There are also indistinct cognitions which are indistinct not through confusion, but through the lack of the representation. The concept of something is entirely simple. Here neither order nor confusion can be brought in. There is a twofold distinctness of cognitions",[]],"wiener-s04-007":["1. A sensible distinctness in intuition, when we are conscious of the manifold which is contained in the intuition.",[]],"wiener-s04-008":["2. A distinctness of the understanding in concepts. When a cognition is developed not as to content, but as to form, and the various parts of the representation that lie in the understanding are developed.",[]],"wiener-s04-009":["All our cognitions are either intuition, or concepts. The faculty of intuition is sensibility. The faculty of concepts is the understanding, and to cognize something through concepts is called thinking. Intuition, however, goes only to something singular, concept to that which several things have in common. From another side sensibility is defined thus, that it is a *receptivitaet*, a capacity to be affected by objects. The understanding, as a *Spontaneitaet*, a faculty to represent things as they are, not as they affect us. Sensibility is consequently the lower cognitive faculty, because sensibility gives me the material for thinking, the understanding on the other hand disposes over this material. There is a twofold perfection of cognitions .",[]],"wiener-s04-010":["1. Perfection according to the laws of sensibility, aesthetic.",[]],"wiener-s04-011":["2. Perfection according to the laws of the understanding, the logical.",[]],"wiener-s04-012":["One of the aesthetic perfections in the lecture is that a case *in concreto* gives intuition to the rules *in abstracto*, and makes them sensible, must however not be put in the place of the understanding, for then it would be spoiled, but it must be its companion. Concepts without sensibility have no object at all. The condition of all our concepts always lies ultimately in the senses. It is therefore a very necessary piece. And although it sometimes misleads the understanding: yet it is properly itself to blame for not investigating it better. A cognition according to laws of sensibility is perfect when it is new, easy, lively, according to laws of the understanding, when it is thorough.",[]],"wiener-s04-013":["Logical perfection rests on the agreement of cognition with the object, the aesthetic on the agreement with the subject. The rules of the agreement of cognition with the object must be necessary, and hold for all cognition and for every understanding, for because my cognition is to agree with the object, it must also agree with that of others. || V Aesthetic perfection rests on the particular laws of the sensibility of the human being, and is therefore not universal for all beings. But since objects are represented not only through concepts, but also through intuition: there must also be necessary and universal laws of sensibility. Herein lies the concept of the beautiful. To be sure the ground of sensible satisfaction is subjective, but subjective with regard to the whole of humanity. E.g., music. Symmetry. But what agrees with the laws of the understanding holds not merely for human beings, but for all thinking beings. But logically perfect cognitions can also at the same time have an aesthetic perfection. For certain philosophical expositions are called beautiful.",[]],"wiener-s04-014":["Among all properties of aesthetic perfection none is more suited to the logical, and more essential to the aesthetic, than intuition. It is the form of sensibility, whereas on the other hand sensation, charm never give such universal laws. – Beauty, distinctness and universality are 3 essential pieces of logical perfection. Whoever wants to convince his listeners must not awaken their sensations, but present the matter to their intuition.",[]],"wiener-s04-015":["Sensations, e.g., charm, emotions are the matter of sensibility, intuition is its form. Imagination therefore holds only of the form of sensibility, but not of the matter. The essential aesthetic perfection is the opposite of the logical, the distinctness of intuition is nothing more than liveliness in aesthetic truth; it is not the main piece that the cognition agree with the object, if only it agrees with our laws of sensibility. Poetically true is not what is logically true, but what is in conformity with illusion. Common prejudices have aesthetic truth.",[]],"wiener-s04-016":["The universality of concepts is the opposite of that which belongs to aesthetic perfection. Here things are to be represented universally *in concreto*, there universally *in abstracto*. Aesthetic perfection, when it is advantageous to the logical, contains the essentially beautiful, when it is detrimental to it, the extra-essentially beautiful. What belongs to sensation, and not to intuition, contributes nothing to concepts. What someone senses he cannot even recount if the other does not sense it along with him, hence this belongs to the extra-essentially beautiful. Charm belongs to the agreeable, it often mixes itself with the judgment of taste, hence we love the womenfolk on account of their delicate build, which yet according to universal rules of taste is an imperfection against the strong build of men. Emotions belong to the sublime, but are very deceptive. Hence the pulpit orator must not seek to attain the end of his sermon thereby, but through distinctness, truth and universality. For sensations are at all times contrary to logical perfection. When the matter moves, it hinders thorough investigation, and the judge can be corrupted by tears. Agitations of the mind do harm, in that they often further the opposite of the proposed end. Hence the preacher must make use of intuition, and speak in images and similes, where an *object* is presented to us, but no consciousness of the alterations of our state is united with it. || V Logical perfection is the *conditio sine qua non*, and the *basis* of all thinking. The aesthetic cannot subsist by itself, but is only the adornment of the already correct logical one. Now one can indeed relax something of the logical. But one must not mutilate it. One can also as it were ignore something of truth, in that one cannot make it entirely universal, without otherwise letting the logical perfection lack anything in the least. First of all poetry is counted under aesthetic perfection. In this the logical perfection is in degree less than the aesthetic, and every aesthetic one at all times contends with the logical for a place. But the need of human nature requires that sensibility and understanding go in pairs, and the greatest scholar cannot free himself from all sensibility. From this arises popularity, or the condescension of the understanding, when it relaxes something of scholastically correct cognition in order to become more comprehensible. Only one must guard, with aesthetic sensations, that our attention not be brought away from the object onto our subjects. Gentle emotions can indeed give occasion for further reflection. But they are nevertheless not sufficient for conviction.",[]],"wiener-s04-017":["|| V Our intuition is enriched through history, which gives us cases where that which the concept says *in abstracto* has been set forth *in concreto*. Further through observations from common life, for which good guidance is given in Spalding's sermons, if only they are not shouted down by sensations. – Recently much was spoken of sensations. But whoever speaks much of feeling cannot think, but anyone can feel.",[]],"wiener-s04-018":["From what has been adduced there arises a union of the [ Textlücke: AA: Ästhetischen ] and [ Textlücke: AA: Logischen ] in the cognitions. For a cognition can be thorough, and logically perfect, but dry withal, it can be beautiful, but shallow withal. Dry can be pieces of knowledge that have the end of instructing, and in this case must be thorough. Others are only to entertain, hence they may well be shallow. Some are to contain both. To accomplish this much genius is required. For the understanding requires insight, sensibility, ease. Hence between the two there is a kind of conflict, where one must make that choice which is nearest to the end, does the most justice to it, e.g., || V Mathematics can acquire the tasteful only late, because everything must be set forth convincingly.",[]],"wiener-s04-019":["The perfection of cognitions in general is",[]],"wiener-s04-020":["1. Logical.",[]],"wiener-s04-021":["2. Aesthetic.",[]],"wiener-s04-022":["3. Practical perfection.",[]],"wiener-s04-023":["|| V Logical perfection goes to the understanding, and is the cognition of objects through it. The aesthetic goes to feeling, and to the state of our subject, namely how we are affected by the object. For through the beauty of cognition I seek not knowledge of the object, but of the subject. Practical perfection goes to our desires, whereby activity is effected.",[]],"wiener-s04-024":["The perfection of a cognition rests on 4 main points.",[]],"wiener-s04-025":["1. On the *Quantitaet* of the cognition, in that one is universal. A cognition that serves as a rule must be more perfect than one that holds only in particular cases.",[]],"wiener-s04-026":["Logical perfection as to quantity or with respect to the judgment is universality. Of the aesthetic, common validity.",[]],"wiener-s04-027":["2. ... *Qualitaet*, distinctness of the cognition. Contains the *quomodo?* Logical perfection as to *qualitaet* is distinctness, of the aesthetic, liveliness.",[]],"wiener-s04-028":["3. ... Relation, truth of the cognition. Truth is the relation of the cognition to the object. || V If a cognition passes over to movements of the mind: then the truth goes only to my subject, and is aesthetic truth. Logical perfection as to relation is objective truth. Of the aesthetic, subjective truth.",[]],"wiener-s04-029":["4. ... *Modalitaet*, insofar as it is a certain and necessary cognition. Logical perfection as to *Modalitaet* is the necessity of cognitions according to the understanding. Of the aesthetic, empirical necessity – –",[]],"wiener-s04-030":["|| V With aesthetic perfection one can think",[]],"wiener-s04-031":["1. subjective truth. It is the agreement of cognition with the way of thinking of the subject. Aesthetically true are ghost appearances and the like. The sun etc. has aesthetic truth, logical is not demanded here at all. The poet needs only aesthetic, as it occurs to our senses, and seems to be. The sun dips itself into the water, says the poet. Were he to say the earth turns about its axis: then he would assimilate himself to logical truth, and be no poet.",[]],"wiener-s04-032":["2. subjective distinctness (*relatione*) in intuition, when I fix the rules of concepts through examples, depiction. When I want to cognize something according to aesthetic perfection with respect to the liveliness of intuition: then it must be very lively. When, e.g., someone wants to cognize spring logically: then he judges according to quality and has everything distinct through a single mark. But the poet here speaks of a multitude of things, of the twittering of the birds and the like. But it is nevertheless very lively.",[]],"wiener-s04-033":["3. aesthetic universality, i.e., *popularitaet*, that a cognition be suited to the *sens commun*. I must presuppose the way I judge, as every other can judge. Here therefore is subjective ( H — —).",[]],"wiener-s04-034":["4. Necessity and certainty of aesthetic perfection, that a cognition of the senses be necessary, i.e., that experience and the voices of all human beings confirm it. Subjective necessity is habit.",[]],"wiener-s04-035":["|| V Manifoldness and unity make up every perfection. Our cognitive power strives greatly for manifoldness. But it has a need, that it must have unity. Otherwise it would not satisfy us, because cognition without unity, if one thing is not tied to another, does not increase our knowledge. With aesthetic perfection, e.g., the painter must know how to group the manifoldness of the figures so that a unity comes out, if his painting is to please. Truth is the foremost ground of unity, and the most necessary and foremost piece. Without truth no cognition at all takes place. In logical perfection it is the positive greatest condition. With the aesthetic it is the *conditio sine qua non*, and the principal negative condition, in that there it is not the main end, which consists in agreeableness and agreement of sensibility. But because no satisfaction can arise where the understanding does not come in and uncover errors: no contradictions can subsist with the aesthetic. No human being can accordingly get ahead in matters of taste if he has not laid logical perfection at the ground. Most of the artists of taste in Germany are extremely disgusting when one reads products of foreign peoples who have not merely studied belles-lettres. True aesthetic perfection one finds in the *Spectateur*, Sulzer, Wieland, in whom one notices that they have heads full of ideas, and add all machines to persuade the mind, in order then to accommodate themselves to the comfort of taste. Certain books have nothing attractive, although they are very thorough, because namely their authors never thought of giving their lecture a verve, and enlivening it. – Logical perfection is the skeleton of our cognitions. Hence whoever has learned something proper can easily soften something of logical strictness, and apply beauty. – Novels are figments of the brain that exceed the course of things, and make the heart wilted. History, geography, reading of the ancients, which unite both perfections, anthropology must be our teachers, and make the spirit more awakened. No science can be beautiful. || V For it contains universal rules, which precede practice. Feelings can be combined with the beautiful, they can please and move; but they contribute nothing to the play of our representation as cognitive powers, though indeed as sensation. These, however, are not so universally communicable as representation and concepts. Charms and feelings belong to the essentially beautiful roughly as a golden frame to a beautiful picture.",[]],"wiener-s04-036":["|| V The beautiful, however, must be judged more from the effect on sensibility than from the understanding, in that beauty is agreement with sensibility, but the understanding alone is the faculty of rules. Taste therefore cannot be brought to laws. For a law serves not only for judgment, but also for compliance. The rules of taste are empirical, but it is not these that make our judgment true, rather they serve only to bring our judgment, when it is cultivated through many exercises, under certain concepts. Taste accordingly can in no way be treated as science. Hence there are also no beautiful sciences. Beautiful arts are arts that take place not according to logical rules, but through empirical attempts. For the effect alone distinguishes the rules of sensibility, which no rule of the understanding cognizes, on account of which one also cannot have rules of taste. Aesthetically, therefore, there can be no canon, but the attempts of the beautiful arts at all times go first, and then follow the rules, which however serve only to criticize the art. One must therefore become acquainted with the models of beauty in order nevertheless not to acquire taste. For in taste the human being is given to fashion, and inclined to complaisance. Someone has taste when what pleases him pleases all. And so the social inclination of all has brought forth taste, and modified it. Now the ancients have endured the critique of many *Seculorum*, and thereby remain in their standing, and whoever reads them, and on whom they leave behind an impression, only he must not want to imitate them, acquires taste. – || V Taste is an effect of the power of judgment. This comes later than wit, and even than understanding. It shows how we can make a use of the judgment of sensibility, consequently in order to choose one must have collected a multitude of pieces of knowledge. Our cognition needs [ein ] certain [ Textlücke: AA: -s Mittel ], and that is language. But this [ Textlücke: AA: Mittel] is subject to many alterations. Hence one must write in a dead language. || V The affected constraint and grimace of the beautiful displeases to the highest degree. Better is the natural understanding in its simplicity. || V We can occupy ourselves in two ways",[]],"wiener-s04-037":["1. *per otium* 2. *per negotium*.",[]],"wiener-s04-038":["All occupation of taste is a play. Occupation of the understanding is true business As plays the former deserve all approval. But when the play seems to be a business: then it is overdone. E.g., when someone in speech chases after wit: then his play becomes a business. In no language are there two words that would actually be *synonyma*. Least of all are philosophy and learned cognition so, as our author would have it, but they are worlds apart. A science is a system. Thereby it is distinguished from universal cognition be it as to the object what it will Naturalists in the field of the learned are those who lack the systematic, and who possess nothing but *rhapsodie*. In oral lecture one can grasp the system better than from books, in that here one does not, as there, reason about it, but proceeds according to a certain thread.",[]],"wiener-s04-039":["A science can be:",[]],"wiener-s04-040":["1. A science of reason, which can be cognized only according to universal *principien* of reason. E.g., mathematics.",[]],"wiener-s04-041":["2. Science of erudition, which one can learn historically. Such as: histories, languages, philology, the knowledge of all tools of erudition, literature.",[]],"wiener-s04-042":["Philosopher is no scholar, but he only sees to what end all erudition is useful in the end. But he must possess erudition in order to be able to make use of philosophy. He is therefore no artist of reason, but one who studies the laws of reason. To become learned means to imitate another in what he knows, consequently the pupil can never become more learned than his teacher. Mathematics can be learned. But from it many new things can still be found. Philosophy, however, cannot be learned, but rather philosophizing, in that we set forth the judgment about the matter more than the matter itself. Whoever begins to learn philosophy by heart removes himself ever further from it. For in the human being lie judgments that, if they are not infected by adopted propositions, are clear and pure.",[]],"wiener-s04-043":["Imperfection our author divides into *defectus* and *vitia*.",[]],"wiener-s04-044":["In metaphysics one has",[]],"wiener-s04-045":["1. *defectio privative dicta* (*defectus*) is to be censured in small measure, namely when dryness but otherwise thoroughness prevails in a treatise. Lack is the non-being of a cognition. – Ignorance is lack of cognition.",[]],"wiener-s04-046":["2. *defectio contrarie dicta* (*vitia*), when, e.g., in a letter *vitia grammaticalia*, and affected things occur. There are logical *e ssentielle*, [ Textlücke: AA: und ] *extra e ssentielle vitia*. In our language many contradictions prevail. I went out alone with another. The English call that a bull. Such an error belongs to essential *vitiis*.",[]],"wiener-s04-047":["Error is a *vitium* of the judgment. Since logic sets forth the universal formal rules, and abstracts from all object: the aesthetic and practical perfections cannot properly belong to it, but are regarded as episodic pieces, not proper to logic. Practical cognition properly flows into our will. But it is taken along for this reason, in order thereby to be able to cognize the logical all the better, and distinguish it from the other cognitions. Practical perfection is determination of the ends of human actions. Aesthetic is *popularitaet*. Horizon is the congruence of the bounds of our cognition with the ends of humanity, and of human beings. It is therefore a sum total of the pieces of knowledge which, taken together, are *adaequat* to our ends. Now we can consider either the ends of the whole of humanity, i.e., the absolute horizon, as also the particular, i.e., the horizon of a human being, which consequently is a relatively determined horizon. As regards the particular horizon, there the horizon of a [ Textlücke: AA: Ungebildeten ] is other than the horizon of the connoisseur. The former learns sciences without proposing to himself a certain end of their application. At most in order to be able to join in speaking in company, the latter in order thereby to become useful to others. The horizon of a religion is a catechism, i.e., a sum total of the pieces of knowledge which are necessary to the human being as human being. But it presupposes a cultivated understanding to determine the pieces of knowledge of all persons, and the *Nexus* of all useful pieces of knowledge among one another, further a power of judgment that has matured through years and experience. – || V To *determiniren* the horizon of universal human cognition logically is not possible everywhere. In metaphysics one can in many pieces discern the bounds of human cognition. In natural science, however, this is not feasible on account of the great domain of this study. But it is possible that it could happen one day. In the aesthetic determination of the universal horizon it comes down to how the aesthetic stands in the neighborhood of the logical. The logical requires distinct truth and certainty, which, however, must not be met with so exactly in the aesthetic, if it is to be universally useful. But we must look more to the logical, and at the least the measure between the two must be exactly determined. Many a thing I cannot know. That is above my horizon. Other things I may not know. That is outside my horizon. E.g., the *Medicus* may not, as *Medicus*, know all legal procedures. But what is useful to know, and what can I do without? About this Gesner, in his writings-lectures on his *Isagoge*, in that he takes exception to the merry questioning of those who ask with every matter: what is that useful for? for what, that I the old dress of the Hebrews , H that I the [ Textlücke ] and their nature know? expresses himself thus: *quaerit delirus quod non respondet Homerus*. What is not useful now can be useful one day. E.g., when through an experiment a new law in nature is given. If you have only properly extended and corrected your pieces of knowledge: then the knowledge of their use will come afterward. This answer is sufficient in determining the universal horizon of human beings. For even he who collects the absurdities of human beings, and thus among all human efforts brings about the least use, in that he as it were only sets up an inventory of the rubbish in the world, can nevertheless bring about some little use with respect to history.",[]],"wiener-s04-048":["Horizon is the concept of our pieces of knowledge which, taken together, are *adaequat* with the ends that we have. It is",[]],"wiener-s04-049":["|| V 1. logical. In relation to our cognitive faculty, how far we can come, and how far we must go. Everything must be laid out for perfection. Hence we can go as far as we come.",[]],"wiener-s04-050":["2. aesthetic is the horizon in relation to taste. This is *popularitaet*. One seeks to win either the approval of the *Publicum*, or of the connoisseurs. In the first case one seeks current erudition, which is suited to everyone and pleases. In this way, however, one falls into shallowness, as is the case with the French.",[]],"wiener-s04-051":["3. practical in relation to use. But with respect to the particular ends one must always ask after the more excellent aim, and have only a universal and determined ground for doing this and that. E.g., when the theologian wants to appear as a scholar: then theological erudition is the greatest among all. He must study natural theology well in order to meet the freethinkers, further *physico-theologie* on account of the difficult *eventuum* in the holy books, mathematics, *theologia sacra* etc. etc. But as preacher and teacher of his congregation he need only have clear ideas of the truths that he sets forth, in order to find their importance. The expression \"above the horizon\" is not quite suited to the matter. For what is on the horizon of the sky I can after all see. One sees well that it is supposed to represent that which lies outside the surface in which all only possible pieces of knowledge are contained. Historically human knowledge has no bounds. For experience extends it continually. The bounds of mathematics cannot be determined, in that new things are still always added to it. But the horizon of the cognitions of physics in general is very limited, because it occupies itself merely with objects of sensibility. Insofar as our reason can depart from all experience, a horizon can be given. Below our horizon is that which we should not know. This can be said *relative*. E.g., when someone brings religious disputes into the pulpit: then the *Publicum* gets things into its head that it should not know. The most important use of this doctrine of the horizon is the relative one. Relatively I can always say: this is above this human being's horizon, if he is cultivated. Yet one can also err in this if one does not go to work properly with it. In the extension of the horizon the human being stands as it were on a mountain, and describes a greater *Radium* of his circle. But beware that you do not ceaselessly alter your horizon, and assume another center. For thereby all interconnection of your cognitions will fall away. Hence we must have a certain center on which all our cognitions agree together. And this must happen in the first years of studying. And indeed one must enrich one's understanding with historical cognitions if one wants to gather a supply for the whole of life. For only around this time is memory capable of taking in things that never become extinguished. But with the increase of the power of judgment the sharpness of memory also ceases. We must further know how to *determiniren* the horizon of our cognitions properly at the beginning. But commonly human beings arrive too late, and then at this insight, when they see where it should have been *determinirt*. And that commonly stems more from the negligence and amusements of youth than from ignorance. Other human beings' horizon one can *determiniren* according to the difference of the pieces of knowledge. In instruction the lecture must be arranged according to what my pupil takes an interest in at these years; in following time as a youth he again has another interest, and so I must always encompass the horizon of my apprentice. I must also not make it all too narrow, and e.g. in the pulpit expect too little of the comprehension",[]],"wiener-s04-052":["of common people. Sciences have a horizon. That is the *territorium* of the science, hence many a thing does not belong in the *territorium* of the science which yet it is necessary to know in the case of the science.",[]],"wiener-s04-053":["The imperfection of our cognition is",[]],"wiener-s04-054":["1. Ignorance, the imperfection of lack, which thus makes up an empty space.",[]],"wiener-s04-055":["2. Error, an imperfection of enrichments, when I have gathered ideas that resist truth.",[]],"wiener-s04-056":["The fate of one who errs is thus worse than that of one who is ignorant, for he hinders the entry of truth. To remove it one must first reduce the human being to ignorance. It is thus a double imperfection. For it is not only a negative lack of truth, but also a cause of resisting perfection. In all science there is danger. But notwithstanding this we must still venture it at the danger of error, because otherwise one could never make progress. Error is generated by 2 things, ignorance, and the desire to know, which properly brings it forth. Ignorance is no great reproach if there has been a lack of opportunity to know certain things. The name of idiot is supposed to signify one who is ignorant, just as the old sophists distinguished the wise and idiots, dunces. But the pride with which one regards an idiot is ill-informed. The name belongs not to one who is ignorant, but to him who presumes to judge of all things, and yet knows nothing. It is an observation made in common life that it is very useful that a human being not make the whole of a thing. An ignoramus is he who does not have as much knowledge as he needs with respect to this or that end. E.g. In the making of a pin more than 4 to 10 human beings concur. This distribution makes everything perfect, and everything easy. So it is also in knowledge. The one finds a pleasure in rummaging through archives, the other holds that to be empty. Ignorance is accordingly no reproach; because there are many things we partly need not know, partly cannot know. The knowledge of one's own ignorance is a science, to which belongs a knowledge of the empty space in which possible knowledge can be encountered. The common man is ignorant with regard to astronomy. But he does not know his own ignorance. It is thus very necessary to uncover to the human being the abyss of his ignorance, and only those can do that who have extended their knowledge, and therefore have insight into its paucity. Helvetius says, now one knows everything, except that which Socrates knew, namely nothing. Opposed to this ignorance is much-knowing.",[]],"wiener-s04-057":["1. Historical *polyhistoria*, learnedness extended according to determinate boundaries.",[]],"wiener-s04-058":["2. The extended knowledge of reason, determined without boundaries, *polymathia*. Both together one can call *pansophia*.",[]],"wiener-s04-059":["In the sciences there is thus a difference between those which one can bring out of reason, and those which must not be taught from it, such as geography etc. In the previous *seculum* the inclination of most went toward *polyhistoria*. For this there is further required philology, i.e., the knowledge of the instruments of the ancients, in which the models of historical cognition have been brought to us. Philology, under which the linguist and humanist are comprehended, and then the *litterator*, make up polyhistory. The philologist must be a *litterator*, he must be a humanist, i.e., he must form his taste, and be able to communicate it to other human beings. Now for the formation of his taste he takes the classical authors, i.e., the models and are exemplary. A linguist is he who studies the ancient languages with critique, and if he chooses them as models of taste, then he is called a humanist. The ancients alone will always remain models of genuine taste, because the languages are dead languages. Now the *litterator* is he who can name many books of the ancients, such as their editions, their authors –. Whoever knows many books is a *litterator*. Whoever knows many languages is a linguist, and this together makes up the great scholar. This is the much-knowing of which Paul says that it puffs up. For if the *polyhistor* has an acquaintance with so many books, then he believes he knows just as much as those who wrote them, although he knows them only historically. Philosophy can strike down pride, and set before his eyes his true ends. A learnedness without philosophy is a cyclopean learnedness. Philosophy is the second eye, and sees how the collected knowledge of the one eye agrees with the collective end. || V One part of philosophy is called *Humaniora*. The character of humanity consists in sociability. *Humaniora* are liberal arts. Liberal art is merely play, the bread-art labor. But what belongs to beautiful art is not yet entirely free, for painters etc. are still always mechanical. Poetry and eloquence are already freer. A humanist is he who treats of beauties, and in the study of the ancients cultivates his mind, in order to drive out its wildness. Thereby arises that urbanity which we become aware of in the ancients, and with this aim, that it drives out rusticity, historical cognition is of importance.",[]],"wiener-s04-060":["All these knowledges can be drawn together upon one principal science, and are regarded as means of it. Perhaps also the bread-science that one studies. It is usual among human beings to do something rather from inclination than for the sake of the constraint of duty. For many their inclinations often miscarry, and do not bring them far. This is a human being's hobby-horse, which he would rather ride than the cart-nag of his office. Since then duty is something from which you gladly make yourselves free, then learn something with which you can play with reason. E.g. the study of botany, of geography, etc.",[]],"wiener-s04-061":["These auxiliary sciences must be thought over in accordance with the plan of our knowledge. Often they are the necessary means in a science. E.g. a physician must have natural science as an aid, further philology, because wide reading gives a multitude of experiences. But often they are only a play of entertainment, and subject to the pleasure of each. There is a distinction among the scientists between bread-art and liberal art. And so scholars are either guild-scholars, or guild-free scholars.",[]],"wiener-s04-062":["A scholar is often opposed to those who pursue mechanical arts. The scholar is thus taken as one who pursues no handicraft. Thus the scholars in China let the nails on their hands grow, as proof that they pursue no manual labor. Bread-art is a science, if it is a means of acquisition; if it is supposed merely to interest the mind, and to civilize the talents, then it is liberal art. Bread-arts are therefore great incentives of activity, but the sciences are very much restricted by these bread-arts, inasmuch as one must thereby conform to the general delusion, for even if truths are of no use at all, one must nevertheless present them because the public has become accustomed to them. Thus many things could be struck out of logic which have once been received, and come up in *examinibus*. In short, guild-scholars are bound to their *métier*. – Liberal art has many advantages here, inasmuch as it is not restricted to the conditions of trade, and it is asked not at all after the delusion of the multitude, but after the inner content of the sciences. Yet on the other side it does not have incentive enough to compel human beings to apply themselves to it with great effort. Faults in the use of our knowledge are gallantry and pedantry. The word pedant is Italian, and has its origin from the *Magister Pedarius*, who was set as supervisor over a young person, and followed him at every step. He had to instruct him in sciences and school-learnedness, but was never drawn into the company of his father, therefore he did not learn to make any use of his knowledge in intercourse with other human beings.",[]],"wiener-s04-063":["In the use of our cognition it can be either more suited to the school, or more to the world. The school contains prescriptions and methods, i.e., means for teaching and learning. A cognition is thus scholastically correct if it contains all the forms according to which one is to teach. Every science has 2 perfections, a scholastic and popular one.",[]],"wiener-s04-064":["Scholastic perfection thus consists in the didactic form, which serves to make cognition easier for a beginner. It makes up the essence and the ground, it is the agreement of the use of our reason, and the punctiliousness of the logical rules. It is thus the presentation that can be either scholastically correct, or popular. Popularity consists in the accommodation of a science to the power of comprehension and to the taste of the commonwealth. Such a cognition is not merely for the school, but also for the world. –",[]],"wiener-s04-065":["The scholastic method accustoms itself to certain painstaking exactitudes, e.g. *terminos technicos*, demonstrations, definitions – which are not suited to a dilettante. Meanwhile the scholastic cut is the greatest perfection, and always the first, inasmuch as it contains the certain rules of our procedure, and without it all thoroughness would fall away. Scholastic perfection must go first, then popularity follow, for to begin with popularity is very absurd. But if we dedicate our knowledge to human society, then we must leave out these rules, and yet retain as much of the science as is suited to the power of comprehension and inclination of others. Popularity thus requires knowledge of the world and intercourse with human beings, and he surely understands his subject best who can make it comprehensible and clear to another who is unlearned. Only in popularity one must not abandon thoroughness, inasmuch as it is nothing other than an alteration of presentation. – Now pedantry is the restricted capacity in the use of cognition, when one restricts it merely to school use. It is reckoned as pedantry when someone in abstract concepts begins from Adam. But it does indicate a certain thoroughness. A painstaking exactitude in the formal, micrology, petty-mongering is pedantry. The pedant is a poor copy of the methodical head, who sets himself the right end for his knowledge, and also knows how to attain it thereby. But a pedant never comes with his great learnedness to his end. There are pedants in all estates, e.g. hunting pedants, military pedants, finery- and politeness-pedants, who always talk of their subject, and have to do with it. The learned pedant is the most bearable. For with him there is only the reproach of the lack of knowledge of the world, although one can otherwise learn from him, even if one feels no agreeableness in doing so. But pedants in another field are empty heads. Besides pedantry, gallantry can also come to cognition. Gallant is he who from the superiority of his refinement concedes to another advantages which the latter does not presume for himself. || V Among the ancients the concept of gallantry was entirely unknown. Horace, *Carmina* lib. II, Ode 16. *ad Grosphum*, when he praises his patron, ascribes to him advantages of possession, to himself advantages of mind, and thus obviously prefers himself. In general one finds among them not the least *délicatesse* in sparing others. – No people has more gallantry in style of writing than France. || V Most books there are accommodated to the taste of the fair sex, and therefore these are also there the judges of scientific objects. To be sure, in social intercourse they must give the tone of conduct, but to water down entire sciences for their sake until they are suited to their capacity causes shallowness, and scholastic perfection thereby loses its content. || V The Germans come to grief, and show something constrained in their manner, when they want to equal the Frenchman through gallantry. This gallantry is dangerous, because it has in it the charming and seductive quality that one, because the whole public cheers applause to us, esteems this higher than the censure of learned men.",[]],"wiener-s04-066":["The magnitude of learned cognition is extensive according to the multitude of cognition, or intensive according to its importance, i.e., with respect to its sequences and effects. This importance one can consider",[]],"wiener-s04-067":["1.) with logical aim, since it is that which contains logical perfection, namely whereby cognitions become more thorough, more distinct and more certain,",[]],"wiener-s04-068":["2.) its practical importance consists in the utility of cognition. This cannot be foreseen, and must therefore be awakened. Logical perfection teaches the practical, inasmuch as the utility is thereby more determined. It is thus the first perfection. Yet a cognition can have logical perfection *respective* entirely.",[]],"wiener-s04-069":["Fruitfulness of cognition goes to the magnitude and multitude of cognition. Content of cognition lies in the manifold, and indicates the parts of it. To the perfection of cognition it belongs that it be true. Truth is an agreement of cognition with the object. But this is properly only a nominal definition; it is one of the most disconcerting phenomena in a science when one cannot answer a question that makes up the essence of the science. Such a question has long since been put forward in logic, namely what is truth? Pilate was the first who raised this question. Here it seems to founder at the name, although it wants to give the understanding rules of truth. The above definition does not fit. For the object is not with us, but only the cognition of it. Therefore we can compare our cognition of it not with the object, but only with our cognition. Since then no other cognition is true than that which agrees with the object, then no cognition of the object is true but that which agrees with our cognition of the object. The ancients called this *diallele*, a fallacy. E.g. if someone, in order to assert his honesty, wanted to appeal to the honesty of another. Thus Lavater asserted much from inferences into eternity, and spoke of the constitution of the future life, which has not the least illusion of incorrectness and conflict in it. But does this cognition have an object agreeing with it? Such cognitions are then called empty. So cognition is true when it agrees with itself. But in that way all lies can be true, if there is need of no other confirmation than that — [AA: of the agreement ]",[]],"wiener-s04-070":["of cognition [AA: with ] itself. – Now is there a universal *criterium* of truth? a rule whereby universal truth can be distinguished? It is impossible. For it is supposed to be a universal *criterium* of truth without distinction of objects. If it were a *criterium* only of certain objects, then it would not be universal. But since it must abstract from all distinction of objects, but all truth is the agreement of cognition with the object, whereby the cognition of one object is distinguished from the other, and the cognition of universality has no characteristic whereby an object is distinguished: then a material *criterium* of truth is thus impossible. The matter of all cognition is the object, the form is the way of treatment. Logic goes to the form, truth to the object. Therefore it is first of all impossible, and contradicts itself, to give a universal material *criterium* of truth. But since logic abstracts from all content, there can only be formal *criteria* of truth. To truth belong two things.",[]],"wiener-s04-071":["1.) Agreement of cognition with the object.",[]],"wiener-s04-072":["2.) Agreement with itself.",[]],"wiener-s04-073":["The agreement of cognition with itself, irrespective of objects, is a formal *criterium* of truth. For this makes a cognition possible as cognition. Without this no cognition at all is possible. Therefore it is always indispensable, although it is not sufficient for the material *criterium*. It is *conditio sine qua non*, e.g. the principle of contradiction is a formal *criterium* of truth. But whoever presents a logic so finely that it agrees with itself, for him this *criterium* does not suffice. The answer to the question: what is truth? would thus be the agreement of a cognition with itself, and is one ground for taking it to be true. A universally sufficient *criterium* of truth cannot be demanded. – If I abstract from all different objects, then I retain only a concept of the object. A universal *criterium* of truth treats only of the form of thinking, which is not the agreement of cognition with the object, but with itself.",[]],"wiener-s04-074":["The rules of agreement with itself are",[]],"wiener-s04-075":["1.) A cognition must not contradict itself. This is the *conditio sine qua non*.",[]],"wiener-s04-076":["2.) *affirmatio*, when cognitions hang together as grounds with their sequences. If e.g. I adduce as the ground of the wetness of the west winds that they come over the sea, then that is one ground of truth. It belongs to the inner *criterium* of truth that a cognition be grounded. A *criterium* which rests on the *principium* of sufficient ground must always conclude from the ground to the sequence.",[]],"wiener-s04-077":["3.) Between which cognitions there is to be unity, i.e., relation of many cognitions to one.",[]],"wiener-s04-078":["Opposed to truth is falsehood. When falsehood is held to be truth: it is error.",[]],"wiener-s04-079":["1.) Falsehood, i.e., a lack of agreement with the object.",[]],"wiener-s04-080":["2.) Illusion of truth.",[]],"wiener-s04-081":["Is truth possible? Truth in logic is an agreement of cognition with the laws of the understanding; since the understanding thus here acts according to its own laws, then there is nothing absurd here why truth should not be possible. Is error possible? i.e., how far is the form of our thinking contrary to the understanding possible. It is hard to comprehend how a power should deviate from its own laws, since it acts only according to certain laws. If these are essential, then it cannot deviate from them; thus if among the formal laws of the understanding which logic presents an essential one is possible, then it cannot deviate from it. E.g. a heavy body cannot cease to fall. – If we had no other sources of cognition than understanding, and were nothing but understanding, then we would think that the understanding would always judge only according to its laws, even if it sometimes judged restrictedly, but through the understanding we have no object at all, inasmuch as it contains merely the form. Sensibility, i.e., the faculty of intuition and of sensation, gives the material for thinking, and is the other source of cognition. Now if a cognition is to be related to the object, then both work together, sensibility gives the intuition, and the understanding the concept. If a body is driven by 2 forces, then there arises a third motion where it remains neither in the track of the one nor of the other, i.e., the diagonal force. E.g. a ball which would have gone straight into the center of the target goes somewhat, if it is driven sideways by the wind, to the edge of the target, whither it would be driven neither by the force of the powder nor of the wind. Now sensibility flows into the actions of the understanding, and from this arises the diagonal direction of the understanding, where it obtains now truth, now illusion. Sensibility thus is the cause of illusion, and the understanding, insofar as it passes judgment on it. But sensibility in itself is also no source of errors, for insofar as it relates itself to its objects, there is an agreement with the laws of this cognitive power. The ground that the senses do not judge erroneously is because they cannot judge at all. For only the understanding judges. Error is thus neither in the understanding alone, nor in the senses alone, but lies always in the influence of the senses on the understanding, when we do not well distinguish the influence of sensibility on the understanding.",[]],"wiener-s04-082":["Logic, since it abstracts from all content, cannot speak of the influence of sensibility further than that it presents the subjective ground of our judgment. The understanding is the objective ground of our judgment. But if something subjective, which yet belongs to sensibility, flows into our judgment, then sensibility has mixed itself in, and this is the source of errors. From this intermixture arises *cognitio hybrida*, a bastard cognition, which is composed of 2 parts. Here sensibility does not serve the understanding, but confuses it, and from this arises the diagonal direction of the understanding. E.g. an adherent of an opinion holds the opinion for something probable. Probability is an objective ground. But the subjective attachment brings about the *effect* which is held to be objective. – Because in every error the understanding is always still active, human beings, when they judge at the danger of error, nevertheless always exercise truth. Those who thus have many errors nevertheless always have something true, for they have used their understanding, and cultivated their powers.",[]],"wiener-s04-083":["We also cannot complain about the limitations of our understanding in error. For then we accuse nature. For the limitations of the understanding are indeed the cause of ignorance, but not of errors. For in ignorance I need only not judge. Consequently every error is self-incurred, when our inclination draws us beyond our boundaries. In all our judgments there is always something true. Never can a human being err wholly and entirely. If it seems so to us at times, then we have not understood the human being. For according to what he perhaps presupposes, he can always have some, even if only partial, truth. A total error would be a complete conflict against the laws of the understanding. For then it could not arise from the understanding,",[]],"wiener-s04-084":["which can bring forth nothing that conflicts with its nature. But what deviates from its laws is influence of sensibility. In every error there is still always a true judgment, but if much is still slipped in by sensibility, then there is indeed falsehood in it, but it is only partial.",[]],"wiener-s04-085":["Since we abstract from all relation to the object, and consider only the rule of the concordance of the understanding with itself, then the *criterium* of truth in logic can only be the agreement of the laws of cognition with themselves. The rule of the concordance of cognition with itself is the principle of contradiction and the principle of sufficient ground, which are indeed not sufficient *materialiter*, but yet are for judging of a thing *formaliter*, whether it agrees with itself. The principle of contradiction is a negative principle. A cognition is false if it contradicts itself. But a cognition that does not contradict itself is not therefore also true. The principle of contradiction is indeed such that nothing can be contrary to it, but as a positive *principium* for cognizing truth it is not sufficient. Insofar it is indeed positive, that through it I can have insight into the necessity of truth in a necessary cognition. – The principle of sufficient ground is when a cognition hangs together with a ground, or when from a cognition nothing but true sequences attach themselves. Both principles thus press for unity. – If a cognition does not contradict itself, but the opposite of it would contradict itself, then it is true. If a cognition does not contradict itself: it is possible, but from the *principio contradictionis* not yet all unity of a cognition can be concluded. E.g. that my thoughts penetrate into other human beings' souls is possible, but therefore not yet true. If one cognition is the ground of the other, then there is a connection of both cognitions. If the ground is true, then the sequence is also true. If all sequences are true: the ground must also be true. The principle of contradiction is treated deliberately in metaphysics, and that the *criterium* of truth is the *principium rationis*.",[]],"wiener-s04-086":["1.) *a priori* the interconnection of a cognition with its ground.",[]],"wiener-s04-087":["2.) *a posteriori* the interconnection of a ground with its sequences.",[]],"wiener-s04-088":["A cognition without ground is groundless but not yet false, e.g. that plants have souls, but for it to be true, there belong grounds, and that the cognition be connected *a priori*. From the sequences one concludes to the truth of cognition,",[]],"wiener-s04-089":["1.) negatively, if only a single sequence is false, then the whole cognition is false. For if the cognition were true, then this sequence too would have to be true. Among false sequences one also boasts of the dangers. But dangers are often only relative dangers, and often rest only on external circumstances, when e.g. the times are so bad that even truth suffers danger. So here it is a matter not of the dangerousness, but of the falsehood of the proposition. Whoever begins with dangers seeks tricks to win someone over before one can use reason.",[]],"wiener-s04-090":["2.) positively. In the cognition of the truth of a proposition it will not do that I conclude from the truth of some sequences to the truth of a cognition. The cognition can have something true, and from this arises the truth of this single sequence. But the rest can be false. Thus I cannot conclude to the truth of the cognition from such a ground. If indeed all sequences are false, then there is nothing true and nothing sound in the cognition The positive inference is thus the *onus probandi*, where I must show the truth of all sequences. But in the negative I need only show the falsehood of some sequences, and therefore this is a sufficient *criterium*. Only it is difficult, indeed it cannot happen at all, that I could know all sequences. For if I knew them, then my cognition would be certain. Yet we use it, and make in doing so this probable inference. If most sequences are true, then they are all true. For if a cognition does not contradict itself, then I seek its ground, and examine the sequences of it. If many, but not all sequences are to be found out, then I accept it as an incomplete cognition, inasmuch as I conclude from the truth of various sequences to the truth of the proposition. For since it is impossible to show the truth of all sequences, then it follows that here too we have no *stricte* sufficient usability of the *criterium*, but can use it only as a ground of probability. The principle of sufficient ground is treated here not as in metaphysics, where it is valid of the object, but as a logical principle. For since the principle of contradiction is not yet a sufficient *criterium* of truth, then the interconnection of cognition must nevertheless be investigated through it. The principle of contradiction is the principle of what subsists together, and of accord, the principle of sufficient ground, the principle of interconnection.",[]],"wiener-s04-091":["A cognition is totally false, or partially false. A cognition can always be false, and yet one can discover partial truths in it, e.g. in polytheism there is more truth than in atheism. – In disputations there are certain subtle fallacies, in order to confuse others. E.g. whoever says, all Moors are human beings. The respondent is a human being. Therefore he is a Moor. The respondent is indeed a human being, but not a black one, and therefore he does not speak entirely falsely. Often it is good to seek out this true element, and not at once to throw away everything that contains an error. We can quarrel in 2 ways, either because we have different interests, and then we quarrel as enemies, or we have a common interest, but do not agree in the way of promoting it, and then we quarrel as friends. All human beings have a common interest in the investigation of truth, and therefore in such a quarrel we must be sympathetic participants, inasmuch as we do not dwell on showing where the other has erred, but where he is right. This sympathetic disposition is indeed moral, and does not properly belong in logic. But logic prescribes the rules of it as correct maxims. If we believe a proposition to be entirely false, we must only recall our above proposition p. 85, that no proposition can be entirely false. One can indeed feign for oneself a total falsehood that no one takes to be true. But this is then no error. If the thing is so false that there is not even an interest in seeking a degree of truth of its own in it, and is thus beneath all notice, then it is absurd. If thus the falsehood is clear to the common understanding: the cognition is absurd. Between total and partial truth the difference concerns either the object, or the cognition. The deficiencies of cognition are partial falsehood of cognition (*vitium*), or it is wholly true, but does not concern the whole truth of the object (*defectus*). In partial falsehood it lies in the lack of the *totality* of the truth of the cognition, not in the *totality* of the truth of the object; if this incorrectness does not make up the essential end of the cognition: it is *tolerabiliter* true. E.g. prejudices in religion, which one must seek to tolerate and to improve. – – We have remarked, a cognition is so constituted that in it as cognition not everything is true, or the whole cognition is indeed true, but the whole truth of the object is not there. E.g. whoever narrates a story and conceals a part of it does not therefore lie. For he here only makes use of a ruse, and it is also sometimes necessary, and unavoidable, to keep silent about a part. One dissimulates when one merely conceals, and the cognition is in this *totaliter* true, even if the whole of the object is not contained in it. – This examination, how many errors are encountered in a cognition, and whether this or that cognition must therefore be contested, is of great importance, and presupposes much prudence, which must be grounded on a power imparted by experience. – With respect to both one makes the distinction between exact and crude cognition. Exactness concerns the relation of cognition to the object, whether the object is cognized wholly or in part. E.g. the determination of distance by paces is sufficient and exact for the determination of the journey, but not for the determination of the boundaries of an estate. Fractions in numbers can be left out if it is to be exact enough for payment. But a merchant who closes his books may not leave out 1/10 of it, because then the balance is not exact, which is the proof of the correctness of the account. Thus a cognition can be called exact with one aim, with another a crude estimate. Exactness often looks like pedantry when it is *objective* exact in the pieces where it is not at all required for my aim. E.g. the ancients determined in fortification how many cubic inches had to be carted out in the making of a ditch. Scrupulous exactness is distinguished from *exactitudo* or precise exactness in this, that the precise is purposive, and for just that reason receives the name *exactitudo*. In the sciences which rest on *principia a priori* exactness matters extraordinarily. E.g. in mathematics. Most ways of thinking almost never go to the exact, and there are few such thorough heads who observe exactness everywhere, although this exactness has its great worth, and must never be omitted, inasmuch as it elevates the power of judgment, even if it is often not purposive, and requires in doing so only little effort.",[]],"wiener-s04-092":["There are judgments *en gros* and *en detail*. || V Madame Geoffrin was a great Maecenas of scholars, although she herself was no great *genie*, in that she encouraged merits, and said one must judge of every human being *en gros*, because he would otherwise have to lose much if one judged him *en detail*. But to judge *en gros* anyone can. E.g., the book pleases me very much, it is beautiful. But what in the book? to censure, and to praise *en detail*, not. Our new great, so-called geniuses learn sciences *en gros*, and hence all their judgments and cognitions are *en gros*, and never *en detail*. From this arises the superficial in cognition. On which account it can never become exact. From exact cognition is distinguished the subtle, scrupulous, i.e., the exactness that goes to parts, skill at noticing something that more easily escapes common attention, which is also called the microscopic of our judgment. Cognition is micrological when one clings to the small, and for the small cannot see the large. Subtle exactness is therefore not yet micrological. For the subtle head can nevertheless be extended in concepts with regard to cognition. For we must distinguish objects not only at wide [ Textlücke: AA: Entfernungen ] but also at the points. But many draw all their cognition together onto the small, and the subtle head is commonly micrological. The diametrical opposite of the subtle is coarse cognition. Diametrically opposed, when from one error one falls into the opposite other. Both are blameworthy. For whoever clings much to subtleties is blind with regard to the whole. Coarse cognition, which goes to the large and whole, seems after all to let still more be expected of it. – Coarse cognition is not coarse error, which contradicts the common laws of the understanding, but it is coarse insofar as it is not yet extended, and is still raw, and contains marks of a merely common understanding. Our author says: all truth is dogmatic. – All our cognitions are either historical, or rational. Cognitions are historical when they are communicated to us from elsewhere, subjectively rational insofar as they are drawn from *principia* of reason. Objectively rational, insofar as the cognitions lie according to their true sources in reason. Objective historical cognition is possible only by means of the senses and of communication, and of teaching. Subjective philosophy is with many historical, objectively all philosophy is cognition of reason. – All cognition of reason is at the same time apodictic, i.e., it carries the proof of necessity with it. Through experience alone I can cognize only contingent things, through reason on the other hand also necessary cognition. Philosophy and mathematics are accordingly apodictic cognitions. That the stone is heavy I indeed cognize through experience. But the proof of the necessity of heaviness reason gives me. Apodictic propositions are twofold mathematical, insofar as they belong to mathematics, dogmatic, insofar as they belong to philosophy. Accordingly all cognition of reason from concepts is *dogma* all cognition of reason from the construction of concepts *mathema*. When I say: every body is divisible: then this is an apodictically certain proposition. – In revealed religion one calls propositions *dogmata* that contain doctrines which reason cannot cognize. This is already an improper expression. But if our reason were extended: it would also have this cognition, and then they would also be *dogmata*, although they are now only historical cognitions.",[]],"wiener-s04-093":["Our author calls system a sum total of dogmatic truths. – But the object of systematic and of common cognition makes no difference, rather both are distinguished only according to form. A system is where everything is subordinated to one idea, which must go to the whole, and determine the parts. E.g., someone can know many histories without having a science of them. For he does not have the form. He has made himself no outline of the whole, and ordered everything according to an idea. This idea therefore makes the systematic form. We can think the system as an *aggregat* or whole of cognition. Totality is now the determination of the whole, and this lies in the idea. With an *aggregat*, however, nothing is determined. For I do not know what will still be added to it. With a system, however, everything is already determined. – A cognition can be rhapsodistic, and is nevertheless not tumultuary. For to the tumultuary the methodical is opposed, and without method a cognition is tumultuary. But a cognition that is brought out methodically, but without system, is rhapsodistic. E.g., when we direct ourselves according to the power of grasp of the subject to be instructed.",[]],"wiener-s04-094":["Some have presented their matter through aphorisms, without representing the *articulation* of the whole, in that this is indeed always present, but is not distinctly to be seen by one who does not have the idea of the whole. It is therefore necessary to compose things systematically, in order to obtain a concept of completeness, and in order to have a touchstone of different parts, how they agree with the whole. With sciences one must always have their skeleton before one's eyes, in order to judge according to it whether this and that belongs in the science. – aesthetic truths are those that are appropriate to universal comprehensibility, logical ones are scholastic, or as our author says, learned.",[]],"wiener-s04-095":["A false cognition and error are distinguished. If I propose and examine a false judgment: then it is not yet error, the taking-beforehand-to-be-true of falsehood is error. Hence illusion must still be added, and indeed logical illusion. In optical illusion the senses are deceived. Moral illusion is when that seems to us to arise from duty which serves our own best. Logical illusion arises from the pure form of the understanding. Now our cognition can well be in conformity with the understanding according to form. For I can easily imitate the form without having inner correctness of the propositions, and so even the logical form gives an illusion of truth, of which the sophists frequently make use. No error is in itself unavoidable, because one need only not judge about things of which one understands nothing. An ignorance can well be unavoidable. For this does not always rest on our will, but often on the limitations of our nature. But in error we ourselves are always to blame, in that we are not cautious enough in the weighing of a judgment for which we do not have enough knowledge. Yet there are cases where something absolutely must be judged, and here it happens that someone falls into an error that in this relation was unavoidable. E.g., a human being wants to go across the river, which is frozen. But through an accident the ice is thinner at one place, and he drowns. Had he been able to judge this beforehand: he would not have gone across. Error is always something positive; the not-being-true, however, is something negative, for the positive in error is that it is after all thought. Illusion is the subjective ground of an error. An incorrectness and falsehood is evident when it is clear to common human understanding. When the incorrectness is not clear to every understanding, it is hidden, but can nevertheless be made evident, and to this all refutations go. In refutations it is a duty first to show whence this or that incorrectness of human cognition has come, i.e., I must discover the source of the error, i.e., the illusion, and afterwards I can prove to him the falsehood of it. For if I do not do the former, and do not make distinct enough to him by what he was misled to take this or that to be true: then I still leave him in uncertainty and doubt. Hence I must also not so directly call someone absurd. For he would certainly not have accepted this or that cognition had he not been brought by this or that hidden illusion to take it to be true. Such a human being, who straightaway calls another absurd, insults the other greatly, and fails to recognize the interest of universal human reason , H in this connection one should recall that true saying of the. —What contains an evident falsehood is an absurdity. But one cannot prove an evident incorrectness to anyone. For to him it is, through some misunderstanding, not evident. The expression absurd, preposterous concerns first the error itself, secondly it has relation to the person who has it. For I thereby declare that person unworthy of being taken into the class of universal human understanding. If I have declared my refutation against the judge whom I hold to be preposterous: then I cannot have the aim of teaching him. For in denying him common human understanding, I have declared him unworthy of teaching, and I would myself act preposterously. My refutation would therefore have to be directed to others. But others, who have healthy human understanding, will see the absurdity of themselves. Hence these expressions are not good to use. One must rather let an absurdity pass, unless it should draw after it something hateful that I must remove from it.",[]],"wiener-s04-096":["Whoever merely senses and does not judge does not err. Thus every error lies in judgment. Judgments are actions of the understanding and of reason. *Generaliter* one can say, truth objectively is agreement with the object; subjective truth is agreement with the laws of the understanding and of reason. The understanding thus agrees with its own or laws of the understanding. We have a faculty of the necessary and contingent, we have therefore occupations of the pure understanding. This part is called transcendental philosophy. Our understanding is of a particular kind, that it is capable of no intuition. For only the senses can do that. Now that second power comes into play, namely sensibility. We have spoken of the understanding with regard to error in judgment. We have now cultivated our understanding so far that by itself it does not err. The senses also do not err by themselves. Why? because they do not judge at all. In our sensibility there is something that is related merely to the object, and that is intuition, and something that is related to the subject, and that is sensation. To the clarity of cognition belong marks, *notae*. A mark is a ground of cognition, chiefly also in the comparison of things. In comparison I look to the *identitaet*, or *diversitaet* of things. One uses marks not merely in order to distinguish things, as our author would have it, but also to discover their agreement. E.g., I want not merely to distinguish a sheep from a goat, but I also want to know in what it agrees with another sheep. – Marks can be considered as a positive representation of the whole representation of the thing. Accordingly a cognition is distinct when we are conscious of its partial concepts, and represent them to ourselves clearly. Now if these part-concepts are again divided: then I get marks of marks. These parts of the marks are called subordinate marks, through which our cognitions ascend up to universal and higher marks, which are common to most things. E.g., what is virtue? A dominion over the inclinations. What is dominion? The power that one has over another. What is power? etc. Through these subordinate marks arises deep distinctness, where one penetrates ever further. – One can have marks through *coordination*, in that both marks are *compartes*. E.g., a body is an impenetrable whole. 1.) a whole 2.) impenetrable. This does not go to the whole, but both go to the concept of body, which they grasp together as *complementum*. The series of coordinate marks can go to infinity. E.g., gold is heavy, ductile, fire-resistant, does not rust etc. etc. Here there is not yet completeness. For one could still find 1000 such marks. A cognition thus becomes distinct through clear marks. Now through an *aggregat* of coordinate marks a cognition can become distinct, and then it grows extensively through the addition of each new mark. I take together so many new marks until they seem to make up the whole *aggregat*, in that, as long as I can, I add a new one. In this way one proceeds with *definitionen*. In a certain intention they can well have boundaries, namely for this or that end. But it grows also through the series of subordinate marks, in that one analyzes these, and lifts out of them new marks, which indeed already lay in it, but which one does not yet represent clearly. To extend one's cognition through coordination is more agreeable than subordination. This is difficult, in that one must cling with attention to a concept whose marks become ever more subtle, until at last they are capable of no further division. Nevertheless this division has boundaries. This is the intensive magnitude of distinctness, which indeed gives the understanding more to do, but extends cognition more, and makes it uncommonly correct. Here marks are not added, but I only decompose the concept, in that I take no mark in addition to the mark, and so add nothing, but divide This way of pursuing the series of marks has a progression that is bounded, for at last I come to such part-concepts as are irresoluble, and I come to the concept of the simple. A cognition is distinct when the marks themselves are clear. If I can state clear marks: then I can explicate myself about a thing. If I can state no marks: then I cannot explain to another what he does not understand, but must perhaps make do with examples. The magnitude of distinctness through coordinate marks is extended distinctness (*extensive*). The magnitude through subordinate marks is deep (*intensive*) distinctness. – || V Poets want to present everything distinctly in intuition, and for the sake of this aim they coordinate marks in a thing, in order to make the distinctness extended. The poet distinguishes himself from the philosopher in that the latter works for the understanding, the former for sensibility. To be sure, the poet too must observe logical correctness insofar as he has unity. But his proper end goes after all to the delight of sensibility. || V The reading of poetry thus has great use, in that it procures for our understanding the faculty of an extended distinctness, which is the easiest and most entertaining distinctness of all, because it is acquired historically. Deep distinctness is a matter of philosophy, and it depends on my getting out another mark from one mark, and continuing this until they become microscopic. Hereby cognition is not extended, but gone down into the depth. It is used especially in metaphysical investigations, where I always ask after the ground of the ground, without adding new ones. It is intensive distinctness, which makes our cognition very dry indeed, but thorough. For if we always remain with the whole, and never touch the parts: then we only swim on the surface, and have superficial knowledge. Marks serve either for the inner or for the outer ground of cognition. A cognition serves for inner use insofar as it serves to see the manifold in the object. It serves for outer use when one wants to distinguish the object from others, or to see its agreement with others. For outer use a few marks can easily suffice, which are nevertheless of slight significance for inner use. The marks on behalf of inner use are of greater importance. For if I know the thing from within: then these marks certainly suffice for outer use, although this does not suffice for inner use.",[]],"wiener-s04-097":["1.) Negating and affirming concepts.",[]],"wiener-s04-098":["2.) Negating and affirming marks of concepts.",[]],"wiener-s04-099":["If the object itself is a lack: then I can have only a negating concept. E.g., freedom is that the will does not stand under the coercion of human beings. In logic, which abstracts from all content, only negating or affirming marks can be treated, and the way of making concepts distinct takes place precisely through affirmation or negations. Through negation I have not extended the concept, and cannot thereby see into the concept more distinctly. The affirming concept must be added, and procure deeper distinctness. Through this we see into cognition according to its content more distinctly and with the greatest clarity. A negating mark is thus not used for the increase of our insight, but serves only to exclude a concept from other things, in order to prevent errors. All affirming marks serve to ground and bring forth a cognition. – The understanding properly goes to furthering the perfection of cognition. But it is also its merit when errors do not come in along with it. Negating marks are here *conditio sine qua non*. For since we are in danger of running out into falsehood: they must serve to hold off errors, and they are among the most excellent means of making our cognition perfect. All negating marks that hold one off from errors into which no human being has fallen have no use at all. For they are useful only when they hold off from a possible error. There are certain sciences that contain fixed negative marks. It would be desirable that one could draw around every science a rampart of negative marks, without leaving a gate open here and there where errors can creep in. The theologians begin to speculate with reason about things that God says in creation, to ask about the relation and constitution of this or that revealed doctrine, which is the source of many errors in theology. Negating marks thus hold off errors. Ignorance in things is without harm. Errors, however, not only do not increase the cognition of truth, but hinder it. It was mentioned above that one is ignorant in some points, that one also cannot always ask after use, but should go after everything worth knowing, as much as one can, without detour. From ignorance is distinguished voluntary *ignoriren*, when one takes no *notiz* of some circumstances that contribute nothing to the aim of the thing. What is outside our end we need not know. In conversations and matters we *ignoriren*, i.e., abstract from some matters, which one indeed knows, but leaves out, because they do not belong to the end. Hence much prudence in religious discourse belongs to *ignoriren*. One should ignore the errors of the common rabble, for if one stirs up the rubbish that smolders under the ashes: then it blazes up brightly. Only what one ignores must not be detrimental to the main end. But to occasion errors oneself is true deception, i.e., the *peccatum philosophicum* of the Jesuits, who, when they had a good intention, believed they acted well even in bad actions. Ignoring is thus something quite other than representing, in that the matter brings it with it so. Importance concerns the relation to use and end. A cognition is important when it is a great ground for use, or serves for many kinds of ends. Importance, however, is always relative. E.g., for the minister *politic* is a matter of great importance, for another it is unimportant. Few place importance in serious and important things, but in side matters. Some hold it unimportant to speak about the most important objects of religion and *moralitaet*, and act honestly either from custom, or from resolve, etc. To inform someone of the importance of a matter is very difficult. Of the importance of the end I can well speak. But the importance of the means is difficult. E.g., to form a courtier, for this end I can recommend speaking French, wide reading etc. etc. But the importance of the means I ignore. But logic abstracts from the importance of the content, and speaks of relative formal importance. A cognition is logically important when it is a ground of great consequences. When a cognition is the ground of many and numerous consequences, i.e., the fruitfulness of cognition. E.g., what makes up the *cultur* of our manners is unimportant, yet just as frequent, so that it is useful and fruitful. Virtue is more than *conduite*, and therefore belongs to importance. Cognitions are sufficient for inner use when from these marks everything can be understood that belongs to it; sufficient in outer use when in comparison they serve to see how things agree with others, or differ, absolutely no mark is sufficient, but always in this or that intention. E.g., marks are sufficient for *praxis*, but not for *speculation*.",[]],"wiener-s04-100":["Necessary marks cannot be separated at all from the concept of a thing, but belong *ad e sse*. Contingent ones are not constituent parts of the thing, but *acce ssoria*. E.g., reason is necessary in the human being. Erudition is contingent. – Extraessential are marks that do not belong to the thing as *constitutiva*, essential ones are *constitutiva* of the thing. Extension *ad essentiam corporis pertinens est*. Motion is extraessential, and *acce ssio* to the concept of body. Thus one can divide all *praedicata* into *ad e ssentiam pertinentia et extrae ssentialia*.",[]],"wiener-s04-101":["Essence is the *complexus notarum nece ssarium interne sufficientium*. In logic one speaks of the essence of concepts, not of things. The necessary marks of a thing are distinguished. Some belong to the thing *ut rationata*, as consequences of other marks of that very same thing, *aliae notae conceptus alieni non nisi ut rationes competunt*. E.g., the human being has a language, is an inner mark, but it comes from other marks, namely that he has reason, tongue etc. etc., but that he is an animal I can judge from nothing else. *Ideae conceptus quia sunt rationes, minime derivatae sunt e ssentiales*, i.e., those which lie in the concept not as consequences but as ground, those which *ad e ssentiam ut rationata pertinent* are *attributa*, these must be derived from the essential ones, *e ssentia ipsa est complexus e ssentialium*,",[]],"wiener-s04-102":["the attributes also belong to essence, but as consequences, and not as constituent parts of essence.",[]],"wiener-s04-103":["*Extrae ssentialia* are twofold, first *interna*: *modi*, or contingent constitution, 2.) *externae relationes* outer relations. All *relationen* are contingent, whereas the inner ones can sometimes be *e ssentialia*. E.g., erudition is extraessential, but in some it is nevertheless an inner determination, whereas to be master or servant are *relationes* concepts of relation. For on a desert island one is neither master nor servant, the entire outer marks are thus outside and inside things.",[]],"wiener-s04-104":["We speak of the essence of things according to the concept that we have of things, according to the logical concept. But we can also ask after the essence of the thing in and for itself. This is what makes up the essence, and necessarily belongs to it, even if it is not contained in our concept, in short, the first fundamental piece of a body.",[]],"wiener-s04-105":["This is the real essence. The other is the logical essence. One complains that the essence of things is unknown to us, namely the ground of everything that properly belongs to things. But nevertheless we can very easily cognize the logical essence. The logical essence signifies nothing more than the sum total of all marks that first make up a certain concept. *complexus omnium notarum conceptum aliquem primitive constituentium*. *complexus omnium notarum* i.e., a concept that is complete with regard to the marks which, taken together, make up the concept. To the logical essence there thus belongs the *totalitaet* of marks. They are to be primitive marks, i.e., those which belong to the concept not as consequences but as *constitutiva*. E.g., if I want to know the logical essence of bodies: then I find many marks. Among these some belong to the essence as consequences, others are the constituent parts, the *e ssentialia* of a concept, which is called *complexus e ssentiae*. Extension, impenetrability, figure are primitive, whereas divisibility etc. etc. are attributes. It is possible to give the logical essence of all things. For of all things of which we speak we must after all have concepts, and these we must after all explain, in order to be able to find their *e ssentialia* and *rationata*, and what belongs *primitive* and *derivative* to the essence.",[]],"wiener-s04-106":["To see into the real essence reaches beyond the human understanding. Of no single thing can we state a complete ground. For this there is required a universal complete experience, and to make all possible experience about one object is impossible; *a priori* and without all experience we can explain no thing of nature, because the understanding cannot speculate about what it does not know. The real essence is also called nature. If therefore I distinguish essence and nature: then I distinguish the logical essence from the real essence. If we abstract the marks of our concept: we have the logical essence. But if we investigate the innermost ground of a body: then I will cognize its nature, i.e., its real essence.",[]],"wiener-s04-107":["The matter of distinct and clear cognitions does not belong to logic. It is to teach the rules of the understanding in the case of representations that are encountered in us. We must therefore be conscious of the representations in order to know the rules with their combinations. But if we are not conscious of them: then we also cannot proceed with them so. Clarity therefore, where I am conscious of my representation, belongs to logic, to make obscure representations clear it is not capable. That we therefore assume obscure representations in the soul is no concern of logic, but how clarity is brought to distinctness, and this to exhaustiveness, how many clear representations are to be made universal, and compared, etc. Of obscure concepts psychology speaks. One calls representations obscure in comparison with those that [...] have the degree of clarity that one demands. When a representation distinguishes the thing from some but only few things, when the thing is appropriate to the comprehensibility of one, but not of all. There is thus also a logical obscurity, which is however distinguished from the psychological, of which one is not conscious at all. The logical is the comparative, the latter the absolute obscurity. E.g., the concept of *instinct* is an obscure concept. One knows well, it is an impulse to act, but that does not exhaust everything. – Comparative obscurity one can have in relation. In one relation, where it is sufficient for distinguishing, it is clear, in another, where it does not suffice for this, obscure. E.g., if a cognition *conscendirt* in relation to certain *subjecten*: then it cannot be otherwise than that it transcends the capacity of others. This is not to be avoided at all. But one must be very attentive in this. A teacher can speak very distinctly even if he understands nothing at all, in that he accommodates himself to the power of grasp of his listeners, usurps their *terminologien*, and therefore distinctly obscure to them ( H— ) [AA: ist ]. One must always speak relatively obscurely if one wants to ascend to a clarity that is to some extent universal. The teacher must through obscurity draw the people up to himself, so that thereby they learn universally distinctly, and not let himself wholly down to them. Distinctness is relative with regard to the human being, it must be accommodated to the speculative understanding, and not to common human understanding. Our object must be so constituted as we grasp concepts through abstraction. If one cannot: one must let it be, and not complain about obscurity. One must study what is appropriate to our power of grasp, and not find absolute sciences blameworthy, even if others can be drawn down deeper. With total and partial obscurity it is to be noted: A cognition cannot be wholly clear, *sed obscurae quaedam notae in conceptu claro universali notae singulares possunt esse obscurae universalis autem clarae*. E.g., one spark of light does not illuminate, but many make bright. So also with concepts. A representation that is clear in the whole, but is not so with regard to the parts, is an indistinct representation. E.g., blue and yellow make green, but with the green color we are not always conscious of these parts that lie in it, likewise with concepts. E.g., the concept of right. It is to designate what is taken straight and without circumlocution and cunning, but it is after all not as with a straight line. For each sees his own advantage, without also harming others. In short, much is required to set out the whole manifold of a concept, and afterwards to be able to develop again parts of the parts, and we have manifold concepts that we can cognize only in the whole. It is a piece of philosophy to give the deep distinctness of parts of parts. But at last one always comes to part-concepts that are simple, and can only become clear to us. E.g., reality, mathematical point. If we ourselves compose a concept voluntarily: then it can become wholly distinct. For because we have made it ourselves: we also know what we have brought into it, and are conscious of the part-representations. But if it is given to us: then we cannot always see into it distinctly.",[]],"wiener-s04-108":["|| V All clarity can be understood either subjectively or objectively. Through the objective the *object* becomes clear, it is the logical greater perfection, whose first degree is that we are conscious of it, and whose second degree is clarity in the parts, i.e., distinctness. Cognition grows subjectively through the magnitude of logical clarity, when the representations in the *subject* in relation to the rest of cognition excite more attention, and occupy the power of the mind more strongly. || V If a representation, instead of being obscured by others in the mind, obscures others: then this is that clarity which excites in the mind a greater alteration of state. We often need to affect our state through a representation, and to tense the incentives of the mind. This subjectively greater clarity is called vivacity. || V The vivacity of cognition accordingly belongs to aesthetic cognition and to sensibility. Here one must note exactly how vivacity is to be applied in the case of obscurity. A certain degree of vivacity can be used everywhere without detriment to obscurity, e.g., an apt expression. Indeed it is good, for without all vivacity no attention would be aroused. – || V In the sermon distinctness and correct analysis must come first, and then vivacity must also be given to the delivery, in order to excite attention, and to *commovere* the soul. This vivacity has its degrees. The intensive degree is the strength of cognition. || V The extensive degree depends on intuition, not on feeling, where through examples from common life, and cases *in concreto*, the thing is placed before the eyes. The more marks we bring in, in order through intuition to unwind the thing from the pure idea, the more extensively greater subjective clarity we bring in. This is the most necessary thing, that through it the concepts become more appropriate and more distinct to our understanding. For the understanding must direct purposively through sensibility. – || V Intensive vivacity rests on feeling, not on intuition, but on the strength of cognition. To feeling belongs that it paints the beauties, and produces emotion, i.e., movement of the mind. These are astonishment, fear, admiration etc. Through this we are put into the most unsuitable state for judging. || V If the intuition is already there: then feeling can well be added. For then we are already convinced, and could not easily lose the intuition through stupefying feeling. Everything that one does must happen from *principia*. Otherwise it is ambiguous, so that the same emotion leads me today to good, tomorrow to bad actions. || V Feeling excites tears, but nothing in the world dries sooner than tears. || V When the judgment has penetrated deep into the soul: sensations can well be taken along, but as subsidiary causes, and as it were as parts. || V But the majesty of religion is too great for one to be able to play with feelings in the pulpit. They must not be the nourishment but the companion of the soul, so that, when our soul is illuminated by cognition, a moderate emotion comes forth, and cheers it. Only this emotion must not shout down the intuition. This is the vivacity which the *aesthetic* has according to form. In the case of that which goes to intuition, not much emotion must be applied. For that acts on the understanding alone, which does not heed it. || V In order to teach exactly how this intuition and sensation must be combined, namely how one can give intuition to all conviction, and yet also admit sensation, one should read Sulzer, and especially his theory of the fine arts, or his preliminary exercises. One should strive for knowledge in history. Rousseau likewise shows much understanding in his writings, but lets himself be carried away too much by his enthusiasm, but he still has so very much that is well meant and true that one must leave it to each to distinguish it from his enthusiastic part. Hume too can be used for this aim.",[]],"wiener-s04-109":["The greatest logical perfection after clarity is distinctness. In the Wolffian logics only the analytic kind of distinctness is ever considered. There is, however, a far more extensive kind, namely the synthetic generation of distinctness. All analytic generations do not nourish cognition, they only resolve the cognition that is given to me, so that I now learn to distinguish better what was already contained in the cognition before. It thus does not grow according to content, but I only cognize it with more consciousness. Philosophy occupies itself especially with this kind, e.g., through morals I do not nourish my cognition, but only place it in a better light. Socrates said he was the midwife of his listeners, i.e., he brought it about that they reflected better on what they already knew, and became better conscious of it. If we always knew what we know, namely in the use of certain words and concepts that are so subtle in application: then we would be astonished at the treasure of our cognition. E.g., philosophers and jurists have never yet been able to develop and analyze the concept of right and equity. But one need only give someone a case *in concreto*, and he will soon say to what extent it is right, and to what extent it is equitable. In my concept of equity, then, there must after all be something that is distinguished from that of right, and I make use of this mark wrapped in obscurity *casu*. It is one of the greatest skills of a scholar who is not merely scholastically correct but also possesses *popularity* that, when he speaks with people who cannot bring their concepts to light at all, he can lift everything out of them, and tell them what they think. Thereby, e.g., the preacher satisfies his listeners in the most agreeable way. If someone should say, I have indeed not been so taught – Yes, but were you properly conscious of all that you knew? – This making distinct of the concept happens especially through *definitions*. But if this is accomplished: then it is also necessary to extend one's concept, and then distinctness grows synthetically, in that new marks are added. Platonic or Socratic questions are of the kind that they fetch out of the knowledge of the other what lies in it, in that one brings him to consciousness of what he actually thought. They are accordingly very different from catechism questions, where one asks only for what lies in memory. – Definitions have the highest degree of analytic distinctness, they are the final aim of all our concepts.",[]],"wiener-s04-110":["Healthy understanding has the particular feature that it runs ahead of speculations so strongly that we have always already brought things a good piece forward before we begin to speculate about them, e.g., in the concepts of happiness, equity etc. If someone makes an incorrect use of his concepts that he has had: then the concept is not only to be taken as *chicanery* and blameworthy, for our concepts always contain much reason in themselves, but if we do not develop them rightly: then we can sometimes stray from the right path. To make a distinct concept is the synthetic method, to make a concept distinct is the analytic. If a distinct concept is to be made: then one must first adduce marks, e.g., a certain object has impenetrability, weight etc., this I will call body. I accordingly feign a concept for myself, e.g., a republic without punishments, where each acts well for the sake of his own best. Such a *fiction* is a distinct concept that I have made myself. Mathematical distinctness is entirely synthetic. The mathematician says, whatever all people may have represented to themselves under circle, I think only that it shall be where a straight line moves about a fixed point. Here I have not made the concept of the circle distinct, but have made a distinct concept. Some say that in distinctness according to logical form, i.e., in the analytic, the *energy* of cognition vanishes, and aesthetic perfection loses its *pondus* thereby, if I always give someone to cognize distinctly what he thought confusedly. In the case of *illusion*, e.g., in the case of beauty, that may be true, but when the talk is of truth: then the dissection of cognition in this respect can be not a hindrance but a furtherance. The dissection must only not last so long that for those who are not aware of it the object itself finally goes out of sight thereby. For in going into the *minutissima* I often lose the representation of the whole. In analytic cognition I make a given concept distinct. The synthetic gives the concept together with the distinctness. The *mathematicus* makes concepts with distinctness. The *philosoph* makes concepts distinct. In that the former the *mathematicus* gives the concept, he at the same time states all the *partial* marks of which it consists. From this it is evident: the making distinct of *objects* happens synthetically, the making distinct of concepts analytically. In the synthetic I add through experience new marks to the concept of an object. It arises in such a way that the parts of cognition precede the whole cognition. In the analytic the whole precedes the parts. E.g., virtue I cannot define synthetically. For I am to say what we all think under the concept of virtue, not what I perhaps understand by it according to my own whim.",[]],"wiener-s04-111":["To comprehend, i.e., to cognize through concepts, the *author* calls *concipere*. This, however, is not quite right. For we have for *comprehendere*, i.e., to cognize through intuition *per apprehensionem*, no expression in German. The degrees of distinctness are",[]],"wiener-s04-112":["1.) the lowest degree is to represent something to oneself. If I cognize that which relates to the object: then I represent the object to myself.",[]],"wiener-s04-113":["2.) to cognize, *percipere*, means to represent something to oneself in comparison with others, and to have insight into its *identity* or difference from them. Thus to cognize something with consciousness. For animals also cognize their master but are not conscious of it.",[]],"wiener-s04-114":["3.) to understand something, *intelligere*, to cognize something in the understanding, not merely with consciousness. The understanding is the faculty of concepts Consequently to cognize something through concepts means to understand something, *concipere*.",[]],"wiener-s04-115":["4.) to have insight into something, to cognize with reason, not merely to understand, but according to such concepts as are universal according to determination. E.g., not merely what a house is, but also the usefulness, arrangement etc. of the house. – How few there are who have insight into something, even though they understand it. E.g., the powder manufacturer knows that it has a propelling force, that it has arisen thus and so. Very many attain to understanding. That is because they have certain rules of the understanding. But they do not have insight into it, *non perspiciunt*.",[]],"wiener-s04-116":["5.) *Comprehendere*, to comprehend, to have sufficient insight into how far something serves a certain aim. Concept without qualification is when I cognize from reason that it is sufficient for every aim. But in this way we can cognize nothing. The *mathematicus* has insight into the properties of the thing for many an aim, but not into their absolute sufficiency. Nonetheless *relatively* one can well have this. – This comprehending is something very *delicate*. People must actually comprehend what they learn, at least in the aim for which it is comprehensible. E.g., in matters of religion, secrets of nature, morals. For if he does not have sufficient insight into them for his aim: then he can make no use of them. Hence one must not at once speak of incomprehensible, even though in speculative aim it is not possible to cognize something. There is in us a particular use of reason, where it seems to us sufficient to have insight into something once the thing is there, so that we persuade ourselves we could also have had insight into that through our reason, but if we ask ourselves whether we could well have cognized that also *a priori*: then it does seem as if we could not have. Here belong all experiments – in natural science. To comprehend what I have determined *a priori*, it is indeed sufficient. E.g., an eclipse of the moon I can comprehend, because I know the course of the moon. But would I well, if I did not know that saltpeter had such a propelling force, have been able to know beforehand that the powder would burst so, even if I could define the powder ever so well, or manufacture it? In the end it turns out, when one goes into the matter, that we can say of only a few things that we have sufficient insight into them. Very many can define the concept. But from comprehending most are very far removed. – What lies in experience, one has tried to *demonstrate* *a priori*. E.g., the property of the lever is demonstrated very well in mechanics. But these cognitions always show something defective in themselves, from which it is evident that, if experience had not given us that, we would never have arrived at it. What therefore I cannot determine *a priori*, that I cannot comprehend, or have complete insight into. E.g., the refraction of the ray of light in water one can now already explain, and reason can make the things in experience quite comprehensible, but that we flatter ourselves we would have been able to have insight into such things *a priori* is false.",[]],"wiener-s04-117":["A concept is distinct whose marks are clear. Marks are *subordinated* or *coordinated*. The *subordinated* constitute a series, and if we go forward in the series of *subordinated* marks: then that is *profunditas*, thoroughness. We ask here first, which is the immediate mark of body? Extension. What is extension? etc. until we finally come to marks that constitute the fundamental concepts. *Profundity* is a progression in the series of *subordinated* marks, until we come to their boundaries. In the *coordination* or in the *aggregate* of the marks of a concept there is no determinate boundary, except when the concept is to become analytic. E.g., virtue is a facility in lawful actions, further facility in actions that are appropriate to the idea of the highest good, further mastery of the inclinations, and so on further.",[]],"wiener-s04-118":["The distinct concept that contains all the marks which taken together constitute the whole concept is an exhaustive concept, and this perfection, which contains all the marks taken together, is called *completudo*. We consequently attain in all cognition an exhaustiveness, namely that all marks are there which constitute the whole concept. – If a distinct concept lacks no mark: then an omission can still have occurred in the concept, namely that one, or several marks are too many, but one wants, in a cognition according to which my understanding is to be put into the proper activity, to have all, but not too many marks. It is an unnecessary waste of the attention of the understanding. This perfection, insofar as the concept does not *abound* in marks, is *precision*. The *author* calls the determinate alone *precise*, or as it were cut off. Mathematics is most excellent on account of *precision*. – The perfection that arises from *completudo* and *precision* united is the *adequate* perfection, *quando adaequatur objecto*, the cognition is *adequate* when it contains neither less nor more than is contained in the *object*. *Precision* in a concept one could call logical *elegance*. *Precision* is properly only negative perfection, in that I take away what *abounds* in marks in a concept. Wolff calls a cognition *adequate* insofar as it has *profundity* and intensive distinctness, and insofar as it is distinct through subordinate marks whose marks are in turn clear. We say, *notae simul sumtae adaequant conceptus*, and indeed correct ones. With those, that is already *adequate* where marks of marks are clear, whereas this is not sufficient for the reason that I can in turn make the marks of marks distinct. The Wolffian school thus has here a wavering concept. *Precision* is a perfection for estimating our cognitions, and becoming certain that there are in them just not too many, not too few marks. This exactness serves for guarantee. For whoever says less here than he should will say more there than he should. The lack of *precision* rests on *tautologies*, that I posit a mark twice, or in different ways. E.g., God is a being that is omnipotent, infinite etc. Here omnipotence already lies in the infinite. There are cases where *precision* cannot suitably be sought and where it causes indistinctness, although for a capable head it procures all the more distinctness. E.g., in the catechism one may well say a thing twice. For of two words that signify the same, one is often easier than the other. – The sum total of the marks that are exhaustive and measured in a given concept are appropriate to the concept, *adequate*. With connoisseurs the lack of *precision* is a great fault. But whoever renounces the demands of the understanding ones, and wants to teach only the ununderstanding, can relax somewhat from *precision*. – The *author* now treats of the clear head. Many can reflect thoroughly on things, but cannot give them lucidity, i.e., *popularity*. Scholastically correct perfection consists in the agreement of cognition with the rules mentioned before, namely that it be *adequate* to the concept. This is the logical or scholastic perfection, namely that which is determined by the rules of the understanding. The popular is aesthetic distinctness, and something becomes aesthetically distinct through examples, in that it is presented through intuition. The more I can combine my *abstraction* concepts with representations *in concreto*, the more *popular* my cognition becomes. *Popularity* is thus where cognition *in abstracto* is combined with cognition *in concreto*, and thus understanding and sensibility with one another. But the scholastic perfection is always the main thing. There are *authors* who are very popular, but if the logical perfection is lacking, they are unreliable, even though they captivate. – What is understandable even to the most common human being has the greatest degree of perfection that is aesthetic. What is clear to the most common man is certainly also clear to the speculative one. This highest degree of *popularity* must take place in religion and morals. But in this the logical perfection must in no way be neglected. For it is the foundation through which a cognition takes place as cognition, and is entitled to be *adopted* by the understanding. For many an end only a certain degree of logical perfection is needed, and the lowest degree of the logical is the highest degree of *popularity*. The moralist must by no means neglect the distinctness of concepts, but he must also have examples at hand, and as a good anthropologist represent human nature, and show the agreement of his doctrines with it. – If these two perfections combined can be comprehended even by the most common understanding, this is the greatest degree of lucidity, which is impossible for many people. Many a one can know the rules *in abstracto*, but does not have the facility of wit and strength of imagination to choose the deepest examples for the seeing-through of others. On the certainty of cognition. There are three degrees of taking to be true. Taking to be true and truth are distinguished subjectively and objectively. If the taking to be true is subjectively incomplete: then it is called persuasion. But a subjectively complete taking to be true is conviction, the state of the mind where I give assent to a cognition, the taking-to-be-true. This is the phenomenon of the human soul about which *reasoning* is done here, and not whether the cognition is objectively true.",[]],"wiener-s04-119":["The 3 degrees of taking to be true are opining, believing, knowing.",[]],"wiener-s04-120":["Opining is not yet conviction for otherwise it would after all have to be at least *subjectively* sufficient, for me in the situation of mind in which I find myself. In opining our judgment is *problematic*, i.e., I settle nothing, but I have only a degree of taking to be true, although this is not sufficient. Opining has nothing harmful, as soon as I am only conscious of the insufficiency of my taking to be true.",[]],"wiener-s04-121":["Opining is a taking to be true from a ground of cognition that is neither objectively nor subjectively sufficient. If I take something to be true, e.g., that there is a passage through the Arctic Sea, but am conscious that my cognition of the thing is still wavering, and insufficient with respect to the *object*: then it is an opinion. Where this does not *satisfy* even me myself, and I would not want to wager anything on it: then it is also subjectively insufficient. Brockes says, the human being is an animal that opines the philosopher a human being who takes his opinion to be true. Opinions accumulate the greatest part of our cognition. The first thing in a cognition is always a will, an obscure foreseeing, which generates in us a taking-to-be-true. In some sciences it is not permitted at all to opine. E.g., in duties one must not opine, but know. On a pure opining of a cognition one cannot venture to do anything.",[]],"wiener-s04-122":["In *speculations* one can perhaps opine something. In metaphysics opinions are spoken of insofar as they can be brought to the touchstone of experience. E.g., of pure spirits. Here it is shown that, because here there would be no end of opinion, it is not permitted to opine. Of possibilities one must not opine, they must be certain. If one says how one lives after death. Can I opine here? No, I must know, or believe. Here nothing is to be settled through experience, and unfortunately *a priori*. What can be proved *a priori*, that one must not opine, but know. What can at all be settled with reason, I must either know or believe. Opining counts for nothing here. – When I say opine, I have to make a claim to knowledge. In opining I always take a step toward knowledge. For it is an insufficient ground, to which complements must still be added in order to make it complete. I think to myself here a *complementum ad totum*, a supplementary piece to the whole. If a cognition is of such a kind that knowledge is impossible for it, then so is opining. E.g., from the need of my reason I have insight that human beings must have been created by a rational being, for human beings are born of their parents etc. But there must after all have been one who was the first, who was born of no human being. This is only a belief. For here there are subjective grounds. Why do I assume this so? Because my reason needs it. No knowledge is possible here, therefore also no opining. Thus it is impossible that the human being can know the continuance of his soul after death *a posteriori*. Here again it is belief, namely since everything in nature is purposive, but the human being has in need speculative reason, through which he is distinguished from other animals, that he asks about the first cause, motion, the sun etc. Here he cannot with reason fathom the first germs up to their final development. For life is too short, and in old age reason also does not have its proper strength. There must therefore be yet a place where human reason can attain to its proper development. Thus goes belief. –",[]],"wiener-s04-123":["In believing we judge *assertorically*, i.e., we declare ourselves for the truth, although it is subjectively sufficient only for us alone, and we cannot convince every other of it. I can have a subjective ground for taking something to be true, yet in such a way that this is constant, but not sufficient for me for every time, but rather my taking to be true is *restricted* only to the situation in which I find myself, for I can come to better insights. This is therefore only relative. But there is after all also a belief that one cannot call theoretical belief, but this is a *practical* belief, in which I cannot alter my taking to be true at all. Hence it is entirely as great as conviction, and the greatest *apodictic* certainty. This *practical* belief is the moral one. This is when, with my moral disposition, I assume something that is necessarily connected with it, which is regarded as entirely unalterable. E.g., that there is a God I can indeed have insight into from the purposiveness and order in the world. Yet many a thing also seems contrary to purpose. For this such speculative grounds are not sufficient, but morality alone. But if I am to make someone convinced of my belief: then it is presupposed that he has just such moral dispositions. Theoretical belief is indeed not changeable, if it is not supported by moral belief. For I can come to better insight, and encounter something contrary in nature, and then my taking to be true ceases. – To accept propositions of reason on belief is possible in only a single way namely in mathematics, because on account of the evidence one does not so easily err, and one at once has insight into the error. But in philosophical sciences it is absurd to accept asserted propositions without proof. In history I can also have a knowledge, if it is so constituted that it can be communicated to everyone. E.g., that London lies in England, because we hear just the same every post-day, and from so many agreeing people.",[]],"wiener-s04-124":["Believing is a subjectively sufficient, but *objectively* insufficient taking to be true. Subjective is always the sense. Knowing is a taking to be true objective with consciousness. Whoever undertakes something must venture something according to his opinion. This belief can be an entirely rational belief, and I can *subjectively* have so sufficient a ground for taking something to be true that I can take my cognition-resolution accordingly. E.g., sowing, setting sail etc. are grounded on this. One believes the grain would come up, the east wind would blow then, and then the west wind. Meanwhile one cannot be certain of all these things, and one has only subjective grounds of taking to be true. But now if I can take many a thing to be true on my own *account*, even if it were not *objectively* so certain: then I can still never have a subjective ground of taking to be true in morals, but rather *objective* truth must always reign therein. The judge cannot condemn anyone to death before he has deserved it according to someone's pronouncement, and no contrary takes place. That one has perhaps found someone bloody is indeed subjectively, but not objectively, sufficient for taking to be true. Someone can take something to be true according to a very rational ground, but of the very thing that he takes to be true he also cannot say that he knows it. – In knowing it does not depend on the ground of taking to be true. For whoever believes something perhaps takes it to be true with more attachment than one who knows it, but rather it depends on the right kind of taking to be true. Many cling to *practical* truths with much zeal, even though there is not logical truth in them. – The wager is a test by which one tries whether the other also takes to be true and knows with firm belief what he believes. Whoever will not wager has a desire to assert something boldly without its costing him anything. Often one will wager only one ducat [ Textlücke ]. Two already frighten many a one away from the wager. Whoever wagers life and limb is completely subjectively convinced. And there are such cases. E.g., whoever lets certain advantages of life go, and lives a holy life, because he knows he will be rewarded, has a strong belief. Whoever believes so strongly that he gives his life for it, it is as good as the greatest certainty. But one still cannot say he knows it, but he only believes it. E.g., mystery of religion. In mathematics no believing takes place. Here there must be knowing. For it would be ridiculous if someone said: I believe a triangle is a figure that consists of 3 sides and 3 enclosed angles. — [ Textlücke: AA: Der Glaube an ] the truth is only practically firm, and its ground is only subjective. To knowledge he attains through his conviction of understanding in the case of the most indifferent understanding, by which he becomes able to make what he knows distinct and certain to others too. Accordingly knowledge is indeed not necessary for the very firmest conviction. But one knows only then, when one can give just the same certainty to everyone, even to the most indifferent understanding. In brief we can grasp the distinction between a belief and knowledge thus: Opining is a subjectively and *objectively* insufficient taking to be true. Believing a subjectively sufficient, but objectively insufficient taking to be true. Thus believing is the opposite of opining, knowing a taking to be true sufficient both objectively and subjectively.",[]],"wiener-s04-125":["If a taking to be true goes merely to the subjective, without looking to the *object*, whereby another too could be convinced: then I say, I am persuaded. Of my subjective grounds of taking to be true I can give no account, they hold only for me, and for no other. If I say, I cannot persuade myself of the matter: then that holds only for me, I indeed see that another, who does not know things so well and cannot distinguish them, might well persuade himself. – Every language has marks of sincerity, of which an honest man at once believes that the matter is so meant in the heart. But this I cannot express to another, how what is truthful in the tone and in the words affects me, and what moves me to taking to be true, I say, I am persuaded of it. – If I say, I am convinced: then this too is still a subjective ground. For it goes only to the subject. But there is also a subjective necessity there, thus a ground must after all lie in the object. Otherwise the state could well change in me too. There lies something in my taking to be true that lets me believe I will never depart from it, I believe I find a ground in the object, even if I cannot make it distinct. – Who is most easily persuaded? Children. For their judgments are merely subjective. They believe what they wish. Their cognition seeks to extend itself, and hence inexperience brings it about that they accept what pleases them, and do not even inquire after what the understanding requires. But people too can be persuaded who are *praeoccupirt* by something, i.e., one opinion has gained a foothold in a human being, and the other now no longer finds the place empty. Human beings are very commonly *praeoccupirt*. For the judgments of our youth come first, and hence later ones no longer find room. For the understanding cannot entirely clear that away again, since it is difficult to achieve something from all impressions, just as in *Jure* the *prior occupans* is always the most fortunate. To *praeoccupation* belongs *praedilection*, when through love one is too much inclined toward someone, when what our patrons, friends etc. have asserted flows into our judgments as well, so that we take these judgments to be objective. If the human being gives assent to such a judgment: then he does not know what actually determines him, whether understanding, idea or sensibility. Sensibility can exercise a mixed effect only together with the understanding. If only we could always distinguish what sensibility and understanding do: then the influx of sensibility would not harm us. But if the judgment rests on *praeoccupation*: then these influences are hard to notice. In taking to be true, therefore, it is the hardest question whence it arises, if sensibility does have much effect: then the matter is nevertheless not to be rejected on that account. For the understanding also has its share in it, and it knows grounds enough as regards the object. But if our assent also has for the most part objective grounds: then we must nevertheless be distrustful, especially when human beings are divided over the matter, and we must then investigate which of the two opinions has subjective grounds. Now how can one distinguish conviction and persuasion? We distinguish these two even through feeling. One says, I am *persuadirt* that this story cannot have happened so, as far as I can judge according to the course of things, I must declare myself against this story. I am convinced when the matter is so logically perfect that I can communicate it to another, when I represent to myself that in all dangers I would hold the matter to be true, were I then to waver, then I would not actually be convinced. But if I believe I risk nothing, even if I set my entire interest as a pledge: then I am convinced.",[]],"wiener-s04-126":["If we therefore opine: then this is a cognition that does not even have subjective grounds, it is the beginning of taking to be true. Belief already signifies a subjectively sufficient taking to be true, which seems not to be objectively sufficient, but nevertheless has all sorts of grounds that are determined in the understanding. Believing always presupposes conviction. For there is after all something firm in it. If someone says, I believe that could happen: then he only opines, and this is only a confusion of words. For he would also gladly let himself be taught otherwise. The cause of belief is, e.g., in the hope of another world, when another cannot prove to us the opposite, and the impossibility of it, and one has ground in the understanding for it. E.g. the unequal distribution of good and evil. Meanwhile the subjective ground, which is grounded on *moralitaet*, cannot be added to the objective ground. The belief is then firm which carries a rational human being away to set the advantages of his life behind belief. Whom duty and hope, combined, impel to renounce all advantages, he believes and is convinced. This taking to be true does not yield to the very greatest certainty with respect to the effect on the subject, and practical conviction is the highest possible. This practical conviction can fall upon certain propositions, and then these are morally certain propositions. These are the ground of all *moralitaet*, and agree with our greatest conscientiousness when we live in accordance with them, and thus subordinate our actions to them. Of the belief that the apparent *disharmonie* of good and evil will cease, everyone can see that no one will rob him of it, and therefore I ask: do I have ground enough to accept it? Yes, a practical one, but not a logical one. But it hangs together so firmly with reason. For before, the greatest *Mathematicus* could demonstrate nothing, as if I would not let the belief be robbed from me even for my life.",[]],"wiener-s04-127":["Even if this conviction serves not for speculative but only for practical convictions. E.g. the concept of God is not given for *speculationen*, but for practical exercise. If one thus tones down certain propositions, which human beings pass off as a science, so far that one gives them practical but not logical convictions: then one has taken nothing from them, but has only taken from human beings the self-conceit that they believe they can have insight through speculative grounds. The practical grounds appear thereby in a brighter light, in that human beings are led back to their interest. Sensible certainty is divided into certainty of the senses, and empirical certainty. Senses alone do not yet make experience, rather experience is the judgment of the understanding about the combination of our sensible representations. Sensible certainty is accordingly nothing other than the certainty of the cognized relation of the senses. – Senses and understanding make up all our knowledge. The senses give appearance. The understanding connects them, and that makes experience, and experience is thus the cognized connection, or unity of appearances. Human beings must thus combine intuitions *objective*, in order to be able to say something of them. There are logicians who speak only of the certainty of the senses, whether our sensible representations are true in themselves, i.e., whether they have *objecte*, or whether they are for the most part only a certain sense that corresponds to the *object*. E.g. We dream. This we take to be just as true as when we are awake, whether now such representations are real as to the *objecte*, or ideal, whether they are as they appear to us, or whether they give us only the way in which the senses are affected, or whether they are only deceptions of our *Imagination*, this question belongs not in *Logic* but in *Metaphysic*, where the origin of illusion and of truthfulness from sense is spoken of. – In the end it comes to this, that there is neither truth nor falsehood in the senses, and that both lie only in the use which our understanding makes of sensible representations. For the senses do not judge at all, and therefore they do not contain errors, not because they never judge falsely, but because they do not judge at all. – Here therefore the question can only be of the certainty of empirical cognition. This is a cognition of *objecte* through the understanding, insofar as they are presented to the understanding. All empirical certainty concerns only the relation of sensible representations, how certain representations can be compared with one another. This sensible certainty we distinguish from rational cognition. This can distinctly be expressed in two ways.",[]],"wiener-s04-128":["1.) A cognition of reason, if its object is a mere *object* of reason.",[]],"wiener-s04-129":["2.) A rational cognition concerns the form, how I cognize an object through reason, its *object* may be empirical or rational.",[]],"wiener-s04-130":["Some cognitions are merely objects of reason, e.g. morals, these are material rational cognitions. Of the constitution of rational cognition as to the object *Metaphysic* treats. *Logic* treats of the second part, namely of the formal. Our cognition can comprehend objects of experience, and our certainty can nevertheless be either empirical or rational. E.g. the proposition of the power by means of the lever has empirical certainty, but also rational. For even if experience taught us nothing of it: I would still be able to cognize it through reason. That certain things are unequal, experience can say. But that they must necessarily be so, reason teaches. It thus does after all heighten certainty, in that it presents the cognition as necessary. – Sometimes we can have no rational certainty. Then we must content ourselves with the empirical. This is especially in natural science. But of that of which we can obtain rational certainty, the cognition is preferable. – With things which, had we not learned them from experience, we would never have arrived at, the certainty of experience is the best. But if we cognize such grounds as are necessary, and through which we could also have foreseen it: then rational certainty has the preference. All our cognition is either from concepts, or from the construction of concepts. The first is called discursive, the other intuitive, and thus also all rational certainty discursive or intuitive. Rational certainty, insofar as it is intuitive, is mathematical certainty, insofar as it is discursive, philosophical certainty. The mathematical is thus distinguished from the philosophical, they are both certain, are both cognized through reason, but the one certainty is discursive, the other intuitive.",[]],"wiener-s04-131":["A moral and a mathematical proposition are both apodictically certain, i.e., they are combined with the consciousness of necessity in contrast to the empirical, which is only assertoric. Both can accordingly convince completely, even if they are distinguished by discursive and intuitive. Hence one calls mathematical certainty evident, manifest, i.e., that the objects are presented not *in abstracto* through concepts, but in a single intuition. But if the question is of the degree of certainty: then one cannot change philosophical propositions into mathematical ones. Some come very close to *Intuitionen*, but are nevertheless still distinct. Wolff committed the error that he wants to judge discursively in *mathematic*. E.g. *definition* of the similarity of triangles. The proposition: do right, may not be constructed. For it is already clear. On the contrary one would get out the proof 2+2=4 through no concept. Hence philosophical and mathematical certainty cannot be confused at all. Our *autor* now speaks of the *modis judicii veritatis et falsitatis*, or of the *modis* of taking to be true.",[]],"wiener-s04-132":["1.) One says a cognition is universally accepted, i.e., when it finds assent with all, although in itself it is not yet certain. This is thus a merely external mark, from which certainty is not yet to be inferred.",[]],"wiener-s04-133":["2.) A cognition is settled, i.e., it is accepted as a universally cognized truth.",[]],"wiener-s04-134":["3.) A cognition is undeniable, indisputable, it is impossible to doubt the truth of the cognition.",[]],"wiener-s04-135":["A cognition is settled in a twofold sense. First, when it is objectively certain, i.e., indisputable, or also *subjective*, when one has accepted that a cognition is cognized as certain. In natural science there are many settled, but also a multitude of accepted propositions. Undoubted propositions one does not call settled, because namely there can no longer be dispute about them. E.g. mathematical propositions. Against some physical problems objections can still be found, if cognition of nature is only contingent, one also may not assert such apodictic necessity in one's judgment. A cognition one calls thorough when it is *adaequat* to the nature of that same cognition in general.",[]],"wiener-s04-136":["Thorough cognitions are different according to the difference of their nature. A moral discourse is thorough in a different way than a physical one. Thoroughness is not a logical perfection, or completeness of certainty, but only that perfection which is appropriate to the nature of this cognition. Someone can be a very thorough teacher of his congregation if he *aptirt* the truths to his listeners' power of comprehension, and this very discourse could be less thorough for *doctores theologiae*, if it is to be a test piece, where one would look to those *principia* that are laid as ground in a cognition according to its nature.",[]],"wiener-s04-137":["Shallow cognitions indicate something other than superficial ones. Shallowness indicates a lack of depth of cognition, that one does not go out to the high sea with one's cognition, but stays near the shore, and does not pursue a cognition to its first grounds. Certain cognitions float, like stubble, on top, but are graspable to the common understanding, and appropriate to the end, these are shallow cognitions. If one wants to have a cognition that is to go beyond the boundaries of the common understanding, e.g. if one wanted to be taught about precise *Subtilitaeten* that reason must have in *Metaphysic*: then that would be superficial if it were only appropriate to the common understanding. If I wanted to instill in him merely the first concepts of *metaphysic*: there is a God, an immortal soul: then I would give him a shallow, but to the end appropriate, cognition. One also calls a cognition superficial when someone knows the titles of books without himself understanding anything of the science.",[]],"wiener-s05-001":["We can see from our expressions: I accept that, concede that, or I withhold my assent, that in our assent there must be something that is voluntary, where we must determine ourselves whether we want to take the cognition to be true, or not. We want therefore to investigate how far our judgment here depends on the will. The understanding can subject itself to our will on no other ground than insofar as it is put into use. Otherwise its conviction rests on grounds, not on resolutions. Now since the will is a great cause through which certain convictions arise: the will has *indirecte* an influence on this understanding. If we could at once convince ourselves through the understanding of what we would gladly wish: then each would make himself through the understanding *chimaeren* as happy as he can, if namely these *chimaeren* which the will brings forth did not have to yield to the refutation of the understanding. – Certain cognitions",[]],"wiener-s05-002":["are such that, even if we find nothing faulty in the motives, we nevertheless, for the sake of the similarity of this cognition with others in which we have often erred, although we find nothing suspicious in it, are distrustful with our judgment, that is, *suspensio judicii*, the resolve not to let a provisional judgment become a determining one. There are here indeed grounds for taking to be true that are more certain than the grounds of the opposite. We can have grounds, but do not hold them to be determined, and so we distinguish the provisional from the determined judgment. We can communicate this withheld assent in two ways. *Suspensio judicii indagatoria* is when one still wants to seek out the grounds of the determined judgment. That is properly a postponement of the judgment, in order to inquire further into it in time, or *renunciatio judicii*, in that we renounce our judgment, and give up all hope of determining something. Scepticism *renuncirt* all judgments with respect to various things. E.g. whether the soul is corporeal. The philosopher does not yet hold this matter to be decided, and *suspendirt* his judgment.",[]],"wiener-s05-003":["To withhold one's assent is a faculty of a practiced power of judgment, and is not found in beginners and in youth, but only with increasing age. For our cognitive faculty is so eager to spread itself that with the greatest impatience, as soon as an occasion offers itself, it seeks to extend itself. This propensity to accept the apparent as true is so great in young minds that it is very difficult for them to withhold their judgment. But when they see how one can be misled into passing a judgment that one must take back, i.e., when they have been made wise through much experience: then they *suspendiren* more. – It is a difficult action to withhold one's judgment, even for him who has a strong power of judgment, to maintain himself long in that state. For the human being has a propensity at every moment to accept one of two propositions as true. It is almost never so perfect in the human being that he should be entirely indifferent, and even if he withholds it: he nevertheless always has more inclination to the one than to the other side. – One often withholds one's judgment so that in the sequel one often need not take back one's judgment, the more one lays claim to the honor of a grounded insight, insofar as one might lose in one's reputation thereby. The greater the *autoren* are, the more cautious they are in their expressions about matters that are still *controvers*, and present their propositions problematically,",[]],"wiener-s05-004":["not assertorically This withholding is always a reserve before this, that one places a distrust in one's judgments, however grounded. – From *suspensio judicii* it is distinct when I do not treat a matter at all, since the decision of it does not belong to my aim at all, so I leave something *in dubio*. This is *judicium anceps*, and is childish, whereas *suspensio judicii* is manly, and takes place in a man of experience. – To the [AA: *judicium* ] *an* [ Textlücke: AA: *-ceps* ] is opposed the *judicium* [AA: *praeceps* ], which is still worse. This is something other still than the suspending one. When I suspend: I do not have grounds enough for a determined judgment. I leave it *in dubio* when it is not at all in keeping with my end whether it is true or not true. Provisional cognitions make up so great a science that it could be a very useful chapter for logic, if it were not too deep for it to decide, e.g., how provisional judgments can be made into determined ones. For this belongs the most precise acquaintance with the objects themselves. Hence *Logic* cannot speak sufficiently of the nature, utility, boundaries, requisites and condition of provisional cognition. That would rather become an *organon* of *philosophie*, which one probably does not yet have at present. – Every human being judges provisionally in what he first judges, e.g. from the face. The first look, bearing, clothing that someone has in society must judge him, although that is not sufficient. But the provisional judgment commonly has, and indeed because it is the first, so strong an impression on the human being that afterward the determined judgments through the strongest grounds cannot extinguish the provisional ones. We judge provisionally of a book by the title. But provisional judgments can gradually have a greater approximation to determined judgments. In *meditiren* provisional judgments must come first. I take this along, and believe it fits into the interconnection. Afterward in the whole survey the determined judgments perhaps turn out entirely differently. Our understanding notices provisionally something of what it is first itself to discover. We accept the truth, and have a scent of the key to our cognition. Our understanding is thus *dirigirt* by them, in that they give us the means to arrive at the end of cognition.",[]],"wiener-s05-005":["Determined is that which is sufficient to us for assent. A provisional judgment must not be held to be determined. The opponent may, in refutation, have passed a good provisional judgment. There one must through modesty bring him to the point that he *suspendirt* his judgment.",[]],"wiener-s05-006":["If only we are conscious that it is a provisional judgment: then we can always judge. *Praevia judicia*, i.e. provisional judgments, are of great importance, in that one has therein certain maxims for the provisional investigation of a matter. In every matter one must after all always judge something beforehand. E.g. In searching for metal in mines I must already have a provisional ground why I dig precisely in this mine. We must judge provisionally in *meditiren* where the truth might well be. The logic that would state the maxims of provisional judgments would be of great importance, it would actually be a *Hevristic* of new truths, which however is not yet invented. One judges provisionally where, before one judges determinately, one has certain grounds to direct one's investigation more to the one than to the other *object*. In the prospect of the objects, in which I seek a future cognition, I judge something so that I can afterward undertake experiments on it. Before all invention investigation must after all be undertaken. For by pure luck one finds nothing without tracking, and without guidance. – As soon as one *meditirt* something, one already makes oneself plans, before the execution of which a certain halved judgment about the properties that are still to be discovered runs ahead. If all this were *illustrirt* through examples of inventors, how through provisional judgments they had hit upon this and that: then that would give guidance to arrive at the discovery of more cognitions. The judge in *criminal*-law could make use of it as of a matter of great importance. One also calls provisional judgments *anticipationes*, where one *suspendirt* one's determined judgment. One has not yet resolved upon determining judgments. One thus *anticipirt* these to a certain extent. Epicurus called *anticipation* the concepts of our understanding which he presupposes before experience. From these provisional judgments prejudices are very much distinct, and are precisely the opposite of them. For without or instead of judging provisionally one determines. – One must distinguish investigating and reflecting. Some propositions can be accepted without investigation, e.g. Whether the whole is greater than its parts. Although here we may not distinguish: it is nevertheless necessary to reflect,",[]],"wiener-s05-007":["i.e., to seek out the interconnection of a cognition with our cognitive power, from which it is to spring. Our cognitive faculty is manifold, and all its powers are in play. They all run through one another, and each acts on the *objecte* that suits it, and we always seek out the appropriate cognitive faculty for it, without its causing us trouble. E.g. the proposition between two points only one straight line can take place is put before the understanding for reflection, and intuition tests whether one can draw only the one here. This one must always do, even with cognitions that seem not to need to be investigated. For if a cognition springs from the influence of a cognitive power which with respect to this *Object* has no validity at all: then the cognition must be either rejected, or suspended. I must see whether the senses, or the understanding, or *imagination* are in play here. For on this indeed rest all errors, when sensibility flows into the understanding. When one believes one has this through the understanding, and sensibility has had a secret influence thereby: then errors arise. – One is accustomed to say, from nothing comes nothing. We must after all have a material from which we make cognitions. This matter we cannot ourselves bring forth. Thus everything must after all come from experience, and thus nothing is ever evident except when reason alone had made it. If you want to convince yourself through understanding alone, whence will you take that? – This conviction cited is necessary even where investigation seems to be impossible. E.g. In mathematical propositions. We accept some judgments without conviction and without attention to the cognitive power which has an influence on the judgment, and from this arise prejudices. Prejudice is the mechanism of reason in principles. A prejudice is a *principium* of judging from subjective causes which are regarded as objective grounds. Subjective causes all lie in sensibility. Objective grounds lie in the understanding. Accordingly, because sensibility has influence, the judgment cannot be true. – A prejudice is accordingly a *principium* of judgment, reason in principles. I cannot say that is a prejudice which, e.g., this man says of his son, that he himself indeed has a good mind, but is seduced by others, although the son himself is a bad human being, but rather it springs from a prejudice. Erroneous judgments from prejudices are not themselves prejudices, hence actual prejudices are a source of many false judgments in the principle and *principium* of them. In the example cited the proposition is taken from a *principio*, and yet has the *effect* of a *principium*. One must with a prejudice always reflect, where does that come from? E.g. the apple does not fall far from the trunk, one could call a prejudice, because it is a universal proposition, and thus a *principium*. Yet one could also say that it arose from older prejudices that children have from their parents. – All prejudices are *principia* for judging. From them therefore a judgment must arise. Someone has found a proposition asserted thus and so by Leibniz. Now he holds it to be thorough, although he does not at all have insight into the proof. This is no prejudice, but the inclination from a prejudice. The prejudices of known merits give great presumptions that everything that is said, e.g., by Wolff, Leibniz etc. will be meritorious. – Prejudice is a *principium* of judging, not from objective grounds, but if all grounds of the judgment are drawn not from the *Object* but subject: then they are drawn from such sources. I may therefore say to myself only: it seems so to me, and not, the object is so. All grounds must thus properly be objective. The objective grounds are opposed to the subjective causes. With grounds I think only of something universal, and with subjective causes a certain state in the human being acts. – Habit brings it about that propositions to which we have long adhered easily become quite necessary to us, so that at last we make a ground for judging from them, and so one can make *principia* of judgments out of pure habits. E.g. it is habit to have a superstitious esteem for someone, because namely this has always been so in former times, so there springs from it a *principium* of judging, i.e., a propensity that has become our own through frequent practice. From this it is evident how a *principium* arises from subjective causes. – One judges critically of others' maxims, and holds one's own to be good. Here the prejudice is *suum cuique pulchrum*, whose subjective cause is self-love In the manners of others themselves it is often nothing improper, if we hold it to be such.",[]],"wiener-s05-008":["Here the understanding is only led astray, and takes that for objective grounds, and that then causes us to judge through admixture",[]],"wiener-s05-009":["of sensibility. The principal sources of prejudices are accordingly subjective causes that are falsely held to be objective grounds. They serve as it were instead of *principien*, because prejudices must be *principien*. The principal sources of prejudices are especially imitation, habit and inclination.",[]],"wiener-s05-010":["Idea of them is a ground for judging from subjective causes and each also counts as a *principium* for judging, i.e., it is of universal influence. Thus",[]],"wiener-s05-011":["1. Imitation. The human understanding seeks to extend itself, and in the ignorance that is found in children and the common man it extends itself through others' examples, in that it imitates others' thinking. Imitation is thus a ground for taking something to be true which the other has said. E.g. What all human beings assert must be true. Here belong all proverbs.",[]],"wiener-s05-012":["2.) Habit is the second source of prejudices. It acts most strongly in the old, just as imitation takes place more in the young than in the old. Young people have not yet lived long enough to let habit become so important in them. But if someone is old: then he cannot so easily take on another shape. Indeed habit acts so strongly in old people that one cannot bring them away from it at all, especially if it has moreover arisen from imitation. There the human being is entirely *incurabel*. For imitation makes all one's own reflection impossible. It brings it about that, if e.g. an *autor* wanted to present a new system in a science, he has many difficulties in the beginning. For time must first pass before such a cognition holds up the stream of habits, and gradually brings it to a standstill, and then gives it the opposite direction. Thus prejudices from habit can only be removed through length of time.",[]],"wiener-s05-013":["3.) Inclination or aversion. *Praedilection* or inclination is when one, before one has investigated a matter, is already in advance favorably prepossessed for it.",[]],"wiener-s05-014":["Imitation is most contrary to the free use of our reason, because here we early accustom ourselves to make use of others' judgments instead of our own. One can divide the free use of reason into the active and into the passive use, and judgments of imitation are *habitus* of a passive use. I make use of my reason actively when I myself derive something from the laws of nature. It is harmful to present certain men to youth as masters, so that they must parrot their language or expressions. For from this arises a passive reason, and that is after all *contradictio in adjecto*. For all reason is by its nature a self-active *principium* of thinking, and even when in thinking I lay experience as ground, it nevertheless makes universal rules of experience. The use of reason thus consists in self-activity, but the laziness of the human being brings it about that they would rather behave *passive* than that they should raise their cognitive power so far as to make use of their own powers. They accordingly have merely subjective and historical cognitions, because they merely parrot. Such conduct is a fertile soil for rampant prejudices, and it hinders all improvement and progress in a matter, and yet it is so universally spread, because it is so very appropriate to the human propensity to ease. There is therefore nothing more harmful than when one accustoms pupils to imitate the author or rather to ape him.",[]],"wiener-s05-015":["A principle of imitation are sentences and proverbs. Proverbs are expressions that are taken from common life and from the language of the rabble. The first source of proverbs is commonly prejudices. E.g. The apple does not fall far from the trunk, and the like. Sentences are aphorisms that indicate wide reading, and since they thus to a certain extent belong to erudition: so those make use of them who give themselves airs and want to show their erudition. – A formula is a rule whose expression itself serves as the model of the application. One has only thereby to come to the aid of memory: Thus one has formulas in algebra, grammar, *jus* etc. etc. Even the greatest scholar makes use of such formulas; when he has at the beginning pondered over the rules, he then makes himself, in order afterwards to proceed more easily in the application, formulas, so that he, when he knows the formulas, at once knows all the *actus* that he has to observe. They are accordingly of great importance, but mere imitation of the formulas does not bring about such good things. – A saying is a proposition whose expression has, in the indication of its sense, a certain measuredness. If, then, an expression is pregnant, and in this full content there nevertheless reigns such a measuredness of the expression that it seems one could not with fewer words deliver so comprehensive a presentation, – that – is a saying. Sayings serve as laws. Hence one calls passages of holy Scripture *dicta*. *Dicta* from philosophers one thus cannot give, as the disciples of Pythagoras, the *acusmatici*, did, in that they said: [ Textlücke: AA:αὐτὸς ἔφα. ]",[]],"wiener-s05-016":["Mechanism in matters of the understanding comes very much to the aid of the understanding in some respect, if I have first thought it through myself. But to accept such things without conviction is also a fruitful source of errors. Such a mechanism reigns in formulas, i.e., general propositions clothed in quite precisely determined words. Of this kind are",[]],"wiener-s05-017":["1.) proverbs, *formulae vulgares*, which hold for the common man's understanding. But to accept these without any examination is not advisable.",[]],"wiener-s05-018":["2.) Sentences, aesthetic formalia, formulas of prescription, in which a rule of conduct is contained. Such things one finds in poets, like Horace.",[]],"wiener-s05-019":["3.) *Canones*, formulas of science. Such things one has in all sciences, and they serve to be able to present the matter more easily.",[]],"wiener-s05-020":["4.) *Dicta*. Formulas of faith in theological matters, where the words themselves are held to be holy, and thus are accepted without any alteration.",[]],"wiener-s05-021":["All these now, if they have first taken root in me without mature reflection, are a root of unspeakable errors.",[]],"wiener-s05-022":["Sayings must thus always be borrowed from someone else. – Sentence does not, to be sure, always signify *dictum*, but has a similarity to it in that it recommends itself through the forcefulness of the thoughts. A sentence is when what is rational, expressed with forceful words, recommends itself to the *sens commun*. A saying is when something seems to be drawn from the common understanding and yet expresses a measuredness with the words belonging to it. *Bons mots* are general propositions that are recommended more by wit than by a rich power of judgment. –",[]],"wiener-s05-023":["A sentence has much advantage. For it serves as a foundation, and is entertaining for the mind, in that with little content much is thought, and this little is suited to everyone's reason.",[]],"wiener-s05-024":["*Canones* are doctrinal sayings that look more to the content of the expression. They are general propositions that serve as a foundation in sciences. A canon can also be expressed through sentences, and then it pleases all the more. Proverbs, mottoes are the most customary expressions in the popular judgment of the understanding and of reason. Something can be an expression of the healthy understanding, and yet is not popular. Then it is indeed a sentence, but not a proverb. Proverbs contain the current wit of the people, but yet expressed in a concise way, so that it is distinguished from the usual turns of speech. They give the common understanding of the people to be cognized, and always have something provincial about them. For they are commonly clothed in national images. They are *canones* for the commonest rabble. For they are the rules that already took root at the first arrangement, and thus hold generally. Hence one also finds that they do not take place among people of refined upbringing. But if someone puts forward a canon, e.g. from an *autor classicus*, in the refined world: then that is very gladly accepted. Prejudices are thereby very much tested, if one wants to ground wisdom on sentences and proverbs, because one always looks around for what others have said. From this there always arise partialities for the opinions of this or that famous man and philosopher. These were the prejudices from imitation. Habit shows its effect when one has long had one and the same way of thinking, and now is all at once to depart from it.",[]],"wiener-s05-025":["He has to struggle with many difficulties who wants to present something new in religion, metaphysics etc. But time brings it about that a proposition with the grounds of proof afterwards strikes the eyes so clearly that people must wonder how they could not have had insight into it before. The first judging of a thing that is new always takes place according to just the prejudices that one wanted to root out precisely by this thing. If one struggles against a prejudice: it defends itself, as it were, by itself. For one cannot so soon be brought away from the propensity toward some pronouncement, and it is just with this propensity, and not with reason, that one then has to do. For with reason it would go more easily. This lasts until finally the inclination becomes more general. Then it is also accepted by all. – The question is: is it necessary for a teacher and advisable to leave prejudices untouched, or even to favor them? So that they gradually take deeper root in the minds of the listeners, it is just as much as if one asks: is it permitted to deceive people with a good intention? The intention may be as good as it will, yet the means is worse than the best intention that one can have.",[]],"wiener-s05-026":["Untouched one could indeed leave them, because one can content oneself with letting an error pass, if only one does not strengthen it; one would only bring the human being out of his old habit, and yet one could not so soon accustom him. – But it is nevertheless advisable to uncover all errors and prejudices, if one is completely convinced of the correctness of one's insight. For in the sequel people do see that one is right. One must thus investigate the procedure of human beings everywhere, and uncover to them what they commit out of prejudices. The disadvantage that one suffers at first through the resistance is richly made good. We do indeed also have a calling not to *cachiren* but to *promulgiren* the general commands of reason, and if we keep silent, it seems, as it were, as if we held people not worthy to know it. – Some human beings do not need an insight into certain things, e.g. into speculative matters, without which they can get along quite well with their conscience, and which do not have the least influence on their actions. But if their prejudices are of practical disadvantage: then present what you know better than they, and believe that the disadvantage that you might have at the beginning is smaller than that practical advantage of the others. Now we come to the positive and negative prejudices. But we can take both on one footing. For with the negative one very soon also understands the positive. They are prejudices of authority, of trust in the multitude, in ages of a people. In all these points prejudices can reign, i.e., where the senses run ahead of the understanding. Hence the understanding, which is already prepared for it beforehand, shows in its judgment a *propension* to have respect perhaps for the erudition of a man, or conversely to place a greater trust in the common understanding than in erudition. Of this point there will be treated in particular below. These prejudices are grounded on a *propension* to assent to the opinion of others. All prejudices by which we imitate others, in that we ground our prejudice on the authority of other human beings, one can call the servile prejudices",[]],"wiener-s05-027":["call. Yet there is also a self-loving prejudice, when one regards the works of one's mind with a *praedilection*, and finds the children of one's understanding free of faults.",[]],"wiener-s05-028":["Prejudices of the authority of a person are servile prejudices. – In historical matters the authority of other persons is absolutely necessary, and makes up the foundation of our prejudices in many cases. For things that rest on two testimonies I cannot bring out of myself, because my own use of the understanding is not sufficient to determine the truth of history, which rests on experience. Here I can build without prejudice on the report of others, indeed it is necessary in this case, for because I cannot attain them through my own experience: so they must come from such persons as stand with us in such authority that we attribute belief to them. This acceptance of what another says is not grounded on a prejudice, but it is a condition that is valid enough for our understanding to give it approval. But if the cognition has its ground of proof in nothing other than in reason, but does not take it from experience: then the testimony of others cannot be a ground of conduct, and just this judgment, which runs ahead of our understanding and makes in us without any examination a *propension* to take something to be true, is the prejudice of authority, if I, e.g., wanted to believe the immortality of the soul because Plato said it. Truth, insofar as it rests on reason, holds anonymously, i.e., one must not ask who said that, but what he said. It does not matter here to which family the cognition belongs, and whether he who said it was of great merits, but what he said. – One complains that matters of state and religion brought to light so many anonymous writings, by which one forged arrows against the matters. To be sure, if the human being is in himself malicious: then it is still worse if he acts anonymously, but as concerns the matter itself, this must hold anonymously, the human being may conduct himself as badly or well as he will. If a noble and upright man says something bad: then it is not on that account less bad because he is upright. – The high esteem of a nation for a certain man is harmful on this account, because it brings the human being to laziness, not to establish himself firmly in their cognition, but to let others rule over it. Locke says of this, the authority of great men serves for disputes, often *ad verecundiam*, when one wants to silence others with it, because a great man said it. This propensity toward the authority of great men springs from the habit of imitating what is prescribed to us as certain, and from lack of insight, in that we always seek out a guarantor on whom we must be able to rely, and who, as it were, has understanding for us, and to whom we can always appeal. – The prejudice of the authority of the person causes something particular in our inclinations because it serves to flatter our vanity *indirecte*. For just as subjects of a mighty *despot* are proud, even if they live in the lowest estates, because, namely, they all are nothing in comparison with him, and the one can tomorrow become as great as the other is today, so also when one among men is raised to a great scholar, it often happens only with the aim that he who praises him wants to be held equal to all other scholars. For because after these eulogies all others are nothing in comparison with the great man, the small difference among the others is not to be reckoned. It is thus envy toward other human beings that one makes a single human being great. The praised person we no longer envy, but we also do not permit that others want to place themselves equal to him. – Citations of learned and famous men cannot be regarded as grounds of proof. For the real question is still always: how much ground does the assertion have in reason, and not who said it? Notwithstanding this, however, the inclination of citations of other authors carries with it such a certain weight to strengthen our approval toward a thing; it is consequently an external *criterium* of truth, i.e., the agreement of the general human understanding is a ground of the presumption that I shall have judged correctly. It is a kind of *experience* of the judgment on more than one understanding. Here, however, it often *arrivirt*, e.g. in metaphysics, that human beings all have one and the same deception. There is a science that has this about it, that it cannot do without *citationen* at all, namely *jus*. There one sees how uncertain all juridical judgments must be. But an original right could actually be conducted without citing. Only observe always the disadvantage of this or that law from experience. For the plurality of cases that can occasion the direction of the will at each time can never be expressed fully determinately in a law. One must therefore take other eyes to one's aid, because no one is so sharp-sighted as to investigate every case exactly. If you have paid attention to the various outcomes of lawsuits: then you will be in a position to be able to decide more. – Citations always have something agreeable, where, namely, whole passages from other *autoren* are adduced. It is indisputably the entertaining that occasions their pleasing us. Yet notwithstanding this they must not serve as grounds of proof, because it is only an external *criterium* of truth.",[]],"wiener-s05-029":["Prejudice from the authority of the multitude is a prejudice either of the healthy understanding, or of the learned, toward which the rabble chiefly has a propensity, since, namely, with it the judgment of the multitude holds. The rabble is not in a position to estimate the worth of a single person, therefore it holds to the multitude, but NB not to every multitude. – Already through proverbs a prejudice of the multitude is accepted, in that one presupposes: what all human beings say must surely be true. Yet this is only a historical judgment. But in matters of religion he trusts, because he does not trust himself to judge of it, the learned, who hold for us as models, as it were, in religion. This holds for all written religions. Here each believes his learned men, who study these books, and of such religions the missionaries report that it is just on this account difficult to make *proselyten* there. Here, then, the prejudice is grounded on a multitude of persons. But it is to be remarked here that what I accept from prejudice can also be true contingently. But that I accept it as a prejudice, that is false and logically reprehensible. Hence the cognition is not blameworthy as if it were false, but because I have accepted it from prejudice. With regard to cognitions there reign prejudices of the common man for erudition. But the scholar too has in turn a prejudice for the common estate. It is easy to find that the greatest crowd always has a favorable prejudice for the learned, and here it surely also has good ground. For these must track down cognition and make it their chief business, but such things come upon him only on occasion. – With the learned there is found a prejudice for the common understanding, and a mistrust in the learned understanding. It arises in the scholar eager for knowledge when he sees that all his efforts will not satisfy him. He believes he has not taken the right path, and therefore returns to the common understanding, because he believes he will find here the right key to a cognition obscure to him. This is then found when he has fairly well run through the circle of the sciences, and especially in such sciences as he could not make sensible without concepts, e.g. metaphysics, right. In natural science he has in no way a more favorable prejudice for the common than for the learned understanding, because he sees that here one can get further through *experimente*. But as concerns the judgments of the existence of a highest being, of the nature of our soul, when there the scholar has squandered all his art and yet does not satisfy himself, and finds so much that is customary in himself and other scholars: then he believes the key is hidden somewhere, and there he seeks it in the common understanding. But this belief is very deceptive. For how will these unpracticed eyes accomplish more than if they were cultivated? and how can such concepts of another world be brought to concepts? Nonetheless there are certain cognitions that can be presented *in concreto*. E.g. all moral questions can be given *in concreto*. Here the common use of the understanding judges more correctly than the speculative use of the understanding, because it has the things before it in experience. One must thus here, in the speculative use, often call the common understanding to one's aid on account of the particular propositions that finally spring from purely speculative ones. There are thus certain sciences where merely speculative reason is not secured without determination by healthy reason, and where a certain preliminary opinion of the speculative understanding must probably mislead the guarantee of the common understanding. E.g. the proposition of the jurists *damnum patitur dominus* is, examined according to the common understanding, highly inequitable. Hence not the scholar can in such cases take his refuge in the common understanding. But this is then no prejudice in the learned, but only a method of putting his speculation to the test. For there are so many cases in practical sciences that one cannot possibly survey them all in speculation, hence one must take cases *in concreto* for it.",[]],"wiener-s05-030":["Opposed to the prejudices of authority is logical *egoismus*, i.e., the prejudice according to which we hold the agreement of our understanding with another's reason, as a *criterium* of wisdom, to be impossible. There are sciences in which we actually,",[]],"wiener-s05-031":["without committing the fault of *egoismus*, must often rely on our own reason, without examining our judgment according to another's reason, and without needing this external *criterium*. E.g. In mathematics the *evidenz* is so great that no one can resist it, if only he follows the proofs laid before him. But otherwise this historical *criterium* of the assent of others cannot be entirely dispensed with. For although it is not alone a *criterium*: yet it is also a *criterium*. For in discursive cognitions of reason, where we set out everything through concepts, one can never hold the agreement of others to be dispensable, for the reason that the faults here are so easy which are not possible with an intuitive representation. The fault that I committed arose from an *illusion* that arose from the position in which I cognized the cognition, hence I cannot hold the judgment of others to be dispensable. For these can correct mine, e.g. if I see something in the distance, I say it is a horse, the other, it is a tree. I have perhaps only deeply impressed the thought of a horse beforehand, and through this *illusion* I believe I see a horse in the distance. Egoism is accordingly the fault where one believes that, when the question is of the *criterium* of truth, one does not need the judging of others.",[]],"wiener-s05-032":["Logical *egoismus* is either indifferentism or indifference toward others' judgments, in that I hold others' judgments to be unnecessary for the judging of mine, or self-conceit and *arroganz*, where one presumes oneself alone, before all others, to pass a correct judgment about a thing.",[]],"wiener-s05-033":["But providence has guided it so that we present our judgments to general human reason, and has placed the drive for it in us. Many surely believe here that this is nothing more than the incentives of vanity. That could be, but the intention of providence is nevertheless attained, and we also actually exert ourselves better. If it does not take place that we lay our thought before general reason, then we have cause to place suspicion in the validity of our judgments, because we will not follow the wise prescription of nature to try our truth on others' judgments. It is accordingly wrong in the state to forbid that human beings should write books and, e.g., judge about matters of religion. For thereby the only means is taken from them that nature has given them, namely to examine their judgment on another's reason. The freedom to think in silence is given by the people who tyrannize so despotically. But that is only the worst, that they cannot prevent anyone from it. Think I can always what I will. But as concerns logical egoism, one must yet also concede that, since human nature is directed to use this external *criterium*, I also have a right to present my thought publicly. || V The prejudice of antiquity is one of the most disadvantageous prejudices. It is very natural that an old person has it especially. For since he can no longer forge himself into a new form: so he *defendirt*, as much as he can, the old, and defends himself, as it were, in his possession. Young people are more for the new, because they still find themselves courageous enough to acquire new cognitions. – Yet there is also something general in this prejudice; there is a certain opinion that lies at its ground, namely human beings believe throughout that everything becomes worse with time, and that everything in the world grows old. || V This is now, to be sure, to a certain extent true. But our lifetime is much too short to observe all of it, how it gradually grows old. One thing is in nature that is entirely unchangeable, that is the species, even if every *individuum* grows old. In this way one imagines that this species is always gradually degraded, and that human beings would gradually become nothing other than skillful oxen, || V the old German honesty is ceasing entirely. But certainly human beings were formerly not greater, more virtuous, more understanding than now, and a man who has not spoiled his body through bad conduct will still beget just as healthy a son as Adam. || V The predispositions are the same as formerly. Therefore we are not in a position to find, in deviation from it, excellent and remarkable things. || V The prejudice of antiquity rises and falls from time to time. Now it seems again to be rising greatly. Formerly novelty rose more. || V If one contents oneself with the old in order merely to combine a historical acquaintance with the ancients with one's other cognitions: then it is useful and good. || V But if we believe we find wisdom in them: then we err very greatly, and we would ruin all sciences if we went back into the erudition of a foreign age and wanted to add to our practiced judgment what the ancients gathered as children. Our taste, however, they can surely form.",[]],"wiener-s05-034":["Prejudices often spring from opposite causes. For according as a head is attuned, it falls into a prejudice from an opposite cause. || V The prejudice in favor of the ancients springs from a disadvantageous judgment against the old, just as the prejudice in favor of the erudition of womenfolk, because one has a disadvantageous prejudice for the *capricitaet* of this sex. If one has a disadvantageous concept of the ancients, one can nevertheless make for oneself an advantageous representation of the ancients, because one confuses admiration with astonishment. For if one has a disadvantageous prejudice about a thing, and yet meets with something in it that exceeds our expectation: then one is astonished. Admiration, however, is the agreeable sensation when we find something very great. E.g. We are astonished when a child, of whom we have no such opinion at all, can already speak so cleverly. || V But in the end this astonishment can also degenerate into an admiration. Astonishment is the state of the soul in beholding the new and unexpected, admiration that of the unexpected good, e.g. when a woman begins to speak like a book: then one is astonished, and believes that she is cleverer than she actually is. – The ancients by far did not have the prejudices in the sciences that we have. One should thus not presume that they should have had the cognitions as we do. Hence we are astonished when we see that they thought as we do. This brings about the astonishment at an ancient, because in the age in which he lived he already said something so clever, and the astonishment degenerates in the end into an admiration, in that we find more merits in him than he actually has. || V E.g. if someone adduces a passage from Plato: then that would certainly be badly said if a modern wanted to say so: but that is yet much, we think, that the man could already then make out so much, and there in the end we admire his proposition, and no longer the man. And from this the prejudice arises. For it is wrong that instead of the man, whom we surely have cause to admire, we admire the proposition.",[]],"wiener-s05-035":["|| V The second cause of the prejudices in favor of antiquity is that all acquaintances that we have of antiquity are, with us ourselves, erudition. Although the sciences of the ancients, of which we now have reports, have very little or no erudition about them. Already the acquaintance with the language of the ancients is erudition, and at all times acquires respect. Accordingly, already the acquaintance with the merits of the ancients acquires respect, and here now again it happens through an *illusion* that the insights themselves acquire respect, although only the acquaintance of the man who knows something demands respect. || V The proposition produces a reflected *favorables* light, that the learned among the ancients also had insight into the proposition. For erudition deserves favor, even if the proposition contains nothing conducive. || V For it is always an agreeable thing to hear: Cicero said that, because it indicates the author's wide reading in the ancients; in that I only accept the presentation with favor, I accept the proposition with favor, even if it does not belong in erudition. || V Because, then, the proposition is presented in such a *vehiculo*, and the proposition thereby gets an authority because it has come to us from a distant place and time: so this brings about the prejudice of believing that it is better said than in more recent times. || V We have cause to make favorable judgments of antiquity for ourselves, without a prejudice being permitted to lurk underneath. But this ground reaches only to a moderate respect. But when we have the ground: then we often leave the limitations, and ascribe to them an immoderate respect. Time sifts all things; what is praised today is despised and rejected tomorrow. But what has not gone out of use in a long time, of that it is to be presumed that it has an inner worth. The writings of antiquity have been sifted in this way, because they have been preserved only by distinguishing themselves from other writings. Hence one has cause to judge favorably of these writings. But this is no proof that the writings of antiquity have an absolute great worth, but they have a great worth only in relation to the remaining writers, and if one makes this relative worth into an absolute one, then a prejudice arises from it. If we then take the bad and good books of the ancients, and want to make from them a judgment of the thoroughgoing acquaintance of the ancients: how astonishing will the judgment be? || V If, e.g., our descendants got into their hands the rubbish of books, of which it would be wished that it did not exist at all: then they would surely not be able to comprehend how, at a time when the right course of the sun was discovered, such nonsense too could be thought. But if all bad books stayed away; then they would all hold us for giants in judgment. So it is with the ancients too. || V If we accept the opinion of the ancients, and entrust ourselves to them in such a way that we thereby give up our own use of reason: then that is a prejudice,",[]],"wiener-s05-036":["that arises here. This prejudices have about them, that they entirely hinder the individual use of the understanding.",[]],"wiener-s05-037":["|| V Another cause of judging in favor of antiquity is gratitude. We are their pupils. For one generation is always the teacher of the following one. || V Gratitude seems to require that we show honor to the ancients, and then one believes that one must praise beyond measure those to whom one owes thanks. We indeed also think one day to become the ancients, and just as one despises him who mocks an old man, since he after all himself wants to become old, so we too would be to be despised if we wanted to mock the ancients, and in order that the wrong shall be made good, the high praising of the ancients is always driven beyond everything. For one holds it to be virtuous to exaggerate even one's gratitude. For then one has after all done something which redounds to their honor. || V Judgments in favor of antiquity also arise out of envy toward contemporaries. If a human being has always studied the ancients, he has neglected the new, and therefore he only ever admires the ancients, because he understands only that. || V Now because he cannot surpass the more recent ones: he does it thus: he always extols the ancients. || V One no longer envies the deceased, but those still living are an object of envy. If, therefore, someone has diligently studied the ancients: then he praises them highly, so as not to allow the more recent ones a superiority over himself. – || V No knowledge puffs up more than philological knowledge, and than that which one calls *polyhistorie* [polyhistory], which takes place when one has a kind of universality in regard to the knowledge of the ancients, although this is not sufficient, but historical. The *Litterati* [men of letters] at once find all of it in the ancients, and do not at all permit that the more recent ones should have found it. When the magnetic needle had been invented: at once a word was found in Terence which already indicated it. The world system of Copernicus Philolaus was supposed to have had already. Philosophy strikes down all pride, and indeed brings about a misology against it, what science is. For it uncovers so greatly the abyss of our ignorance that we beware of going further on the path. But all philology cannot extend our understanding by one degree, but it only brings it about that we do not lose the combination and the knowledge of the interconnection of our cognitions with the ancients.",[]],"wiener-s05-038":["|| V The prejudice of novelty. The new itself is never a prejudice. The ancients have laid something at the ground. We hope, however, that the new would be better. That engenders much vanity, just as the prejudice of antiquity engenders much pedantry.",[]],"wiener-s05-039":["The prejudice from an adopted doctrinal edifice does not properly belong to the universal prejudices. A doctrinal edifice is unity in a cognition, which can be derived from one *principio* [principle]. For the *principium* we finally acquire a certain *praedilection* [predilection], in that we judge that everything that occurs in the system deserves great respect, and this brings it about that we often shut an eye when errors occur in the system. This is properly only a private prejudice of self-love.",[]],"wiener-s05-040":["The prejudice of mistrust in antiquity. All the negative prejudices are to be cognized from the preceding ones, for it is easy to cognize that out of too great a love toward myself a mistrust in antiquity arises, etc.",[]],"wiener-s05-041":["Prejudices are properly not at all for logic. For it has to do with the objective grounds of the understanding, and not with the subjective causes of the same. The subjective causes are explanations of the actual appearances, how it after all comes about that human beings form the one thing thus, the other thus according to their understanding. It belongs therefore properly to *anthropologie* [anthropology]. Logic considers only the objective universal grounds of reason, without seeing whether the understanding is corrupted or not. Prejudice is a mere given fact. All *explication* of the given fact belongs to *psychologie* [psychology]. One can, however, still take it along here, because it can be possible in the use of logical instruction, and one must pay attention to the causes that can lead us away from the right use of the same.",[]],"wiener-s06-001":["The error is committed by all, and so too by our author, that instead of its being said, as it should be: the cognition is probable, the talk is of the *object*, insofar as I hold the *object* to be probable, although my cognition can be true and certain. My cognition is probable if there are more of the cases that determine the thing than that determine the opposite. If one has that before one's eyes: then one can well say, a cognition is probable, but the logic of the probable is no pure logic. It would be a science that would have for its object the *objecte* for which the causes are possible in themselves, where we then see how many causes",[]],"wiener-s06-002":["are there for the effect, and how many for the opposite. Thus it is to be presumed through a ground that that for which there are more grounds is probable, although it is not sufficient. All probability is a fraction, whose denominator is the number of all possible cases, and whose numerator contains the number of hits. E.g., if someone is to throw 8 with 2 dice: then he has 6 hits, and 36 possible cases. The fraction is therefore 6/36, of which the opponent has 30 and he 6 cases. Probability of the *object* rests on the relation which grounds an occurrence has to complete certainty. One therefore cannot rightly have insight into probability if one does not know what belongs to complete certainty. For since one must have a great measure for probability, and the measure for every insufficient ground is the sufficient ground: the measure for probability is certainty. – Mathematics can here give a certain measure, through which it compares the magnitudes, because magnitudes contain nothing but that which is homogeneous. But in philosophy that does not work, because here the grounds of the possible hits are all heterogeneous. If I take all possible cases, and say: in these the hits must also be contained: then the relation of the grounds of taking to be true is heterogeneous. E.g., There are many testimonies for an accused. 1.) the deed had happened when he was not at home. He was also actually not at home. This is *res facti* [a matter of fact]. 2.) another had seen him at the time. Here the credibility of the other is the ground. The grounds in philosophy are nevertheless always distinguished according to *qualitaet* [quality], and cannot be *numerirt* [counted], but *ponderirt* [weighed]. The magnitude of probability can often not be so determined in philosophy, because the grounds are not homogeneous. But still in the cognition here it is not the cognition that is regarded as probable, but the *Object*. The cognition in itself can be true.",[]],"wiener-s06-003":["As concerns the probability of cognition: these are grounds of taking to be true before all investigation. If I find in the cognition no contradiction, but interconnection with the other things: then I do not say: the *object* is true. The probability of cognition does not signify much, but what matters is whether the *Object* is true? – Human beings often think, when they predict something probable, that they have obtained a real triumph, even though they had no grounds to take it to be true, it came about by chance, although the judgment was ungrounded, the cognition was on that account not at all probable, even though it was the cognition of a probable *objecte*. We must not always follow probable cognition. It is only the beginning of the beginning of examination, and can lead us to investigate further about the truth. A cognition is doubtful if there are grounds for and against it. No doubt can be made against it, because it just as well asserts the opposite. This indifferent in the logical is just that which the indifferent is in the practical, where therefore the understanding is wholly indifferent in regard to the cognition. The cognition is doubted if one has already declared oneself for it, but on the other side counter-grounds are brought against it. Doubt signifies a mere hindrance of taking to be true. It is the state of the mind when there is something that hinders us in assent. One can consider it objectively and subjectively. If the doubt is objectively distinct: then it is objection. If it is objectively indistinct and obscure: then it is scruple. In common usage of speech *dubitatio*, subjective doubt, and *dubium*, objective doubt, are confused with one another. One says: I have many doubts against it, if these are not objective grounds: then one could well doubt for oneself. But one cannot present that to the other as grounds in order to move him that he should also doubt. The grounds of subjective taking to be true are to be posited in upbringing, in that one cannot yet rightly accustom one's head to accept another opinion. – One has a scruple against a cognition if one cannot remove the hindrance of taking to be true from oneself. This scruple can also be subjective. It is that which makes us anxious in the case of cognitions, so that we do not want to assent to the cognition with our whole soul, because something in us opposes complete assent. – Scruples are not to be despised. For in the beginning all our cognitions are obscure, and so in the beginning admittedly much is presented to us scrupulously, because they are grounds that conflict with the *objecte*. If a scruple becomes clear: then it becomes an objection, and before it is made clear, the scruple therefore must not be rejected. It indicates a great talent if one can make his scruples clear to another. Objections presuppose that cognitions are already taken as true. Doubts do not presuppose that, but there we can always proceed problematically. – Objections do not yet refute, but they are only supposed to weaken the cognition. They therefore have, with every taking to be true, the utility that the certainty of the cognition comes to greater perfection. Hence a thing can no longer be attacked, because the opponent has set up all grounds of the opposite. Law and religion consequently certainly require objections, because through them they come to greater certainty. Doubts can be answered. Objections are refuted. Every doubt must not only be answered, for then it is merely turned away, but it must also be resolved, in that I must show how it could have arisen. If that does not happen: then the hindrance of taking to be true is not yet completely removed, and the ground for new doubts remains there. From this it flows that refutation of objections must happen amicably, so that instead of merely driving the other back, one must also inquire through what an otherwise good understanding could have been brought to such objections, i.e., through what illusion the objections have arisen. If we then find confusion: then we must explain this to him, and so *nodum resolvere, non secare* [untie the knot, not cut it]. *Probabilitas* is probability, *verisimilitudo*, plausibility. A cognition as cognition can be called plausibility, but the thing which I take to be true, or probable, is not on that account *verisimile*, but probable, or improbable. The difference rests on this. In probability I compare the grounds of taking to be true to certainty. If the taking to be true is greater than half of the sufficient ground: then the cognition is probable. If it is less than half: then the cognition is improbable. For if I have more than half of the ground: then I have more than grounds for the opposite are possible at all, and these necessarily also more than all grounds that are possible on the other side. In mathematics one can determine the sufficient ground of taking to be true in probability with numbers, because the things are homogeneous in the grounds of possibility, and if things are only of the same kind: then they need only be counted together, whether my grounds make up more than half. But the grounds of truth in philosophical cognitions I can determine only discursively. For through the fact that here the grounds are not homogeneous, it happens that I cannot say: here is half of the sufficient ground. For one cannot indicate how a ground relates to the sufficient ground, because between heterogeneous things no equal relation can be given. Laws and examples from nature, without testimonies of others, are grounds of that kind. Where is half to be found here? Hence a logic of probability is an impossible undertaking. Indeed, if one understands by it the part of logic where the concepts of the probable and doubtful etc. are made understandable: then that can fittingly happen. But if degrees are to be given in order to assess whether it is probable or not? then it is impossible on this account, because in the case of certainty one does not attain insight into the grounds for certainty sooner than when one has actually arrived at certainty, and since in philosophy the grounds of taking to be true are heterogeneous: we can only *ponderiren* [weigh] them, i.e., attempt to measure the grounds against our assent. We cannot, however, measure them, but the art of measuring probability falls to mathematics. – One always has in the usual logics certain rules according to which the understanding is to be used in probability. But the rules are even more obscure to understand than if one wanted to make use of them. They also help us nothing in use, but merely reflection belongs to it. – Understanding is the faculty of rules. (Power of) judgment is the faculty of distinguishing whether a rule is to be used in this place, therefore the faculty of subsuming under a rule. To this faculty I cannot give mere rules that are set over it. In the investigation of probability, therefore, in philosophy, whither the power of judgment after all also extends, there are no precepts of probability. A mathematics of probability there indeed is, and this is also practiced. E.g., in schools in *calculiren* [calculating] instruction is given about the best way to get out the probability, in relation to the errors of the other methods. E.g., I calculate the height of a star 20 times. The one time there comes out 10 degrees, 13 minutes the other time 13 degrees, 11 minutes etc. All these *facit* [results] I calculate together, and divide by it 20 so I have the probable height. Thus mathematics determines certain rules according to which the *object* can be cognized as probable. In philosophy, however, that is impossible. Examples you can well give, in order to exercise your power of judgment in them. But rules you will never be able to give, how far something is probable or not. *Probabilitaet* [probability] pertains to things. Plausibility pertains to whether in the cognition there are more grounds for than against the things. For if I compare the grounds of taking to be true with the sufficient ground and have no grounds of the opposite: then plausibility comes out. E.g., Someone adduces, the thinking being, the soul, will not cease even in death because in life it has not depended on the body, and I objected perhaps the contingency of generation: then I ask: is it probable here? The objection means nothing, consequently the cognition is plausible. If, with a ground for taking something to be true, there is no ground of the opposite there: then the cognition is plausible. If I know a ground, which however says just that which stands in the opposite: then it is doubtful. But if I compare the thing with the sufficient ground of truth: then it is probable. For all probability consists in the approximation of the taking to be true to certainty. Objective doubts are the ground which makes a cognition false. Subjective doubt is the state of the mind where we have just as many grounds for as against the things. The human being is in doubt subjectively, has doubts objectively. Principles of doubt are 1.) the principle of asserting. The of this maxim: all cognition is to be treated with the aim of asserting something through it, is called the dogmatic principle. Dogmatic is a cognition through which all meant things are asserted. The maxim of treating a cognition with the aim that one has insight into the complete uncertainty and impossibility of arriving at certainty is 2.) the skeptical maxim, the way of philosophizing where one renounces all asserting cognitions, and regards all grounds of one's judgment so that one opposes to them equally strong counter-grounds. We treat something dogmatically if we decide the thing with complete certainty, skeptically, if we seek to show the complete uncertainty. Both, taken universally, are faulty. For many cognitions are impossible, while dogmatism nevertheless seeks to make them certain. E.g., Of the change of the weather. From the prospect into the universal constitution of things one cannot dogmatically make out whether there is a simple being. Here we must often take another path in order to make something out. To proceed skeptically annihilates all our effort, and it is an antilogical principle, an attempt to drive off ignorance. For if I bring cognition to the point that it finally annihilates itself: then it is as much as if we regarded all cognitions of human beings as nothing. Notwithstanding that skepticism is so harmful: this still does not prevent that the philosophical method of treating something that is uncertain, in that I endeavor to drive the uncertainty to the highest degree, in the hope of finally discovering through this procedure the traces of truth, is good. It is the *suspension* of judgment in order to arrive at certainty. This *methode* therefore, where we not only doubt everything, but also seek out the cause of the conflict of the understanding with itself, in order thereby to explain the truth, is the critical *methode*. We have accordingly a dogmatic, skeptical and critical *methode*. –",[]],"wiener-s06-004":["To proceed dogmatically with all cognitions, i.e., to hope for decided certainty without taking into consideration the grounds of the opposite, produces an inadequate illusion. For if I believe that nothing more can be sought out against the truth: then I at once stop in inquiring. But then the thing also has little foundation. One can well attempt something dogmatically, but not proceed dogmatically. One must in the dogmatic *methode* also proceed skeptically, i.e., when I try whether in the case of the thing something cannot also be adduced that I set on the side of an opponent. Skepticism is therefore where on both sides there is dogmatic assertion. This one can do within oneself, but which of the two can now decide more? Now comes the critical *methode*, i.e., I investigate the sources of the dogmatic and skeptical *methode*, and then I begin to see on which grounds an assertion, and on which its opposite, rests. The critical is therefore the intermediate method through which a cognition can arrive at certainty. It forestalls the dogmatic illusion, because it opposes skepticism to dogmatism, and in that it has thereby weighed the grounds of both, it alone can *decidiren* [decide] how many grounds I have for taking to be true. Skepticism is a deposing of all teaching of reason, and contradicts itself. It gives out everything as illusion, and yet it distinguishes illusion from truth, because it warns us to accept nothing as truth, so it must after all have a mark in which it can recognize truth, and since it gives the ground why one is to doubt all that, so the opposite must surely also be possible. It can therefore be refuted through itself. The doubters of ancient times were very remarkable. Pyrrho is the first doubter. He lived after the times of Socrates, and doubted all things, which he expressed thus: *non liquet* [it is not clear]. This is the Pyrrhonian",[]],"wiener-s06-005":["doubt. But the academic doubt is also remarkable. For after Plato's death Speusippus continued his school, after him Arcesilaus, the third school Carneades. Plato had the *methode* of Socrates in his *dialogen* [dialogues], but he was no skeptic at all, but he did that only in order through long *suspendiren* [suspending] finally to *eruiren* [elicit] the truth. Speusippus remained true to him, and taught as dogmatically as Plato. But Arcesilaus set up a skeptical school. That is academic skepticism. He said: everything is so uncertain that it is even uncertain whether that is uncertain, that one called a real [ Textlücke: AA: Purgativ ] of reason, that he, in that he sought to purify reason, purged away reason itself along with it. The third school of Carneades consisted of dreadful sophists and dialecticians, what they asserted today, they denied tomorrow. Hypothesis, the author defines, is an opinion, and indeed of the philosophers, when they explain the world from the appearances. But opinion he does not yet explain adequately.",[]],"wiener-s06-006":["The difference between opinion and hypothesis is this. Opinion is an incomplete taking to be true from insufficient grounds, from which I derive nothing. Hypothesis, however, signifies the judgment of truth from grounds that are adequate. If something is assumed as ground, from which I can have insight into the sufficient ground of given consequences: then it is *hypothesis*. It is accordingly the taking to be true of a presupposition, not of a cognition insofar as it is itself derived from grounds, but because it is mediately certain. Immediately certain cognitions are experience and *axiomen* [axioms]. Axiom is when I accept a cognition as certain because it can be cognized *a priori* from the nature of the thing. Experiential cognitions are led from sense.",[]],"wiener-s06-007":["Mediately a cognition is concluded from another, or it is taken to be true from a ground, and that is hypothesis. If I take a cognition to be true because it is a capable ground for other grounds: then it is a hypothesis. I assume something, and then I see what other cognitions can be derived from it. I therefore assume it not for the sake of the ground from which it is derived, but I take it to be true for the sake of the grounds that can be derived from it. It is therefore something reversed in comparison with other cognitions. The taking to be true is grounded on this. I assume the cognition arbitrarily as a ground, but through the assumption of the same I can give grounds of other cognitions which are certainly true, and insofar as it is therefore accordingly connected with the truth, it is called hypothesis. All taking to be true of a hypothesis is grounded on explaining, through it as a ground, other cognitions as consequences. I therefore properly infer from the truth of the consequences to the ground, and therefore properly conclude the truth of the cognition from the consequences. E.g., I say: there is a central fire present in the earth, because the fire-spewing mountains, earthquakes and other *phaenomene* prove that. To infer from the consequences to the ground is also a *criterium* of truth. *pag.* 88 namely if true consequences are derived from it: then I infer to the truth of the cognition. But although it is also a *criterium* of truth: still it is far from sufficient, but the cognition is true only when all consequences from the cognition are true. But since we cannot possibly derive all consequences from the cognition: this path *a posteriori* actually also gives an uncertain inference that the ground is the true ground. From a false ground some true consequences can be drawn, because after all there is in part truth in it. A hypothesis therefore always remains hypothesis, i.e., does not become complete certainty. For I after all cognize only the adequacy of the cognition to some consequences, and only the adequacy of the cognition to all possible consequences would make apodictic certainty. The derivation of many consequences does not yet at all concern the certainty of a cognition. For other grounds can draw just the cognition after them. It therefore makes indeed a ground of probability, but not of certainty. Some sciences suffer no hypotheses at all. E.g., mathematics. In natural science, however, they are indispensable. But if after all as many consequences as we can ever find can all be derived from an assumed ground: then the hypothesis grows ever in probability, and indeed so much that, although it can never be apodictically certain, still an *analogon* of certainty comes from it, i.e., one gives oneself up to it as to complete certainty. E.g., in the hypothesis of the Copernican system one has not yet hit upon a single observation that could not be derived from it. Here there is therefore an *analogon* of certainty. Apodictically certain, however, it is not. But it is after all possible that the creator has made another arrangement of the planets, as the hypothesis of Tycho de Brahe shows. Our conviction, however, is grounded on this, that as many appearances as are found, these can all be derived from the system of Copernicus. We accordingly *approximiren* [approximate] to apodictic certainty, and it is *comparative* [comparatively] combined with it.",[]],"wiener-s06-008":["A hypothesis is not a cognition completely taken to be true, but some things in the cognition must be completely certain, and indeed",[]],"wiener-s06-009":["1.) the possibility of a presupposition must be completely certain, if an assumed ground is possible: then the thing still remains uncertain. For another ground can also take place to which the cognition fits. But the possibility itself must be completely certain in the hypothesis. E.g., I might want to assume the *commercium pnevmaticum* [spiritual community], i.e., the force through which the human being can perhaps act on other minds. This hypothesis cannot be assumed at all, but it is a pure fiction. For the possibility of this presupposition is not even certain. If we have no acquaintance at all with the possibility of certain forces: then we must not lay them at the ground as hypotheses. The use of it may only approximate to certainty, but the possibility must be certain.",[]],"wiener-s06-010":["2.) The *Consequenz* [consequence] must be completely certain, i.e., if I hold something to be certain: then the consequences that I infer from it must be completely certain. Otherwise it is held to be a pure *chimaere* [chimera]. – E.g., if in a theft one wants to know the perpetrators: then one makes a hypothesis, and there it must",[]],"wiener-s06-011":["1.) be possible that these people could do it. If that is so: then",[]],"wiener-s06-012":["2.) also the execution of those who stole must be completely comprehended, i.e., it must follow securely that they could have done it.",[]],"wiener-s06-013":["3.) For hypothesis unity is required. No more than one presupposition is needed in order to explain a plurality of consequences. As soon as we make many presuppositions, every hypothesis loses in its probability. In the case of unity I infer: because many consequences fit one ground, the more probable it is that the ground is true, and the right one. But if some, but not many, consequences can be derived from the hypothesis, and ever new hypotheses must be made in order to support it: then there is little probability there. – *Hypothesis subsidiaria*, an auxiliary hypothesis, is when something is assumed in order to come to the aid of a hypothesis which without a new hypothesis would not be able to maintain itself. Thereby a hypothesis loses all its credibility. –",[]],"wiener-s06-014":["Of this kind was the explanation of Tycho de Brahe, in order to explain the standing still of the planets. He made *Cyclos* [cycles], and in order to explain these deviating *Cyclos*, he assumed *Cyclos* in the *Cyclos*, and so ever further, and there one sees that it goes on without end. In philosophy everything said of *hypothese* is explained distinctly, and often very true and correct consequences are derived from *hypothesen*. Every human being who makes *experimente* first makes *hypothesen*, in that he believes that this or that trial will have such consequences. He may not, in an unsuccessful investigation, at once let his courage sink, just as the alchemist still keeps working on the *hypothese* of making gold.",[]],"wiener-s06-015":["In philosophy, especially in natural science, they cannot be dispensed with, inasmuch as we also take that which can obtain as the right cause of things to be such. We assume, according to the analogy of things, one and all other causes. Thus it is e.g. a hypothesis that rock crystals form themselves prismatically. There are, however, sciences where no hypotheses at all obtain, and indeed in metaphysics. E.g. that the soul of the human being can also act in the world without the body, whereby at the same time it can be conceded that it can appear after death, and act on the souls of other human beings. To make such hypotheses is not permitted, because I cannot prove the possibility of such efficacy. The possibility must always be given through experience. At least similarity with this power must be shown to us, so that I have some cause to assume the proposition. Persuasion. One could say that the taking-to-be-true from grounds of which one does not oneself know whether they are subjective or objective is a persuasion. We are taken with something, and yet call it a persuasion, because we cannot give account of our taking-to-be-true, and cannot say whether the cause of the taking-to-be-true lies in our sense, and concerns our inclination, or whether the understanding has it from the knowledge of the object. If that does not obtain: then we are conscious that it is a pure persuasion. Persuasions often precede conviction. But that seldom occurs with him who has had insight into much, and has learned much, in order to attain certainty, because he can distinguish them well from convictions. Further, whoever has been much deceived and made wise by it is not so easily persuaded. Meanwhile there are many people who feed themselves very much with hope. But that is not good. For the mind must be prepared for everything. It indicates a shallow way of thinking when a human being lets himself be persuaded so easily, or persuades himself of something. This is a great cause of the fact that human beings in religion find no difficulty in easily assuming this and that proposition. They easily persuade themselves, although they can give no ground for it. Many a human being cannot do that, but rather he must have something that satisfies him, and he does not straightaway give grounds of which he does not know whether they are subjective or objective. We see, therefore, well that we must not blame any human being because he will not immediately give assent to the matter. For all human beings are very different in this. The one can easily persuade himself of it, the other also wishes a hundred times that the matter might be so, and yet his understanding will not immediately applaud, because, namely, he demands objective grounds. – There is something on which assent rests which is a mixture of inclination and understanding. Now many a human being does not seek to separate this properly, so that he would investigate which of the two produces his assent. – Many human beings call conviction what is actually persuasion. Persuasions, however, are not always false. Only this human being has the matter from persuasion, not from conviction. The human being can cognize some grounds from the object through the understanding. But many subjective grounds are nevertheless added. E.g. from the example of famous men, who after all, as one says, were no fools, from the great antiquity of the matters, and more besides. All this together therefore mixes itself together, and produces the effect that is called persuasion. Human beings can through persuasion assume certain, but also unimportant, matters. It then does not lie at all in the heart, if someone will not at all assume certain matters, but in the head. E.g. parents easily persuade themselves that their children are innocent, and that they are seduced by the neighbors' children; whence does this propensity arise? But they imagine this, even though a stranger sees that the boy is himself a good-for-nothing. The propensity of inclination is so important that they make do with subjective and actually halved grounds. Another demands fit grounds, and does not let himself be so easily dismissed. This will make us fair when others will not immediately assume what seems so clear to us; because we cannot after all pass off our grounds as the first *principia* of habit.",[]],"wiener-s06-016":["Science. A sum total of cognition. Is divided into aggregate and system. Aggregate is a common cognition. System is a science. System rests on the unity of the idea, namely how the manifold of a cognition is ordered next to one another. It therefore presupposes an idea of the whole in which the science is contained. With the aggregate one also thinks to arrive at the whole, inasmuch as one always adds parts. With the aggregate, therefore, the parts precede the whole; with the system the whole precedes the parts. This distinction is very important. All metaphysics is nothing but aggregate and rhapsody, because we have never yet had the idea of the whole, how far the human being goes beyond reason, and on what sort of means he builds that which he says. Hence metaphysics is a fixed rhapsody. The metaphysician rummages up everything of which he believes that it stands in combination with the whole. But he has as yet no concept of the whole, and the whole is unknown to him. –",[]],"wiener-s06-017":["Sciences are historical and sciences of reason. It is not good that the author understands by sciences merely a cognition of reason. For a system can also be given of historical matters, namely inasmuch as I send ahead an idea according to which the manifold in history is to be ordered. Unfortunately, however, the *historici* are commonly rhapsodists. The idea could be this. Human actions come from human nature, in order to fulfill its vocation completely; if I make this my idea, how human nature has developed in the various ages, and gradually approaches its whole vocation, i.e., the completion of all its ends, which are set before humanity on earth, then I bring a system into my head according to which I can order history. Certainty is either",[]],"wiener-s06-018":["1.) empirical certainty. This rests either on one's own or on others' experiences, when for the sake of their testimony I hold the matter to be certain. It is also called historical certainty. It is, however, just as much empirical as my own experience. For often I must not trust my observations as much as many another's, of whom I know that he is an attentive man, and that I probably overlook much in the matter.",[]],"wiener-s06-019":["Certainty is so much the stronger. For I do not attend to many things as much as another does.",[]],"wiener-s06-020":["2.) Certainty of reason is at all times apodictic certainty, i.e., that something cannot be so, but rather must necessarily be so. Empirical certainty is never apodictic. Mathematical certainty is apodictically intuitive, philosophical apodictically discursive.",[]],"wiener-s06-021":["Arbitrary truth. It seems to be strange that the author speaks of arbitrary truth. For just because I say something, it is surely not yet true, but rather the truth must surely lie in the object. We therefore say better arbitrary propositions. They are such propositions where I will that something is to be so, which therefore actually rest on my will. They are commands of my reason, where the cognition depends on the arbitrariness of my cognition. I command, e.g., the circle is to be divided into 360, one is to count with 9 digits and the 0. These are arbitrary propositions. For one can also count with 4 digits, as Leibniz did. – Proof is divided into acroamatic proof, which is discursive, and into mathematical proof, which is intuitive. The first is conducted from concepts, the other from the construction of concepts. Of empirical proofs we do not speak here at all. One must therefore not straightaway triumph with the certainty of one's propositions, for no proposition in philosophy can have mathematical certainty. For I cannot, e.g., present to anyone a whole world in intuition. In every proof there occurs",[]],"wiener-s06-022":["1.) the proposition that is to be proved.",[]],"wiener-s06-023":["2.) the ground of proof, and",[]],"wiener-s06-024":["3.) the sequence of the proof, namely how the cognition follows from the ground of proof. The ground of proof is called argument. Sometimes the inference is also called argument. In philosophy one must always investigate the ground of proof, whether it is possible with the cognition.",[]],"wiener-s06-025":["Such investigations are very important, whether a ground of proof can be taken here or there, whether a cognition relates to experience, or whether we become partakers of it through reason. On this, whether we will arrive at sequels through the ground of proof, it depends in metaphysics, which examines the sequels themselves. Logical cognitions are indemonstrable, says the author, which are certain even without consent. Otherwise indemonstrable also signifies a matter that requires a proof but has none. Indemonstrably false is a cognition whose falsity cannot be proved. An indemonstrable proposition is an immediately certain proposition. What is certain without any ground of proof is immediately certain. However much in a cognition may be mediately certain: yet one thing is always immediately certain. Therefore we will have indemonstrable propositions in all sciences, which not only do not need proof, as the author would have it, but rather in philosophy every proposition is indemonstrable as soon as it is immediately certain. Proof is a direct, i.e., a positive proof, or it is apagogic, i.e., negative. The direct shows that the proposition is true, the apagogic that the opposite is not true. It merely refutes the opposite. In mathematics it is very evident, but in philosophy one must use it very sparingly. For the apagogic never does as much as the direct proof. This also goes back to the sources from which a proposition comes. The apagogic, however, shows me only an error, and that I would run into an absurdity if I did not assume the proposition. I have indeed proved the proposition thereby, but not comprehended it. They are, however, also not to be taken in philosophy because I can often [ Textlücke ]refute my opponent, inasmuch as we on that account both [ Textlücke: AA: falsch ] judge. We are both wrong, and when I have refuted the other, he can just as well refute me *apagogice* without any contradiction, and neither can prove his proposition thereby.",[]],"wiener-s06-026":["Historical belief. Our author relates belief merely to testimony. We, however, distinguish believing something and believing someone. We can believe something without anyone having told it to us. We can believe someone when we have assumed something on someone's testimony. Proofs from experience can indeed establish that the matter is, but not the absolute necessity of the matter. For experience gives only something contingent. Proofs *a priori*, however, give apodictic certainty, whether they come from concepts or from the construction of concepts. If I cognize an object not from mere concepts, but *a priori* in the intuition that corresponds to the concept: then I construct the concept, and that is a demonstration. This proof has evidence with it, i.e., the proof is intuitive. This is mathematical proof. Philosophical proof is never intuitive, but at all times discursive, i.e., is always conducted with words. Since philosophical proof is not cognized in intuition: it also cannot be called demonstration. Both proofs are apodictic, but with the difference, the one in intuition, the other in pure concepts. Hence we must never use the word demonstration of philosophical proofs.",[]],"wiener-s06-027":["It has become the custom that one speaks of demonstration in the case of apodictic proofs that have nothing intuitive at all about them. Demonstration comes from *monstrare*, to show, to lay before the eyes. Hence it can properly actually be used only of proofs where the object is presented in intuition, and where the truth is cognized not merely discursively but also intuitively. We will therefore here set limitations to philosophy, and never call its proofs demonstrations, however apodictic they may always be. In mathematics one makes no progress at all if one wants to prove everything from nothing but concepts, but rather they must necessarily be constructed. – The Greeks are the first who invented demonstrations. No people knew what demonstrating was before it had arisen among the Greeks. It is said the Greeks learned their wisdom from the Egyptians. But the Egyptians are children compared with the Greeks. They had indeed manifold pieces of knowledge, but not sciences. The Greeks first enlightened the human understanding. Several stories are all cited in the authors that led to the demonstration of this or that proposition. Had they only learned them from the Egyptians, then they would not have needed to demonstrate them any more. The Egyptians had merely empirical surveyors, who according to certain rules could measure well without proofs. No people, except those who have learned it from the Greeks, knows what demonstrations are. All those who have not learned it from them take it for circumlocutions, and yet demonstrations are the sure step that extensive insight into mathematics has taken. Without this all its attempts would be deceptive, like all philosophical attempts, in which human reason often deceives itself. With belief a distinction is to be made between believing something and believing another. Believing something relates merely to the cognition, and to the ground of the taking-to-be-true. A cognition can be from reason, and the ground of the taking-to-be-true, although it is logically insufficient, is yet practically completely sufficient, i.e., although logically, before the pure understanding, the ground of the taking-to-be-true of this cognition is not completely correct: the cognition can yet practically be taken as true. This is rational belief, which is completely distinct from knowledge, and signifies logically insufficient taking-to-be-true, which, however, is practically sufficient, and both from grounds E.g. in mathematics it is said at the beginning that the diameter is related to the periphery as 100 to 314. It is only proved afterwards in trigonometry. Here, therefore, the taking-to-be-true is grounded merely on the fact that famous men have discovered it; but because one already knows that in mathematics an error cannot so easily hide itself: he has great cause to believe firmly that it is true, but he cannot after all say, I know it. – To believe something on others' testimonies, where the cognition rests entirely on reason, is a complete misuse of reason, for the testimonies of others must rest only on experience. But because in mathematics errors that hide themselves for long cannot so easily remain: I can surely rely on the fact that the diameter is related to the periphery as 100:314, although I only believe it, and cannot say: I know it. We can also assume certain propositions of reason on belief. Great mathematicians assume propositions in mathematics without perhaps having gone through the demonstration. In philosophy, however, one cannot do that, because there errors so easily hide themselves. Historical belief, where we take something to be true because we believe another, can become certainty. – Belief is therefore a rational belief and a historical belief. Rational belief is that which from the ground of reason is sufficient not logically, to be sure, but practically. Historical belief is where I take something to be true merely on the testimony of another, also perhaps without grounds. A rational belief is one that is only assumed, although we as yet have no certainty at all of the matter, but it has practical grounds, e.g., that the soul is immortal can impel me to a better arrangement of my life. This practical ground can never rise up to a ground that is called knowing. For knowing signifies that the taking-to-be-true is also logically sufficient. There can, however, be so strong a taking-to-be-true that is just as unshakable as the greatest certainty. But whoever wants to speculate must have cognitions that are sufficient also according to logical grounds.",[]],"wiener-s06-028":["Historical belief can be an actual knowledge. Accordingly, belief from a testimony must not be distinguished from knowledge, although rational belief must at all times be distinguished from knowledge. That historical belief can also be a knowledge we see from this, if I ask someone: what is the capital in Spain called? and he would say, I believe Madrid: then I would say, you must not believe that, but know it. If one might say, one surely cannot know it if one has not oneself been there: then the answer is: if I myself am there: then I surely cannot experience it otherwise than that the inhabitants there tell it to me, and I thus assume it after all on the testimony of others. Also it does not at all prevent there being certainty in it, that it is a testimony. For we can assume something just as well on the testimony of others as on our own experience. For in our experience there is just as much that is deceptive as in the testimonies of others. Our thinking in the taking-to-be-true of an experience is subject to many dangers. To be sure, the testimony of others that we assume is subject to just as many dangers as our own experience is subject to errors. But we can after all have certainty through the testimony of others just as well as through our own experience. Believing is therefore one and the same with knowing. If, therefore, we contradistinguish believing from knowing: then it is only a practically sufficient taking-to-be-true. What we cognize through reason we cannot always know. What we cognize through historical belief we can know. – About what can I question a witness? I can take something to be true then, if the other has infallible reason, or his experience bids him say it. I can take something to be true by means of a witness of infallible reason in truths of reason, or for the sake of that which he has from his own experiences. Since in experience not so many errors are to be met with as in speculations: it is easier to believe what experience states than what the other has cognized from reason, because reason is not so infallible. Mathematics has an infallible reason. Hence as soon as a proposition is asserted by it, it is infallible. – But since the concept of the infallibility of reason belongs only to the highest perfection: we can assume no testimony as infallibly true but the divine testimony itself. One objects here: can a human being then not testify to divine oracles? *Resp.* he can testify to nothing other than through an experience of the inspiration that he has had, and here it is still always possible that the human being can err. Here, therefore, one cannot believe *subjective*, but rather all grounds must be precisely examined. – And so on the testimony of another I believe much that he gives out as his own experience, also on the testimony of others. E.g. if someone relates what he has read: then he testifies to his own experience from a book. Here, therefore, I rely 1.) on the statement of another and 2.) on the experience itself that the other has had.",[]],"wiener-s06-029":["One science, namely jurisprudence, is of the kind that, although it rests entirely on reason, one nevertheless makes much use of testimonies in it. In philosophy it does not depend on which testimonies but on which grounds one adduces, and the matter indeed deserves closer investigation, that, although *Jus* is of the kind that it rests on reason, one nevertheless takes along the judgment of others with it. The cause is perhaps because *Jus* rests on laws, and these contain a great manifoldness of cases, for which a particular determination is necessary, where errors are possible, that one takes something to be universal that admits of exception. Now if one wants to have a law completely valid and usable: then one believes others will not so easily have overlooked something. For this reason jurists are also called *Juris periti*, although it is a science of reason. But it is a lamentable constitution when it goes thus. For however practiced human beings may be in attending to all cases: it is yet possible that they have still overlooked some. – All citations, if they are to furnish grounds of proof, contain a historical belief; if I would not take something to be true on the testimony of another: then I would not cite him, i.e., call him as witness. I can indeed relate something, e.g., that Plato asserted this and that, and I adduce the passage, but there I do not cite Plato. – Citation with respect to science of reason is a *heterogeneum*. The two do not fit together. It is consequently a great frailty when one sees oneself compelled to add historical belief to the grounds of reason. – In all empirical cognitions historical belief is indispensable. What we do not know, or cannot ourselves experience, we must cognize through the experience of others, and by its nature historical belief fits merely the experience of others. In matters that concern reason I can call no one as witness, but rather with that which others have experienced, this declaration of others of their experience, on account of which I take this matter to be true, is the testimony that produces historical belief. For the credibility of a witness there is required.",[]],"wiener-s06-030":["1.) The competence of the witness consists in this, that he has been able to tell the truth. To this belongs",[]],"wiener-s06-031":["a. that he has a sufficient skill to make experience. It is not so easy in all cases to make experience, and practice belongs to it. And in experience there is much deception of the senses. The common man is not a proper witness. For he cannot rightly make experiences. For when fear overcomes him: he sees one thing for another, and overlooks much.",[]],"wiener-s06-032":["b. that he has been in the circumstances in which he has been able to make experiences. Above all the common man cannot do it in all inner circumstances. E.g. he is often sleepy, fearful, distracted, etc.",[]],"wiener-s06-033":["c. that he also has the skill to declare his experiences, so that one can well understand his sense. This is not so easy. Many a one relates very punctiliously. Others can give only a light silhouette of their experience, and not give account of all its circumstances.",[]],"wiener-s06-034":["2.) The sincerity of the witness, that he has willed to tell the truth. The common man is too crude to place a great value in truth. A mouthful of lies, if something matters to one, does not, he believes, make much difference. Evils they always relate with an enlargement, so that everything is exaggerated and falls into the gruesome. In short, they seek to arouse astonishment. Here one must make them attentive through the threat of the oath. Then they begin to hesitate, and become very doubtful. – The very same also holds of ancient times. Not a single historian of the ancients restricts himself so punctiliously to the truths, but rather they always strive to write beautifully. They assume all rumors without investigating them. E.g. Herodotus has many old wives' tales. But they also do not hold it very important to tell the truth. E.g. Livy seeks to write floridly, and has generals give speeches that were never given. – We in our times are already more compelled to speak the truth, through circumstances, above all experimental physics, and that one seeks through observations and experiments to determine appearances precisely, in order to see through the laws of nature with more certainty. Only at the beginning of the previous *saeculum* did one begin to have insight that it is necessary to tell the truth wholly, and everyone then had to be entirely precise in his reports, and if someone deviated only a little from the truth in his writings: then he was disgraced and dishonored. For this reason one is accustomed to make use of all kinds of cautious expressions. E.g. Jurin always says, insofar as this is true etc. This precision in the report of experience afterwards passed over from natural science into history. For one had insight, it is necessary not to violate the truth in the slightest point. Now we have facilitations for experiencing the truth. The printing presses show immediately where he has erred in his writing, further the gazettes; little stories can no longer be related as in Rome. For whereas formerly someone came from one land into another after 3 years: now through the arrangement of the posts the report is in a few days from one place in the other. E.g. the old tales of Apollonius of Tyana and Alexander of Paphlagonia came to Rome, inasmuch as they grew on the way. In Rome they found approval, and one believed them, as indeed Philostratus also writes of it. Now, however, the enormous rise of rumors has been remedied by the post. But it was not so in ancient times, that the truth was so precisely determined. There are many honest people. But they often do not hold it necessary to be so punctilious when the untruth does no harm, or brings amusement with it, or they even believe that it is a report of great utility. This is the so-called *pia fraus*, if it is not a contradiction. – From all this it is evident that historical belief carries astonishing credibility with it, because the witness must have so many kinds of qualities in order to speak the truth. – Every human being has his various interest, which one cannot always judge. Many a one seeks merely through a narration to embellish the matter, etc. Since, therefore, without such critical grounds in the judgment of a testimony we cannot believe a witness: historical belief in important cases rests on such questionableness that it must be greatly examined. For how manifoldly is the human will disposed? It is therefore better to leave out this belief entirely, if it does not have the proper qualities, if one cannot guess why human beings speak thus and not otherwise. Meanwhile, certainty can nevertheless be grounded on the testimony of other human beings, as history and geography are grounded on it.",[]],"wiener-s06-035":["The ear-witness testifies to his experience of the testimony of another, of a matter. The eye-witness testifies to his own experience of the matter. Whoever relates a report that he has read somewhere is an ear-witness; who presents something that the listener could not cognize from his own experience is an ear-witness. With common saying, and tradition or rumor, one cannot determine the witness oneself. The witness is a multitude of persons, where one believes that the multitude will have credibility. A tradition is, insofar as the one has transmitted his testimonies to the other orally. Is therefore a series of subordinated testimonies.",[]],"wiener-s06-036":["If the concept unbelieving, which also occurs in theology, is to signify a reproach: then it cannot consist in the difficulty of attributing belief to a testimony, for that is no difficulty, but rather, as soon as it is a reproach, the ground must be moral. The first can redound to his honor, if he is slow to believe, or unbelieving. E.g. with a general rumor, this cannot redound to his moral disadvantage. With all narrations it is good and praiseworthy to be unbelieving, and one must there, if it is possible, proceed critically. Belief can be divided twofold into the theoretical and practical. With the theoretical one can be unbelieving. With moral belief he is called unbelieving who has no moral interest in assuming something as true, while he yet sees how important this or that proposition is. Moral belief one can therefore presuppose in every human being, and moral unbelief is consequently very blameworthy. One can also call him unbelieving who rejects the proof of the existence of God according to mere reason, and also him who no longer believes in virtue, if he does nothing from a moral interest, but everything from hypocrisy. Here the human being gives up all resolve to virtue, because he believes in none.",[]],"wiener-s06-037":["Of practical cognition. Here a twofold division is to be noted, whereby an ambiguity is prevented that can be occasioned by the words. A cognition is called practical in counter-relation to the theoretical, and also in counter-relation to speculative cognitions. – If a proposition is a commanding proposition, an *imperativus*, and says that something ought to happen: then it is a practical proposition; it says which free actions would be good in a certain aim. An *imperativus*",[]],"wiener-s06-038":["is thus a proposition of the sort that impels to a possible free action, insofar as it would be good and necessary in a certain aim. All practical propositions, if they are opposed to the theoretical, are *imperativi*. These are different: in sciences that are nothing but science or skill, there one takes up certain arbitrary aims, according to which one presupposes what ought to happen. E.g., in geometry, as I say, to measure a straight line, take etc. theoretical propositions, on the other hand, say not how it ought to happen, but how the thing is. E.g., A straight line is the shortest way etc. These can be distinguished in an instant from the practical, because the practical are at all times *imperativi*, and are quite easily to be distinguished from such propositions as attest the properties of the action. – But practical cognitions are also, second, opposed to the speculative. Cognitions can be theoretical, and yet are either practical, or speculative. For although they do not say what ought to happen, because they are theoretical: yet practical propositions can be derived from them, and are to that extent opposed to speculative propositions. E.g., There is a God, is a theoretical proposition, but *in potentia* practical, you must only act as if there were a supreme lawgiver of your actions. Thus cognitions, although they are in themselves theoretical, are not distinguished from other practical cognitions, because namely they are *in potentia* practical. – Speculative propositions are all those from which no rules and *imperativi* for our actions flow, in natural theology propositions are merely speculative. E.g., whether the omnipresence of God occupies a space, or consists merely in an influence on the creatures. This has no influence on our actions, and is thus a mere *speculation*. But whether the divine will is also a holy will, is a proposition of practical importance, from which rules for our conduct can be derived. Theoretical propositions are accordingly either speculative, or objectively practical propositions, i.e., which are practical *in potentia*. One could also say: All cognition is practical or theoretical. Practical is that where the propositions of the *imperativi* are expressed, in that they indicate the necessity of a free action. Theoretical cognitions contain the cognition of an object, what it is, but the use of them is always a speculative, or a practical use. And this difference is not so much as a difference of cognition, but of the use of cognition. Some we have merely for speculative use, some for practical use, and for rules of our conduct. The use of moral propositions can be a speculative use. Those who rationalize about the *principia* of virtue have merely a speculative use of them. The ancient philosophers, who philosophized about the highest good, never applied that in practical use. Not all cognitions can be applied to practical use. – Who can, e.g., make a practical use of mathematics or natural science? But we gladly seek to apply our cognitions to practical use. For through practical use our cognitions always obtain more truth, and the final end of all our cognitions seems after all to rest on this, that they are to contain a rule of that which harmonizes with our highest ends, and if no consequences for our conduct flow from it: then a cognition seems without utility. Hence also all speculation looks out toward the practical, even if one does not see it immediately. He who drew the first mathematical figure thought, after all, that a use could one day be made of it. Otherwise that would have been only a business of boredom. But supposing we wanted through mathematics merely to cultivate our reason: then that would be as good as a practical use. For reason can prescribe rules to our conduct, and that after all belongs to practical use. Hence the standing of our cognition is grounded preeminently on practical use. For in expectation of practical use we cultivate cognition. This worth of cognition we can sometimes consider as a mediate worth, as a means to other ends, or as an immediate worth, and that is then called dignity. No thing in the world has a dignity. It is the final end for the sake of which the world has this arrangement. Much in the perfection of the human being has a mediate worth, e.g., many properties of it. But one thing in him has after all an immediate worth, and that is his dignity. Mathematics has many a utility, that according to it calendars can be made, or fields measured etc. That is the mediate utility, and the market price of it. But in this it is not to be preferred to many other means, in that everything that is only a means is without worth. But already Plato says that this mediate utility is by far not the highest end of mathematics. For the propositions have in themselves their inner excellence, which by far surpasses all fields to be measured. Longinus says, that is not sublime, the contempt of which is sublime. E.g., wealth is not sublime. For the contempt of wealth is sublime. Thus by determining the field and the property of the human being mathematics is not sublime, but it has an inner worth, and there is found in mathematics a magnificence, and something that elevates our reason, and in which we feel our reason in its whole strength. Through the thoroughness of its proofs and *methode* it has a sublime worth. But now if we again consider this rightly: then we see well, this is after all not yet the right dignity, and that only that constitutes the true dignity, if the will is good. For what would all skill help, if the human being wanted to make use of it craftily? The will to make use of this skill is his true worth. All cognitions that contribute to the dignity of the human being are far from constituting his highest worth, but this is it: to make good use of all his talents. The true dignity of the human being thus rests on morality. Hence also that is called [ Textlücke ]practical which serves to improve our will, for which reason also philosophical morals is called practical philosophy, because in the science practical things occur. – If cognitions are so constituted that nothing practical is to be drawn from them, and that thus no actions at all flow from them: then such a cognition has a merely speculative use. –",[]],"wiener-s06-039":["The whole doctrine of practical use, which the author treats, does not belong in logic at all. For nothing belongs in logic but the logical form of all cognitions, i.e., the form of thinking without regard to the content. It can thus contain only propositions that can be demonstrated. Experience of things, in which empirical principles must be laid at the ground, does not belong in logic, because it cannot be demonstrated. But practical cognition is distinguished from speculative as to content. Hence logic can have to do with the practical. – The author treats of desires and aversion. But in logic one must think as if one had no will at all, a practical science would come of it, we therefore have the science of thinking, and not of willing. –",[]],"wiener-s06-040":["He speaks further of living and dead cognitions, dead, i.e., those which could move the will, because they are practical, and yet do not move it, and are thus merely *speculation*. – But it strikes the eye at once that all this does not belong to logic. He speaks of indifference, when something is indifferent etc.",[]],"wiener-s07-001":["Up to now we have spoken of cognition in general, insofar as cognition is distinct or clear, certain or uncertain. We have treated of the kind of proofs and of taking to be true, of belief and knowledge, and so forth. Now the author divides cognition, which should have happened right at the beginning, into its elements. Logically all beginnings in thinking are divided thus:",[]],"wiener-s07-002":["1.) Cognition is a simple cognition, a concept.",[]],"wiener-s07-003":["2.) Cognitions combined become a judgment.",[]],"wiener-s07-004":["3.) That judgments are combined, and inferences arise from them. The ancients said: *Quot sunt operationes mentis? Resp. tres. apprehensio simplex, Judicium et Ratiocinium.* [How many are the operations of the mind? Answer: three. simple apprehension, judgment and reasoning.] Now as concerns the concept, we will begin with what is the very simplest in our cognitions, namely that cognition is a representation, *repraesentatio*. A representation with consciousness is *perceptio*. I have a delusion and think I am conscious of it. Insofar as with such a representation I also pay attention to the object: then it is *cognitio*, cognition. Cognition is twofold, *intuitus*, intuition, *conceptus*, concept. *Intuitus* is a single representation, *repraesentatio singularis*.",[]],"wiener-s07-005":["In *perception* we do not relate our cognitions to an object. Through sensation, well-being, pain – one cognizes no object. It is only a kind of representations. But it is not distinguished from a particular object. In general the relation of the representation to the *subject* is called sensation, to the object, cognition. Logic occupies itself merely with cognition, but not with sensation",[]],"wiener-s07-006":["*Conceptus* is *repraesentatio communis*, which is common to many things. Whoever wanted first to have a representation of the red color had to see the red color. But when with the red color he compared cinnabar, c ramoisin and ponceau red: then he became aware that in the red color there is something *general*, which was also in other representations",[]],"wiener-s07-007":["of the red color contained along with it, and he thought under red that which was common to many objects, and that was a concept. A concept is thus a representation that is common to many things. In the *intuitus* I consider single things. E.g., the sun, earth. But if I think a certain genus of planets: then it is *repraesentatio communis*, i.e., *conceptus*. – Concept is distinguished from intuition in that all intuition is single. He who sees the first tree does not know what that is which he sees. If he becomes aware that these objects have something in common, then he thereupon leaves out everything that they have that is different, and takes together what they have in common, and so he has *repraesentationem communem*, i.e., *conceptum*. From this it is evident that one must not say *conceptus communis*, because it would be a *tautology*, because every *conceptus* is also *communis*. – Every *conceptus* is *empiricus*, or *purus*. *Conceptus empiricus* is that which is generated through the comparison of objects of experience. *Repraesentatio empirica* is that which arises from the senses. This can become a *conceptus* if I take what is common to several empirical representations. One sees well that in the distinction of *empiricus* and *purus* it comes down to the origin of the concept, and this is thus already a metaphysical investigation. For logic does not ask where concepts come from, but how they can be formed and ordered according to the laws of the understanding. That a concept is, thus belongs to logic. But whether it comes independently of experience, or from experience, does not belong before it. – There are many *conceptus empirici*, i.e., we often compare experiences, and form from them *repraesentationem communem*, and this is then called *empiricus conceptus*. – To the *empiricus* the *conceptus purus* is *contradistinguished*. *Purus* is the concept that has its origin independently of experience in the mere understanding. The concept, e.g., of cause, magnitude, *quality* etc. Whether there are such concepts is an investigation that belongs in *metaphysics*, because this is *cognitio pura*. – Our morals has many *conceptus*. The concept of virtue is not encountered in experience, and even if the world showed nothing but vice: we would still have a concept of virtue. – This *purus* can either arise from the understanding, and indeed, if its ground is merely in the understanding, its object can still be represented *in concreto*. E.g., cause and effect of the understanding. One can well distinguish the things in the understanding,",[]],"wiener-s07-008":["sense, of which there is talk with effect, cause etc., but the concept of *causality* lies merely in the understanding. Now the question is: can one encounter the object of this his concept of the understanding in experience? Answer. Yes. This happens through the examples. An example of *causality* is: the fire destroys the wood. –",[]],"wiener-s07-009":["Or this *conceptus purus* is so constituted that its object can be given in no experience. Then it is a concept not of the understanding, but of mere reason, i.e., one does not have to understand the concept from experience, but I go beyond that to which experience leads me. – *conceptus purus* is called *generaliter notio*. *Notio* is thus already more than concept for, e.g., the concept of possibility cannot be exhibited in experience. A *notio* whose object can be given in experience is called *notio intellectualis*. If the object can be given in no experience, it is called *notio rationis*, or *idea*. Thus the *notio*, insofar as it can be given in no experience, and also corresponds to no experience, is properly an *idea*. The concept of the whole can be given in experience, *est notio intellectualis*, but if I make for myself yet a new concept, e.g., the concept of a world, which is to be the ungenerated whole that encompasses all things, and in which all parts stand together in combination, one cannot give it in experience. What can, if the world is eternal, comprise the whole eternity of it? The concept of the world thus is merely an idea. – The concept of the part is *conceptus purus intellectualis, seu notio*. But the concept of a part that is not composite is *notio rationis, idea*. So long as my reason represents something divisible, it can always still be divided. But my reason finally demands the last part, which cannot be divided further, i.e., is simple. This concept cannot be shown in experience, is thus a concept *a priori*, an idea. The concept of God is an idea. For experience cannot teach us whether all possible perfections may be together anywhere. – The whole of morals rests on ideas. A virtue we cannot encounter among human beings at all. But yet my reason must have a concept of virtue, as it must be in its whole perfection. We can well perceive virtue in experience. But there much more must still be added, it is thus an idea. – The doctrine of ideas is very important, but belongs properly in metaphysics. It has hitherto been expounded wrongly. But if one does not distinguish them rightly: then we cannot rightly determine the use of our cognitions. The *ordinary* authors use the word *idea* quite wrongly. E.g., one speaks of the *idea* of the red color, whereas this is not even *notio*. One speaks of the *idea* of a triangle, whereas it is only a *notio*. In metaphysics it is shown that *rationes* are either *intellectual*, or *sensitive*. *Rationes intellectuales* are necessity, cause etc., which are given the name *categories*. *Rationes sensitivae* are where an object of the senses is given which I can cognize not in empirical intuition, but in pure intuition. The concept of the triangle is thus *notio sensitiva*. The ideas exclude all our knowledge. They are drawn merely from reason, but yet flow into experience, as rules according to which one can institute most experiences.",[]],"wiener-s07-010":["How do concepts arise? I.e., how do representations become concepts? Logic does not concern itself with how *data* for a cognition must be constituted, but merely with what the understanding does therein, thus pays attention to the form, and not to the object. How does it come about, then, that *repraesentatio singularis* becomes *communis*? Answer *per comparationem, separationem seu abstractionem* [through comparison, separation or abstraction]. I compare things, and *attend* to what they have in common, and *abstract* from all remaining things: then this is a *conceptus* through which all these things can be thought.",[]],"wiener-s07-011":["It is a misuse in logic that one retains the expression *abstract* in such a way that one says: *aliquid abstrahere* [to abstract something]. E.g., as if, in order to have the concept of a tree, I took the concept of the leaves and of the trunk separately, and *abstracted* from every difference of the tree, and said: what has trunk and leaves is a tree. No, I do not *abstract* the leaves and the trunk, but I retain them, and *separate* them from everything remaining. I must pay attention to what a cognition has in common, and *abstract* from what it has that is different. e.g., from the largeness or smallness of the tree. Accordingly the word must not be used in such a way that it says *aliquid abstrahere*. I *ignore* from the remaining things. *Abstraction* thus adds nothing, but cuts off all that which does not belong to the concept, and marks merely what it has in common with other representations. There thus belongs to the distinguishing of a concept *Comparatio*. I must have *attention* to the agreement and *identity* of things, which one calls wit, and second *abstractio*. – It is thus falsely expressed when one speaks of *conceptibus abstractis*, since *abstractis* is supposed to mean as much as *qui abstractus est a re* [which is abstracted from the thing]. This is false, *nam non abstrahimus a re, sed separamus conceptum ab aliis rebus, conceptum abstrahimus a ceteris* [for we do not abstract from the thing, but separate the concept from other things, we abstract the concept from the rest]. We must *abstract* from many things, if I think merely *purely* the red color in red scarlet: then I already *abstract* from many colors, if I think in scarlet merely an extended being, then I already *abstract* from many more things, if I think therein merely that it is a thing, then I *abstract* from still more things. But properly we should say use of the concept is *abstract*. For every use of the concept is either *in abstracto*, or *concreto*. I cognize a concept *in concreto* if I apply it to that which is contained under it. I think the concept *in abstracto* if I think it in general. From this one sees: A cognition is *abstract*, not because the concept is thought *in abstracto*, without being applied to objects. E.g., One speaks of education, and treats merely of the early formation of the human being, without speaking just of the application to the rearing of children. Here the concept is not expounded *in concreto* at all, but merely *in abstracto*.",[]],"wiener-s08-001":["The form of a concept consists in common validity. *Repraesentatio, quae pluribus est communis* [Representation which is common to several]. That thus constitutes the *conceptus*. – *Conceptus communis* I cannot say, because it would be a *tautology*. *Tautology* is an *enunciation* when I make the concept distinct through itself. For if a representation is not *repraesentatio communis*: then it is no concept at all. Since it is thus already contained in it: I must not add it. – But the use of a *conceptus* can be *singularis*. For what holds of many things can also be applied to a single case. I think a human being *in individuo*, i.e., I use the concept of the human being in order to have an *ens singulare*. I can make use of a concept insofar as it is applied to many objects, then the concept is used as *repraesentatio communis*, i.e., used *in abstracto*, e.g., house. If now I say of all houses, they must have a roof: then it is *usus universalis*. But it is always the same concept, and is used here quite universally. For to have a roof holds for all houses. The use of the concept thus goes universally to all. But a particular use goes only to many. E.g., some houses must have a gate. Or I use the concept only for a single thing. E.g., this house is plastered thus or so. We thus do not divide the *conceptus* into *universales, particulares* and *singulares*, but the judgments, as we will soon hear. I can in my judgment compare the thing with all, some, and a single thing. This serves to determine our expressions exactly, and not to speak of everything in universal concepts. In kind all concepts are universal, and can always hold of other things in a certain way. E.g., plant holds for all grasses, but also for some bodies. The understanding has the faculty of concepts, and one can also define it thus. We said above, the understanding is the faculty of rules. But this is the same, for if I give a concept, I always give a foundation of rules.",[]],"wiener-s08-002":["Concepts arise *per comparationem, reflexionem et abstractionem*. In one consciousness I grasp many representations, in which I compare what is only a repetition of the other. From *reflection* one thus cognizes that which many things have in common, afterwards one takes away the *abstraction* in which they do not agree, and then *repraesentatio communis* remains. No concept thus comes about without comparison, without perception of an accord and without *abstraction*. If I could not *abstract*: then I would have no concept, because then something other would always come to my mind than what is common to the single representation. E.g., If with the expression house there always came to someone's mind only the jug that he had seen, he retained always an *intuitus*. One sees well that through leaving out and *abstracting* no concept comes about, but it completes it, and makes it that it does not remain a *singularis*. The *positive* in the arising of a concept is *comparing* and *reflecting*, the *negative* *abstracting*. For many a human being it becomes hard to *abstract*, even if he can *compare* and *reflect*. Hence one has one's dear trouble when one has to do with such human beings. Habit has already brought them to the point that it costs them great effort to leave out what does not belong to the concept. That most human beings have such bad principles comes from this, that they think so vulgarly",[]],"wiener-s08-003":["and cannot *abstract* the seemly, the fitting etc. from a concept of virtue.",[]],"wiener-s08-004":["*Abstract* concept is *abstract* only in use, it is less *abstract* if I say *in concreto* what I have said *in abstracto*. E.g., Body is an extension that has a figure. Now I give examples, i.e., I take all things that I have left out in the concept. The concept is used *in abstracto* if I leave out all differences that are common to the thing. The concept consequently arises indeed always through *abstracting*, but the concept itself is not *abstract*, but its use. The use of the concept *in concreto* has its degrees, until finally the concept becomes *maxime in concreto*, and belongs only to one *individuum*. Likewise I can also go ever further out in *abstraction*. E.g., human being, animal, living being etc.",[]],"wiener-s08-005":["Every concept contains more possible concepts under itself, and contains that which is common to different representations of several things. If therefore a concept contains something that is common to several things: then it is itself contained in other possible concepts, it is a part of them, but contains only what they have in common, and leaves out what is different in them. Every universal concept is thus contained in the concepts from which it is *abstracted*. E.g., the concept metal belongs to gold, copper etc.",[]],"wiener-s08-006":["Conversely these things are contained under it, and just this constitutes the usefulness of a concept. With intuition I have no other usefulness than only a single object. Now the ground always contains the sequences under itself. Accordingly every universal concept is a ground of cognition of many things, and equipped with a concept I have a ground of cognition of many things. But the concept itself is also contained in the things. For it constitutes only a part of their reason.",[]],"wiener-s08-007":["One calls a concept a higher concept insofar as it contains others under itself, and every concept is consequently a higher representation of others, because it always contains many under itself. A lower concept is such a concept as is contained under others. E.g., the human being, he is an animal. The concept of the animal belongs to all human beings, and also to still more things, consequently human being is a lower concept than animal. – All *conceptus* stand in such *relation* to one another that one *conceptus* is always *respectively* *superior* and *inferior* to the other, insofar as one is contained under the other, and from this there finally comes about a series of *subordinated* concepts. The *Conceptus infimus* cannot be determined. For as soon as I have a concept that I apply to *individua*, it would yet still be possible that among the *individua*, although I make no further difference, still smaller differences take place. In this *gradation* now one is always again higher than the other, until I come to the highest. The lowest cognition is intuition, because it always goes to something individual. The *conceptum summum* I can state, because there must after all be a concept in which I can leave out everything. For if I want to make a higher concept: I must always *abstract*. But if I cannot *abstract*: then no higher concept can be made any more, e.g., of Something. – The *conceptum superiorem* we call, in regard to this *inferioris*, a *genus*. If we thus compare many *conceptus* with one another: then we can call every concept, in regard to the *inferioris*, the *genus*, in regard to its *superioris*, *Species*. *Genus* and *species* consequently make in themselves no difference in the concepts, but only in the *Relation* of the concepts. Scholar is a *genus* in regard to the *philosophers*, but scholar is a *species* in regard to the human being. Now we can think a series of *generibus* and *speciebus*, among which yet some will have to be *genera superiora*, until we finally come to a *genus summum*, namely Something, object.",[]],"wiener-s08-008":["Every *species* is at the same time a *genus* in regard to the *species* contained under it. *Species infima* is only comparatively *infima*, and in use the last. It must still always be possible to find another *species*, through which this would then in turn become a *genus*. But applied immediately to the *individua* a *species* can be called *species infima*. – We can here take into consideration the domain and the content of a concept. *Sphaera* is the domain of a concept, and pertains to the multitude of things that are contained under the concept. We consider the concept according to its content when we look to the multitude of representations that are contained in the concept itself. The greater the domain of a concept is, the smaller is its content, i.e., the less it contains in itself. E.g., the concept human being has a great domain, but the concept Negro contains still more, namely also the concept of the black color, has consequently more content. Now the higher a concept is, the more one must abstract from the manifold, and the more the content dwindles. It is a concept all the higher, the more things it is contained in, and the more it contains that which belongs to more things. Now since in the case of the highest concept I must leave out so much that there remains for me only that which is common to all things: so this contains, precisely for that reason, the very least in itself. From the highest concept, consequently, the lowest will be determined, and this at once comprehends the correct *determination* of all things. A thoroughly determined concept is the lowest of all. For it is not common to several things. A higher concept, which is *multimode* undetermined, and the highest concept is that which is undetermined in all things. The logical *sphaera* always grows, as a gold leaf stretches when it loses in thickness, and precisely on that account it becomes so hard for the human being to go up into the heights, and to think matters without content. The nearer, on the other hand, the concepts come to experience, the fuller or more concrete is the representation. The question is, which is better, a very concrete or a very abstract representation, that one there approaches the *individua*, or the *genus summum*? *Resp.*: the one is as good as the other. It is fine when our representations have a great *sphaera*. For then a concept is everywhere valid for that which is contained under it, consequently the universality of cognition always rests on the *sphaera*. But notwithstanding this the concept nevertheless loses in content, and this lack is unavoidable, and in regard to the human understanding lamentable. Every lower concept is also called *angustior*, every one that has a higher *sphaeram*, *latior* The *genus* is thus always *conceptus latior*, *species conceptus angustior*. If two concepts stand next to each other, and have the same *sphaeram*: then they are called *conceptus reciproci*. The chapter on *definitions* is the most important of all in logic. For it is always the greatest demand that one can make on a philosopher, that he should define his concept. All logical perfection of our cognition consisted in this, that our concept was distinct. The second perfection was that it was also at the same time complete, that the distinctness namely rested not merely on some clear marks, but on as many as, taken together, constitute the whole concept. Now if the concept is exhaustive: then, to be sure, no mark is lacking. But although none is lacking: still one could perhaps be superfluous, inasmuch as one mark could already be contained in another. This is an imperfection in the concept, when we multiply marks unnecessarily, and through *tautologies* make one mark into many marks. Accordingly it also belongs to the logical perfection of the concept that the concept be measured, i.e., that the concept contain no more marks than are necessary to constitute the concept. Measured, *praecisus*, is probably not a good Latin expression. However *determinatus*, which the author calls measured, signifies nothing at all. For *indeterminatus* is a concept that contains too little, consequently *determinatus* would be a concept to which more and more is added, and which consequently is not exhaustive. But *praecidere* is used of plants for pruning, and so it is also with our concepts, which we prune, namely in regard to the superfluous marks. If we take *completudo* and *praecision* together: we can call a concept *adaequat*. It is adequate to the object when the marks contain neither more nor less than precisely constitutes the concept. So that the concept not be so small, it must be complete, so that it also not be so large, *praecis*. A *definition* is thus a *conceptus distinctus adaequatus*. Wolff, however, takes the word *adaequat* quite differently, and calls a concept *adaequat* where I not merely have clear marks, but cognize the marks themselves distinctly. These, however, are intensive degrees of distinctness, of which there is no talk here. Now if the marks are clear: then it is already a *definition* after the fact. If I can also define the marks: then this is a *definition* of the parts of the *definition*. If with him that concept is already called *adaequat* whose marks are merely distinct: then he is indeed already at the end. But one can indeed still make marks of marks distinct. With us, however, the expression *adaequat*, or *conceptus adaequatus*, completes the definition and fits the object. Our *definition* consequently says just as much as *definitio est conceptus distinctus completus praecisus*.",[]],"wiener-s09-001":["The *definition* has a certain perfection beyond which nothing can go. One could object: do you not give marks of marks in the *definition*? *Resp.* One can also define the marks of the marks, and from that make a new *definition*. Since they thus constitute the greatest perfection of a concept: so we must know all their *requisita* precisely, and see how far it is feasible to make them, how far we get with it. All our concepts are either given or made concepts. A concept is given insofar as it does not arise from my choice. It can, however, be given either *a priori* merely in the understanding, or *a posteriori* through experience. I have many concepts that are given to me through the nature of my understanding, and which I have not invented. E.g., the concept of cause, time, etc. Likewise many concepts are given to us through experience. E.g., that water is a fluid body.",[]],"wiener-s09-002":["To the *conceptibus datis* are *contradistinguished* the *conceptus factitii*, the made concepts, which likewise are made either *a priori* or *a posteriori*. A concept is made *a priori* when, without the objects being given through experience, it is made through pure reflection. E.g., I represent to myself a 1000-gon, without ever having seen it. Such a concept is also called *conceptus fictitius*, a fictitious concept. Better, it seems to me, a thought-up concept. I can also make a concept *a posteriori*, so that the object is given to me in experience. E.g., I have a piece of metal, that is always given *a posteriori*, not made. But if I want to have a distinct concept of it: then I must test the metal according to all its properties, and in this way I find them through various experiences which do not lie in the concept, the nature of the metal is thus a made concept *a posteriori*.",[]],"wiener-s09-003":["To make a concept *a posteriori* thus means to track the given experience further, and so to extract an *adaequat* concept. – That would thus be nothing more than to extend the concept. –",[]],"wiener-s09-004":["All our concepts, insofar as they are given to us, be it *a priori* or *a posteriori*, can only be defined *per analysin*, through dissection. For because it is given: so I cannot make it distinct otherwise than by making clear the marks that lie in it, and that is precisely then *analysis*. If this *analysis* is complete: then from it there comes a complete distinctness. If there are, thereafter, not so many of the marks: then it is *praecis*, and so a *definition* arises.",[]],"wiener-s09-005":["But if concepts are not given but made, so that we are not merely to bring forth the distinctness of the concept, but are to make the concept itself: then we will bring about a *definition* only *per synthesin*. E.g., if I want to represent to myself a spirit: then I must make the concept for myself. I say: I want to represent to myself a thinking being that is combined with no body. Here I have first made the concept through the *definition*. Made concepts are thus not possible to define otherwise than *per synthesin*. The whole of mathematics defines *per synthesin*, it defines not given but made concepts. I want to think a figure, says the *mathematicus*, which is to look thus and so, and to be called thus and so. One can also have a concept *a priori factitium* whose *materials* experience has indeed given, the concept itself, however, has been made. E.g., let one think of an unconquerable fortress, of which there is yet none at all. The materials, such as ditch, stone – lie in experience. The concept itself is *factitius*. So it is with him who invents a new instrument.",[]],"wiener-s09-006":["*A posteriori*, however, there are also *factitios conceptus*. If I want to define an experience by explaining, through experience, that which is not contained in my concept: then this too must happen *per synthesin*. I want to procure for myself a more extended concept than I had, thereby I get in the end a concept that consists of marks which I gathered together *per Synthesin* from experience. – It is thus a universal rule, all given concepts can be defined only analytically, all made concepts, be they made through the understanding or experience, synthetically. When can I say that a concept is defined in the *analysi*? When all its marks are complete and *praecis*. When can we be certainly convinced that our *analysis* is complete? *Resp.* We can never be, because it can happen only through tests, in which we can still always overlook much, consequently all analytic *definitions* are combined with an uncertainty. If a concept is a concept of experience: then it is still less noticeable to know that I have analyzed correctly. Through *analysis* one never has apodictic certainty. All analytic *definitions* we know in such a way that one can give no sure *criterium* of them.",[]],"wiener-s09-007":["Synthetic *definitions*, insofar as they have a fictitious object, can never err, because I say according to my choice, I want to think this. – Synthetic *definitions* of concepts drawn from experience are wholly and entirely impossible. We cannot make any object in experience complete, and give all the marks that constitute the concept. For *experimental*",[]],"wiener-s09-008":["*physics* still always discovers from time to time new properties of bodies.",[]],"wiener-s09-009":["Analytically I make a given concept distinct by merely *exponing* the concept, *per synthesin*, however, I make a distinct concept for myself. If we are to define a concept analytically: then it is only given to us, and we are only to make it complete and *praecis*. – If this *analysis* is only incomplete: then that is indeed no *definition*, but it is nevertheless already a degree of distinctness, which can be continued further, and approach ever more to *completudini*. Before a concept becomes complete, it must yet necessarily be incomplete beforehand, and secondly it is indeed also not always possible to attain to complete concepts. Should a distinct concept indeed take place that is not yet complete? I can certainly also use a complete *analysis*, because I do also already have some clear marks. Hence it is not always possible with every concept that I have defined it, and it is not necessary that, in order to speak of an object, one have defined it *per analysin*. For where I cannot say everything, I must say only as much as I can say with truth. The *definitions* are consequently not so necessary as one believes, and this is to be noted for this reason, because the *definition* is in most cases extremely difficult, and the analytic completion cannot always be expected. But that one is sure that with the complete *analysis* one cognizes enough is evident from this, because the incomplete *analysis* is yet already a part of the *definition*, and consequently is yet only true and correct. E.g., if I say, virtue is a facility in morally good actions, and I leave out thereby that it is also a mastery over our inclinations: then I ask: can nothing at all be inferred from the first incomplete concept? *contraire*. From the first most philosophers have concluded all their morals. From every mark of a thing I can draw consequences, if I cognize only some marks in the thing, then some consequences can be drawn, but if I cognize everything, then all consequences can be drawn. Thus to begin first with the *definition*, as yet happens in most *philosophies*, and not to engage in anything before one has the *definition* in advance, means actually to make all investigation impossible. For I must indeed begin first from the imperfect. The endeavor of *philosophers* is, to be sure, to attain to the greatest perfection through *definitions*. However, we must not say that that of which no *definition*",[]],"wiener-s09-010":["has been given deserves no treatment at all. For of many things we can give no complete concept. E.g., the jurists have never yet been able to give a *definition* of equity.",[]],"wiener-s09-011":["Concepts that are given *a priori* independently of experience can be defined analytically. For here the concept concerns an object that I am to cognize only through the understanding, consequently as much as I am to say of it must be in my understanding, because we cannot go out of our understanding and seek elsewhere. So the whole of metaphysics and morals has to do with the analytic *definition*. But although these two sciences are the true *objects* of analytic *definitions*: still it is evident that the *definitions* are not always indispensably necessary, but rather with the analytic *definition* one must never begin by saying, this or that is to be a *definition*. For it requires a proof whether all *analysis* is complete, I must first show that the marks lie in the concept, and then show that, taken together, they constitute the concept.",[]],"wiener-s09-012":["It would be very excellent, and would give our cognitions a great worth, if in *philosophy* we had all such concepts adequate to the thing, which thereby also did not overstep the *praecision*, for that is the attainment of our sciences. But since we are not in a position to achieve such: so we must make do with as many clear marks of a concept as we can find in our reason. Such incomplete concepts, which also take place in *physics*, we call *descriptions*.",[]],"wiener-s09-013":["To want to say straight out that this alone constitutes the concept is too *dictatorial*. For we must first say how we have come to the *analysi*. It is thus quite false, what Wolff attempts in his *philosophy*, and each of his *definitions* is also on that account false in *philosophy*. The concept is commonly incompletely analyzed, although one cannot deny that he gives fairly clear marks, and often comes very close to the whole concept, and his *definitions* in *philosophy* and natural right can notwithstanding be used very well, inasmuch as they show much acumen. He had learned the sending ahead of the *definition* in *mathematics*, where that is to be sure quite good, because this has synthetic *definitions*. One finds that even in social gatherings. One speaks, e.g., of right, and the one says: define right for me. He wants me to have fathomed the whole depth of the concept, instead of one's being able to *reason* about that which one knows, and which yet also lies in the concept.",[]],"wiener-s09-014":["Hence it also comes about that such *definitions* are always 99 / 100 false, and Baumeister's defined *philosophy* teems with errors. – Of mathematical *definitions* not a single one is false, because the concepts are made arbitrarily, all analytic ones, however, are given concepts. Can concepts of experience be defined analytically? If the natural scientist, e.g., defines water as such a fluid body without taste and color: then one indeed sees how wavering the *definition* is. Whoever does not otherwise know water will not become acquainted with it thereby. But it is not at all needful to define concepts of experience *per analysin*. For what do I need such a *definition* for? For when I say the word water; then the other understands me. But in order to teach him to know it, for that the *definition* is wholly unfit. For in the concept water there lies so very little that I at once go out of the concept, and must gather new marks through experience, but that means to define a concept synthetically, and not analytically, through the *exposition*. Analytically the empirical concept can indeed be defined according to that which lies in it. But one never seeks that, because so little lies in it. Synthetically, however, it also cannot be defined, because we cannot know all possible marks which experience can teach of an object, consequently concepts of experience can never be defined.",[]],"wiener-s09-015":["The *result* of all that has been said is thus: our concepts are either given or made. The given ones are either empirical or concepts of the understanding. The given concept of experience can be properly explained neither *per synthesin* nor *per analysin*, because in the former case I can explain it merely [ Textlücke ] through new marks, in the latter case too little lies in it to be able to distinguish it from others. With concepts of the understanding, and indeed given ones, likewise no proper analytic explanation is possible, which, however, lies not in the matter but in us, inasmuch as it is difficult to give the marks of a concept *praecis*. Arbitrary concepts can be made both *a priori* and *a posteriori*, but only synthetically. Synthetically only *conceptus arbitrarii (factitii)* can be defined, which are made *per synthesin arbitrariam*, to which all mathematical concepts belong. Indeed, what is still more, with the *conceptu arbitrario* one must always go ahead with the *definition*, because precisely thereby the concept is brought forth, and because precisely thereby I *declare* what I want to say through such a concept, which I have made arbitrarily, consequently without *definition* such a concept would be nothing.",[]],"wiener-s09-016":["The *nominal definition* is that distinct concept which suffices for the distinguishing of a thing from others. *Real definition* is that distinct concept which suffices to cognize and to separate off everything that belongs to the thing, it consequently suffices internally to explain the thing, and to understand that which belongs to the thing.",[]],"wiener-s09-017":["It is *essential*, and contains the concept of the essence of the thing. The *nominal definition*, on the other hand, is only externally sufficient, in comparison with others, to have insight into its *identity* or distinction with others. It signifies hardly anything more than what the expression *nominal definition* says, a certain attestation of the term of the thing, in order to make the name of the thing distinct, but not thereby to have better insight into the thing itself. It does not comprehend in itself the essence of the thing, but only a comparison of some marks of difference with others. It can therefore also have merely *comparative* validity. E.g., if I say: the human being is an animal that has the faculty of speaking. Here I can distinguish him from all animals (but yet not from the starling). Meanwhile the concept is already sufficient in comparison with other animals. –",[]],"wiener-s09-018":["How can I know that my concept is sufficient to distinguish the thing from all possible ones? *Resp.* Only those marks can suffice absolutely which, taken together, constitute the whole essence of the thing, for the whole essence of the thing cannot – be common to two things. And this is then the *real definition*. The *nominal definitions* are only valid *in tantum*, so far as I make use of them. The *real definitions* are thus the proper *definitions*. When Wolff says that the arising of the concept can also be comprehended from the *real definition*: then this is false. For the arising must afterwards be inferred from the *real definition*. For it makes possible the judgment how it could well have arisen. His examples put what we have said very well into light – as when he, e.g., gives as a *real definition*: a circle is a curved line which moves around a fixed point, and whose parts are equally far removed from the center, then the first is superfluous, because it can be inferred from the last. The arising is only a consequence from the *real definition*. Wolff indeed also says merely that. Otherwise the *real definition* has nothing to do with the arising, but only with the inner possibility of the thing. – If I know a thing according to an *attributo proprio*, then I can also define it through such an *attributum proprium*. E.g., the human being is an animal that judges. Because judgment is an *attributum proprium* of a human being: so it must necessarily flow from the whole essence of the human being. If it flowed only from a part of the essence: then several things which are of different essence could nevertheless agree with the essence in respect of this part. The *definition* thus actually holds for the whole essence. For it cannot be understood otherwise than of the whole essence. – Through the *real definition* the thing can be distinguished at all times, and from all the rest. It is very hard to distinguish the two, and often one takes for *real definition* what was only *nominal definition*. With synthetic objects the *definitions* are always *real*. For the object is given only through this one *definition a priori*, and thereby it is possible to think it, but to make *real definitions* of given analytic objects is very hard. I cannot even know whether my *nominal definition* is sufficient, because I can never find the marks to distinguish the thing from all possible others, because I cannot compare it with all possible ones, but only then, when I know that my marks *exhaust* the whole concept, i.e., when I can give a *real definition*, is certainly also a *nominal definition* sufficient. So it stands with all *definitions* in natural science –. In morals, metaphysics, etc., on the other hand, nothing but *real definitions* must occur, because this is yet the goal toward which we must all direct our *analysis*, to bring about a complete concept. *Definition* is properly a sum total, a *recension* or *exposition* of marks, insofar as they contain the materials for a possible concept. Describing is no determinate rule. One can be as circumstantial as one wants, or be brief. We can accomplish much with it, inasmuch as we thereby make matters recognizable beforehand. Nevertheless descriptions always go before the *definitions* of such concepts as are empirical, indeed since we cannot define empirical concepts: so they are capable only of a description. Concepts *a priori*, on the other hand, if they are made *per synthesin*, e.g., in *mathematics*, must always be defined. A given concept of reason, however, need not always be defined, but rather one then often makes do with an incomplete concept. Descriptions, however, are not at all fitting in concepts of reason. So, e.g., Lavater, who through the liveliness of his *fantasy* gives more *excursions* than that he exhausts the matters, when he describes love, gives very many effects of love. But from all that one cannot get out *praecis* the concept of what love is. For speculative sciences *descriptions* are not at all suitable, but only *in praxi*, where I can combine a lively image with my concept.",[]],"wiener-s09-019":["Distinctness is the most important of all in the *definition*. Then follows the *completudo*. The *praecision*, however, is not so important as the *completudo*. This *praecision* is difficult in many cases, because we unnoticed take one mark twice, because we take matters to be different which have different names. E.g., a body is an extended divisible being. Extension here already lies in divisibility. With *descriptions* one does not take this whole precisely at all, because there one seeks only the proposition for a possible *definition*, which one perhaps does not want to make at all. So in the catechism I can give many descriptions of God according to his properties, although I see in the end that the *praecise* concept of God is a being of all beings. Even in the *definition* nothing depends on it if something is lacking in the *praecision*, and there is too much in the *definition*. That can do no harm at all, except that one offends against the *Canon*: *quod fieri potest per pauca etc.* *Praecision* is only a law of economy, and facilitates the consciousness of the *completudo*. How do we judge a *definition* that comes up? *Resp.* We ask:",[]],"wiener-s09-020":["1.) Whether the *definition* as a proposition be true, whether that actually belongs to the thing which is said of it in the *definition*? E.g., ground is that from which I cognize why something is, is a true proposition.",[]],"wiener-s09-021":["2.) The concept must be distinct. Even if something is true as a proposition: it is not on that account yet distinct as a concept. So the above example does not become distinct to me at all through the *definition*. For why means as much as through which ground. Consequently I would have said: a ground is that from which I cognize through which ground something is true. This is thus *idem per idem*. That is a principal error which is very usual, and most explanations in *discourses* are of that kind.",[]],"wiener-s09-022":["3.) If the concept is only true as a proposition, and is also distinct: then I can ask whether the distinct concept is also complete? Whether the marks taken together constitute the whole concept? This is very hard. For how can we know that nothing more is lacking to our concept, and that we have left nothing out? We do not, to be sure, notice that something is still lacking to us. But that is no sufficient ground to call the concept complete. For how often do we not notice something which we nevertheless still find after the fact?",[]],"wiener-s09-023":["4.) The exhaustive concept must also be determinate. This can still be done, and if it is not there: then it is no great error. The *praecision* is the *reduction* of the *definition ad minimos terminos*, just as 12 / 24 can be *reduced* to 1 / 2, so I must also be able to *praecide* the concept. It is very fine when we can do it, and know how to express all our *definitions* in *sententious* propositions. This is the elegance of a cognition.",[]],"wiener-s09-024":["Accordingly a false *definition* can contain true propositions, and precisely on that account be possible, although it cannot be taken for a concept adequate to the thing. Only I must not convert it. Otherwise an error arises, inasmuch as the one mark does not exhaust the whole thing.",[]],"wiener-s09-025":["Through what is a concept made distinct? Through clear marks. But if these marks are *tautological*, i.e., if one is contained in the other: then the concept does not become distinct in the least, although it sometimes seems so, because the *tautology* is hidden in it, to which one has to look closely in the investigation of the *definition*.",[]],"wiener-s09-026":["How then are *definitions* found? It goes without saying that this concerns only the analytic *definition*. For in the *synthesi* of an arbitrary concept, where I make a concept, the *definition* need not be found, because I say out distinctly what I have invented in the understanding. Such *definitions* of the mathematician are properly only *declarations* of the opinion of his will, and mathematical *definitions* consequently need no rule at all. For he need only say distinctly to everyone what sort of concept he wants to *establish* and fix. Yet the *mathematici* sometimes mistake their concept, and indeed in ancient times more rarely than in more recent ones. Euclid never did it. But Wolff brings all *philosophy* into *mathematics*. When he gives the concept of similarity: then he analyzes the concept. But here there can be great danger that he has not made his *analysis* complete. But everything that the *mathematicus* says must be indubitably certain. He should therefore have said, a similar figure, which I want to think, is etc. Here, consequently, definition must always be synthetic.",[]],"wiener-s09-027":["The rules for the analytic *definition* are the following: seek true marks. You want to analyze, therefore *attend* to the marks, and seek true ones, which actually belong to the thing. One could call those propositions which are found through the *analysis* of a concept, through which I become conscious of the marks of the concept, *elementary* propositions, because they set out the *elements* for the *definition*. For although no *definition* yet comes from them: still they are the representations for it etc. E.g., if I say: virtue is a facility, a freedom in acting etc., then I here seek through the *analysis* one mark after another, in order thereby to attain to the *definition*.",[]],"wiener-s09-028":["Now we analyze the concept, and seek as many *elementary* propositions as we can. But then we must also see whether in this or that mark another does not already lie, and there one must then guard against ever positing one mark twice in the *definition*. – Now when I have sought true propositions, and have seen thereby that these marks are different marks: then I gather them all again, and compare them with the whole concept, and thus put them together again. Here now lies precisely the whole difficulty, namely the dissecting can still well be achieved, but to know whether the marks are completely there is extremely hard, and we have cause to place great mistrust in it. Since we thus cannot always assert that our *analysis* has been complete: so we must *attend* to our *definition* with fear and trembling. And since the matter is so difficult: so I would say: I will define by way of trial, I will establish a *definition*, as it were like a trial, I will not rely on it as on something free of error, and will regard it as if it were not a *definition*. Here I can then assert, even if it is no *definition*: still it must 1.) be a true proposition, 2.) be a distinct concept, even if it is not a completely distinct concept.",[]],"wiener-s09-029":["I will be able to make useful use of this presumptive definition. For I will always be able to say: *cui competit definitum, ei competit definitio* [to whatever the thing defined applies, to it the definition applies]. E.g., whoever is virtuous has a firm resolution to act. This *definitio* always applies to the *definitum* of the virtuous. But I will not be able to say conversely: *cui competit definitio, illi competit definitum* [to whatever the definition applies, to it the thing defined applies]. I cannot say conversely: whoever has a firm resolution to act is virtuous, and yet I do not call him virtuous. I thus see well that I can make for myself every direct concept from a presumptive definition, only I will not be able to convert it, because I have not named everything, and have not completely cognized all marks. I can say, e.g.: the human being is a rational animal, but not every rational animal is a human being. For something is lacking in the definition. – If I now finally believe I have all marks completely together, and then put them together: then I have to look to nothing further than that my definition be precise. This last is good and neat, but does not constitute an essential *requisitum* [requirement] of the definition, so that even without precision such concepts have their sufficient usefulness. – If I have sought the first part, namely merely true propositions in the definition: then I define *obscurum per aeque obscurum* [the obscure through the equally obscure], and one could call that tautological propositions. – With definitions, if they are otherwise only true propositions, one can accomplish much, even if it is not an exhaustive concept. But whoever wants to reciprocate must have a complete concept.",[]],"wiener-s09-030":["General rules of definition.",[]],"wiener-s09-031":["1.) One should not define through a circle. To explain through a circle means to define through a mark of the sort that presupposes the concept as *definitum*. E.g., if I define ground through a cause why something is: then my mark \"why,\" i.e., from what ground, is a mark of the sort that already signifies the concept of ground. This *circulus vitiosus* [vicious circle] occurs very frequently. One takes a mark, and without further examining whether the concept is for itself, independently of the concept, definitively clear, one explains *idem per idem* [the same through the same].",[]],"wiener-s09-032":["2.) *definitio nec sit latior, nec angustior suo definito* [let the definition be neither wider nor narrower than what it defines]. I demand a sum total of marks which, taken together, present the whole sum total. Hence one seeks no more, no fewer, than are necessary in order that *definitio* and *definitum* are reciprocable, i.e., that the one can be posited in the place of the other. E.g., if I say: a body is an impenetrable being that is heavy, then the *definitio* is *angustior definito* [narrower than the thing defined]. For through the mark of heaviness the concept of body is too restricted and narrow. For it applies only to the bodies that we know, consequently if the definition has altogether too much determination: it becomes *angustior definito*. But the more undetermined a concept is, the greater is its *sphaera*, *et latior est definitio definito* [and the definition is wider than the thing defined]. E.g., if I say: figure, then that applies to many things. If I say: a triangular figure: then that already restricts itself more etc. *Latior definito* is the concept that is not determined enough through the definition, *angustior*, on the contrary, if it is narrowed too much through so much determination.",[]],"wiener-s10-001":["Here we can be quite brief. A universal concept has a *sphaeram*, and has lower concepts under it. These lower ones we investigate insofar as the lower concepts differ from one another. Division is thus the complete representation, insofar as they are considered according to their difference, in which, taken together, they are equal to the *sphaera* of the whole concept. E.g., all beings on the earth are organized and not organized. The organized are plants or animals. Logical division is thus nothing other than the dividing up of the *sphaera* of a concept. This dividing is something other than the dividing up of a concept. In dividing I distinguish the manifold under the concept, i.e., the *sphaeram*. In dividing up I analyze the concept. The concept itself I do not dissect, but only the *sphaeram* I divide, the lower concepts, insofar as they are contained under the universal.",[]],"wiener-s10-002":["The greater the *sphaera* of a concept is, the less it contains in itself. But it contains all the more in itself, the less it has in itself. Thus in every division of a concept we divide this concept into its parts without dividing up the concept, i.e., one divides the multitude of things that are contained under the universal concept. And this will be able to be continued, there will be all the more members of the division the greater a *sphaeram* the concept has, and the smaller its *sphaera* is, the fewer members of the division there will be. Since, then, every concept, the fewer parts it has, has an all the greater *sphaeram*: it follows that a concept can be divided all the more, the fewer parts it has. E.g. We represent many things to ourselves under the concept of animals. There are animals that move on land, in the air, and in water, and the mark of all of which is motion. Here we have not divided up the concept of the animal, i.e., analyzed it. For then we would have had to say: animal is a matter that lives etc. No, we divide the concept. We see only how many species are contained under the concept. In every division we represent to ourselves, first, that a multitude of different things entirely fills the *sphaeram* of the concept, i.e., are equal to the *sphaera* of the concept, that consequently no concept is lacking as part. Second, that these different concepts, which are all supposed to be contained in the *sphaera* of the concept, are different from one another, that thus one part of the *sphaera* is not contained in others. Thus we say according to the example we have used: all animals, divided according to motion, are such as can move either on the earth, or in the air, or in water. This \"either, or\" expresses that they are different, and that one species is opposed to the other. But through the word \"all\" one expresses that the marks together constitute the concept. –",[]],"wiener-s10-003":["In every division we thus have a *sphaeram* and *membra dividentia* [dividing members]. These latter must be opposed to one another, and must also, taken together, fill the whole *sphaeram* of the *conceptus*. E.g., if I say: all human beings are either virtuous or vicious: then that will not do. For there are human beings who have no character at all, as e.g. a savage. In logic there occur many rules which it is good to indicate, so that one proves that one understands all actions of the understanding that occur in its operations. But these rules are of the kind that one can very well dispense with these rules as precepts, because everyone already observes them of himself, and afterwards can also well expound them *in abstracto*. Thus, e.g., this rule in division: the divided concept must not be wider or narrower than all the members of the division, if they can be taken together through an opposition, that is, no member must be lacking in the division. For if a member is lacking: then the concept would have a greater *sphaeram*, because I would not have enumerated all members, i.e., it would be wider. But if I say more members than there actually are: then the divided concept has a smaller *sphaeram*. Here one sees how difficult it is for a rule of the understanding to be given *in abstracto*. *In concreto*, however, everyone thinks it without being conscious of it. Hence in such cases one must not think of the rules *in abstracto*, but of the examples *in concreto* that one has before one. – The members of the division must also be opposed to one another. For otherwise I would not be able to say \"either, or.\" From this we see everything that has already been noted in the *prolegomenis* p. 14. The common and healthy understanding is very well versed in the rules. But it cognizes only *in concreto*. The speculative understanding, on the contrary, can also prove them *in abstracto*. – In mathematics such rules are quite good. But it is also a science that cannot be thought at all without abstraction. A divided concept must contradict no member of the division. E.g., if I say: all triangles are round and quadrangular. In the division the members are supposed to be contained under the concept, i.e., the concept of the division must be contained in them, which does not take place here, because neither round nor quadrangular triangles are contained under the concept of the triangle.",[]],"wiener-s10-004":["One must distinguish division, subdivision, and codivision. Division is in general representation of the manifold concepts that are opposed to one another and fill the whole *sphaeram* of the concept. This can now be continued. I can divide a concept in one *respectu* [respect], and again in another *respectu*. All are, according to character, virtuous and vicious, according to sex men and women, according to age young and old, according to knowledge learned and unlearned etc. This is codivision, where each time I divide only one given *conceptum*. But I can also subdivide the concept. For then I divide the members of the division. Every subdivision is thus a division of a *membri dividentis*. E.g., human beings are learned or unlearned. The learned are learned in reason, learned in experience. Those learned in reason are philosophers or mathematicians etc. For him who assumes *species infimas* [lowest species], the subdivisions will finally have an end. But since we have just shown, p. 373, that according to the nature of the matter every species can always still contain *species inferiores* [lower species]: we will thus never arrive at such concepts whose subordinate concepts should not be able to be divided again. The subdivision must thus go on to infinity, although of course many a subdivision *comparative* [comparatively] has an end for us.",[]],"wiener-s10-005":["Codivision also goes on to infinity. E.g., I can divide the triangle with respect to its *laterum* [sides] into equilateral, non-equilateral, with respect to its angles into right-angled and oblique-angled. More can probably not be given here. But with respect to the things of nature countlessly many codivisions can be given.",[]],"wiener-s10-006":["*Quaeritur* [it is asked]. How does all division actually proceed? Is division through our pure understanding always a pure dividing up only into two parts, and then into subdivisions, i.e., is it always a dichotomy, so that all remaining divisions are only subdivisions, or can I right at the beginning divide up a concept into more than two parts? *Resp.* [Answer] It is obvious that every immediate division is a dichotomy. If we divide into several parts: then it is polytomy, which each time is a subdivision. E.g. If I want to divide up an apple into many pieces: then I must still first divide it into two pieces. These may be different according to magnitude, or not. Afterwards I can divide it up into several parts. But those are then already subdivisions. Every immediate division is consequently a dichotomy. The dichotomy is brought about at once through the pure word: not. E.g., triangles are either equilateral or not equilateral. The equilateral are either *aequicrura* [isosceles] or *scalena* [scalene]. The mathematicians, when they divide triangles into *aequilatera*, *aequicrura* and *scalena*, have consequently brought the subdivision under the immediate division and divided falsely. But since this is so long-winded, because with many subdivisions one finally loses sight of the *conceptum divisum* [divided concept]: it is not always observed.",[]],"wiener-s11-001":["Judgment is *generaliter* [generally] the representation of unity in a relation of many cognitions. A judgment is the representation of the way in which concepts universally objectively belong to one consciousness. If one thinks two representations, how they are combined together as cognitions and together constitute one cognition: then it is a judgment. In every judgment there is thus a certain relation of different representations, insofar as they belong to one cognition. E.g., I say: the human being is not immortal. In this cognition I think the concept of being mortal through the concept of the human being, and through this it comes about that this cognition, which constitutes the unity of two different representations, becomes a judgment.",[]],"wiener-s11-002":["To every judgment belong matter and form. The matter is the cognitions *generaliter*, and the concepts, the form must constitute the combination",[]],"wiener-s11-003":["and unity of the representation. If, e.g., I say: God is just, evil is punished, and the other asked: are these rhapsodies that you are putting forward? then I would say: no! because God is just, he punishes evil, which then constitutes the form through which the cognition was brought to unity. All actions of the understanding that occur in a judgment reduce to 4 and according to these all judgments are considered.",[]],"wiener-s11-004":["Namely, first, according to their *qualitaet* [quality] they are divided into affirmative, negative, and infinite judgments. Even if the *Logici* [logicians] say that the infinite can be used as affirmative judgments: then that is a proposition that can be expounded in a particular remark. But at bottom it is, according to form, something other, and at the beginning one must divide as the difference of the action of our understanding is. But how much is thought under it belongs in a remark.",[]],"wiener-s11-005":["2.) according to *quantitaet* [quantity] our judgments are divided into universal, particular and singular judgments, and even if the *Logici* show that singular judgments, as far as the matter is concerned, are as much as universal judgments: yet a singular judgment is distinguished from the universal, and this must be distinguished at the beginning, although one can afterwards say that the singular belong to the universal. The *actus* of the understanding are obviously different, although one sees that the one use of the understanding is worth just as much as the other.",[]],"wiener-s11-006":["3.) according to relation judgments are divided up into categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive judgments.",[]],"wiener-s11-007":["4.) according to *Modalitaet* [modality] into problematic, assertoric and apodictic judgments. Each of these 4 functions thus yields a particular kind of judgments.",[]],"wiener-s11-008":["Quality is the relation of concepts insofar as they stand in relation of unity with one another; according to it they are divided into affirmative judgments, when I combine one concept with the other positively, into negative, when I separate one concept from the other, into infinite, when I limit one concept through the other. E.g., human beings are mortal, here I affirm mortality of the human being, or I think the human beings as they stand under the concept of mortality. No human being is mortal, here I deny mortality of the human being. When I think the human being, I think him as he is distinguished from all that is mortal, as e.g. *Anima non est mortalis* [the soul is not mortal] is a negative proposition. On the contrary, *anima est non mortalis* [the soul is non-mortal] is an infinite proposition. – All affirmative propositions show their affirmation through the copula *est*, which copula indicates the relation of two concepts. If the copula *est* stands *simpliciter* [simply], it signifies the connection of two – if the copula *est* is affected with the *non*: then it signifies opposition of the two concepts, and indicates that the one concept does not belong to the other, or is not contained in the *sphaera* of the other. E.g. *anima non est mortalis*, here I represent to myself that mortality does not include the soul within itself. But if I say: *anima est non mortalis*: then I say not merely that the soul contains nothing mortal, but that it is also contained in the *sphaera* of all that which is not mortal. Here something particular is thus said, namely that I not merely exclude a concept from the *sphaera* of another concept, but also think the concept under the whole remaining *sphaera* that does not belong under the concept that is excluded. I actually do not say: *est immortalis* [is immortal], but I say: among all concepts in general that may be thought outside the concept of mortality, the soul can be counted. And this actually constitutes the infinite judgments. –",[]],"wiener-s11-009":["Affirmation and negation are accordingly qualities in the judgment. A negative judgment is not every judgment that is negative, but such a negative judgment where the negation affects the copula. But such a judgment, where it does not affect the copula, but the predicate, as happens in an infinite judgment, and the copula is thus without any negation, is accordingly an affirmative judgment, consequently all infinite judgments are affirmative, because the negation affects only the predicate. But although every infinite judgment has the nature of the affirmative: yet there is always a negation there, not, to be sure, of the judgment, i.e., of the relation of the concepts, but of the predicate.",[]],"wiener-s11-010":["The relation is, to be sure, the same as in an affirmative judgment, but the negation is still always there, and consequently they are distinguished from the affirmative. This matter seems to be a subtlety in logic. But in metaphysics it becomes of importance not to have passed over it here. For there the difference between reality, negation and limitation is greater. In the case of limitations I think something positive, but not merely, but also negative, and it is something limited positive. – They are called *judicia infinita* [infinite judgments] because they are unbounded. They always say only what is not, and such predicates",[]],"wiener-s11-011":["I can make countless, for the *sphaera* of the predicates which, affected with *non*, can be said of the subject is infinite.",[]],"wiener-s11-012":["The principle of all possible *praedicatis contrarie oppositis* [contrarily opposed predicates] must come from the thing. This is the principle of thoroughgoing determination. But this thoroughgoing determination of a thing is impossible, because an infinite cognition is required to seek out all the predicates that belong to a thing, and I can therefore go on to infinity, and still not determine the thing thoroughly. E.g., the soul is corporeal, not corporeal. The soul is mortal, not mortal. In logical use they can here count as affirmative.",[]],"wiener-s11-013":["For every thing is distinguished from others through determination. All other things, affected with *non*, can be said of it. E.g., the mark of stone is hardness. Now I can always go on to infinity, and say: a stone is not metal, not wood etc. But do I thereby say something new? For what does it help that I know that everything remaining outside the concept is not stone? The *sphaera* of this all the remaining is infinite, and therefore one calls these *judicia infinita*. The quantity of judgments distinguishes them so that there are universal, particular and singular judgments. E.g. All human beings are mortal, some human beings are mortal. Caesar is mortal. Here I have *judicium universale, particulare* and *singulare*. In every *judicio singulari* the predicate holds of the subject without exception; if I say: Caesar is mortal, then no exception can take place here, because the concept Caesar is a singular concept, which does not comprise a multitude under itself, but is only a single thing, consequently it holds just as much without exception as the *judicium universale*, because, namely, it has no *Sphaeram* at all from which something could be excepted. The *Universale* holds without exception because the *sphaera* comprises everything, and consequently the *singulare judicium* is in use equal to the *Universali*. But there is still a difference there to which one must look, although in formal use both can go together.",[]],"wiener-s11-014":["The *Logici* have a custom of making the matter distinct through certain *vocales* [vowels] in the following verses:",[]],"wiener-s11-015":["*Asserit A Negat E sed universaliter ambo.* [A asserts, E denies, but both universally.]",[]],"wiener-s11-016":["*Asserit I Negat O sed particulariter ambo.* [I asserts, O denies, but both particularly.]",[]],"wiener-s11-017":["i.e., *universaliter affirmans* [universally affirming] is the *vocalis* A. E.g. All human beings are mortal. The *judicium universaliter negans* [universally negating] is to be signified through E. E.g. No human being is mortal. *Particulariter affirmans* [particularly affirming] is I. E.g., some human beings are learned, *particulariter negans* [particularly negating] is O. E.g., some human beings are not learned. This one uses afterwards in syllogistics, where unintelligible words, e.g. *celarent*, *barocco*, which signify nothing, are used in order to show through the 3 *Vocales* of the word the quality of the proposition. – Thus one also has some school sayings that one must remember. One asks: *quae, qualis, quanta est propositio?* [of what kind, of what quality, of what quantity is the proposition?] *quae propositio* refers to the relation, whether they are categorical, hypothetical or disjunctive judgments? *qualis propositio*, whether they are affirmative or negative? *quanta propositio*, whether they are universal or particular propositions? From this the quality and quantity of judgments have arisen.",[]],"wiener-s11-018":["The *autor* [author] here also treats of the condition of judgments. This is the determination of the subject in a categorical judgment, which contains the ground of the truth. E.g., if I say: a human being is punishable, then I see that without condition this does not belong at all to the concept of the human being. But if I say: a human being who is vicious is punishable: then being vicious is the determination of the subject. Properly this does not belong in logic, because logic does not occupy itself at all with the content. – Analysis of the judgment consists in the estimation of a judgment according to the 3 *modis judicandi* [modes of judging]. This was demanded in schools in former times, when one asked: *quae, qualis, quanta?*",[]],"wiener-s11-019":["According to the relation of judgments, all judgments are categorical, or hypothetical or disjunctive. The matter of all categorical propositions consists of a concept in which the concept of the subject belongs to the concept of the predicate. E.g., the human being is mortal, the subject, human being, belongs to the concept of the predicate, being mortal. – In hypothetical judgments the matter for the concept consists of two judgments. E.g., if God is just: then evil is punished. Through a *propositionem hypotheticam* [hypothetical proposition] I want to say as much as that, if the one is assumed, the other must also be assumed. One judgment holds under the condition of the other. – The *propositiones disjunctivae* [disjunctive propositions] are, according to their matter, two or more judgments, which are always considered in opposition, and of which I represent to myself that all these judgments, when they are taken together, constitute everything that can be said of the judgment. If one of them is true: then all are false. If all are false except one: then the one must be true. For because everything is thought that can be thought, and all the rest are false: then this one must indeed be true. The matter of disjunctive judgments is thus different judgments, which however are considered in opposition, so that all judgments taken together constitute the whole judgment. One sees well that they constitute merely a logical division. For if, e.g., I say: all triangles are either isosceles, or equilateral, or equiangular: then the triangle is divided up into as many members as it actually can be.",[]],"wiener-s11-020":["The form in judgments of relation is that either the relation of a subject with the predicate, or of a ground with the sequence, or the relation of two or more judgments, insofar as they *disjunctive* [disjunctively] fill the *sphaeram* of a concept, is weighed. – Categorical judgments constitute the *basin* [basis] of all the rest. Here the relation of the subject with the predicate is indicated. The hypothetical is composed of two problematic; but the disjunctive of two or more judgments. In the hypothetical judgment I consider the combination of two judgments as ground and sequence. In the disjunctive, all categorical judgments are members of the division, yet it is to be noted that the judgments in hypothetical and disjunctive judgments cannot be transformed back into categorical. – The matter of a categorical judgment consists of two concepts, the form in the relation in which the one concerns the subject, the other the predicate. E.g. All human beings are mortal. – In the hypothetical judgment the matter consists of two judgments. E.g., if the soul is corporeal: then there is no hope of the necessity of another life. The \"if\" expresses the relation. If a judgment were thought thus, without saying what is true and not true: then that is a problematic judgment, which is assumed in order to see whether the other will follow if I assume this. It is thought merely according to its possibility. I see only whether the judgment is possible, even if the thing itself is not possible, since human beings have indeed arrived at all sorts of strange propositions and have asserted them.",[]],"wiener-s11-021":["This is thus such a judgment as I do not regard as true according to modality, and is called problematic. I say: there is no other life, I just want to think this merely in order to see how the proposition that I think would stand in combination with the other. Thus the matter of hypothetical judgments consists of two propositions. Now I say: let it be with the propositions as it will, yet this much is certain, if I assume the soul is corporeal, then one cannot possibly say that it is immortal. In all hypothetical judgments there are two judgments that are thought problematically, not assertorically, and constitute the relation of a concept as ground to sequence. – One otherwise says in schools, the *autor* says it, *per thesin*, i.e., assertorically, or *per hypothesin*, i.e., problematically. *Per thesin* means to say something categorically, *per hypothesin* to say something problematically, where, however, the propositions are still categorical insofar as the relation and the *nexus* of the ground with the sequence is indicated. In all hypothetical judgments there are two problematic judgments. The form of them consists in the *consequenz* [consequence], according to which the one judgment is the sequence of the other. The ground in the hypothetical judgment is called the *antecedens*, the sequence is called the *consequens*, consequently the matter of hypothetical judgments consists of the *antecedente* and *consequente*, whereas the form is the *consequentia*, i.e., what we conclude from the other.",[]],"wiener-s11-022":["It is good if we can always have quite proper words for our concepts, and need not make do with circumlocutions. Thus judgment and proposition are actually distinguished according to the usage of speech. But if the *Logici* say: a judgment is a proposition clothed in words: then that means nothing, and this definition is good for nothing at all. For how will they be able to think judgments without words? We thus prefer to say: a judgment considers the relation of two concepts insofar as it is problematic, whereas by propositions we understand an assertoric judgment. In the judgment I test my proposition: I judge beforehand, before I assert. But in the proposition I posit, and assert something, and it is precisely in the assertion that the proposition consists . Hence we assert: the consequence of hypothetical judgments is called proposition, because this consequence is certain, and consequently a position.",[]],"wiener-s11-023":["Some think one could quite well transform hypothetical into categorical propositions. One can do it. But then it is no longer the same assertion. That means to cancel the hypothetical proposition and to posit another in its place. It does seem to be all the same whether I say: All human beings are mortal, or if something is a human being, then it is mortal. But it is different. For in the second judgment it is problematic whether something is mortal. Being mortal is not asserted categorically, but holds only then, when being human holds. Consequently it is with categorical propositions something quite other than with hypothetical. In the hypothetical proposition it is not yet asserted at all that something is, but that it is then, when something, namely the ground, is assumed.",[]],"wiener-s11-024":["But in categorical propositions there is no settled condition. They are thus judgments essentially distinguished from one another. In all hypothetical judgments I have two *modos*, a *modum ponentem et tollentem*. The *modus ponens* means, if the *antecedens* is true: then the *consequens* is also true. The *modus tollens* means, if the *consequens* is false: then the *antecedens* is also false. This occurs afterwards in syllogistics.",[]],"wiener-s11-025":["The disjunctive judgment contains the relation of different judgments insofar as they, as *membra dividentia*, are equal to the *sphaera* of a *cognitioni divisae* [divided cognition]. E.g. All triangles are, according to the angles, either right-angled, or acute-angled, or obtuse-angled. – I represent to myself the different members as they are opposed to one another, and as they, taken together, constitute the whole *sphaeram* of a *cognitio divisa*. This is in fact nothing other than a logical *division*, but in the *division* there need not be a *conceptus divisus*, but it can also be a *cognitio divisa*. E.g., if this is not the best world: then God has not been able or willed to create a better one. That is the division of the *sphaera* of the cognition that is given to me. These are problematic judgments, of which, however, it is said assertorically that they, taken together completely, constitute the *sphaera* of the concept.",[]],"wiener-s11-026":["As regards the modality of judgments, the ancients did not take the division as exactly as we, but every concept of combination they called modality. E.g., the world exists necessarily. Here for them the word \"necessarily\" was the modality. But can logic indeed judge whether a thing is necessary or not? No, for it has nothing to do with things and their necessity. Hence it can only ask whether a judgment is expressed with a necessity or not? I ask only about the necessity that is to be found in the judgment. If possibility is determined from the form; then it is a problematic judgment. If it is actually there: then it is an assertoric one. And if it is even combined with necessity: then it is an apodictic judgment. An assertoric judgment can be merely contingently true, or apodictically true. The contingently true are empirical propositions. For experience shows me only how it is, but not that it must be so. Apodictic propositions, however, are propositions *a priori*, where I at the same time cognize the necessity of them. *Judicia exponibilia* [exponible judgments]. E.g., if I say: if few human beings are learned: then I can derive from this: 1.) some human beings are learned. For few are still some. 2.) Many human beings are not learned, for few is the opposite of many. These two propositions are enclosed in one proposition, which contains an affirmation and negation, yet is expressed with the form of affirmation. These are exponible judgments.",[]],"wiener-s11-027":["Something not logical, but something aesthetic can concern the judgment. A judgment is 1.) something logical, which concerns the understanding. 2.) something sensible, which concerns the senses. E.g., if I say: the worth of virtue is the greatest: then that is logical, but the worth of virtue surpasses everything to the point of astonishment, or if I add an O or Ah!: then these additions are something aesthetic in the judgment. It would look very ridiculous if someone wanted to bring aesthetics into mathematics. Likewise he would write not purely logically, but also aesthetically, who wanted to bring mockeries into philosophy. One must distinguish these well from one another and not mix them. For the impression of the aesthetic often makes one blind with respect to the logical. When the truth of the matter is at issue, then everything must be left out that can stir up affects. One leaves that to those who want to obtain approval surreptitiously through prettily adorned expressions, and not to acquire it through grounds.",[]],"wiener-s11-028":["Practical judgments contain an ought, the necessity why something happens for some aim. Theoretical judgments contain a being, or not-being, I say whether they are in a certain aim, or not. Provable, *demonstrabel*, is a judgment that is capable of a proof. An unprovable judgment is one that is capable of no proof. There must after all be among all judgments some that are capable of no proof, and are immediately certain, i.e., are unprovable propositions. But those that still require a proof are provable. – However many propositions there may ever be that are mediately certain, there must after all in the end be some that are immediately certain. E.g., between two points I can draw only one straight line. –",[]],"wiener-s11-029":["Empty judgments. Among those that are immediately certain, some rest on *identity*, e.g., every body is extended. If I develop the concept of body: then I find that I think the concept of extension in it. Here there is thus an *identity* of body and extension. For impenetrable extension is a part of the concept of body and lies in it. –",[]],"wiener-s11-030":["If the *identity* is *explicata*: then such an analytic proposition, which rests on *identity*, is a tautological proposition. E.g., the human being is a human being. But if I say: the human being is a rational being: then the concept is *implicite* the same. Every proposition that is *implicite* identical is provable. For it can be developed according to its parts. But if a proposition is *explicite* identical: then it is entirely indemonstrable. Such a proposition can be called an empty proposition, e.g., every body is bodily. On the other hand propositions that are *implicite* identical are not empty propositions. For through them a mark that lay hidden is after all made clear. E.g., All bodies are extended. Here I need only make the *identity* distinct *per analysin*, then I see that it lies therein not tautologically, but mediately. – But it is very false, and unworthy of so great a man, when the *autor* says that in the end all our cognitions of reason rest on empty propositions. For then they would surely rest on nothing. No, they rest on propositions that are true, but *implicite* identical, and a great part rests on *analysis*, according to which I have insight into the relation between *subject* and *praedicat*. One calls propositions empty that are empty of sense, but also those that are empty of fruit and without use. Identical tautological propositions are not empty of sense, but empty of fruit. All judgments serve only to give distinct concepts of things. E.g., Every human being is a rational being. This is a distinct concept of the human being. Here I have made a mark of the concept clear, i.e., I have made it distinct through its *praedicat*, I have judged. Consequently we can have no clear marks except through a categorical judgment, and judgments consequently serve to arrive at distinct concepts. But empty is a judgment that brings forth no distinct concept. It is not empty of sense, but logically empty. Through them no use is attained, because they give no distinct concept, and do not fulfill the ends of the understanding. Battology has similarity with tautology, is the useless use of many words, where the concept with many words < breaks off >",[]],"wiener-s11-031":["In a new hand to the end on an attached sheet:",[]],"wiener-s11-032":["A system thus combination of certain cogn. which according to their nature necessarily follow upon one another. Requirements: 1) Definit., 2) *principia acroamatica* only through words not through intuition. 3) postulates. Here I know how to go to work. 4) *theoremata* capable of and requiring proofs, what can must be proved. 5) *problemata* practical propositions that are provable, they must also be. Problem 3 pieces 1) *Quaestio*, 2) *Resolutio*, 3) *Demonstratio* (is often not necessary). 6) *Scholion* elucidation or application. *Lemma* is episodic e.g. in morals God. *Lemmata* necessary. Members of the system named differently in main aim Definit. *axiomata theoremata problem principia minus principalia cor rollaria*. Difference between *theoremata* and *corrol.*? *Scholia* for interesting and embellishing only extend usefulness. *Qualitates occultae* e.g. whence generative power of animals? Through an animating power. This whence etc. tautological. So sympathy antipathy etc. Conjecture would rather a false than no ground, but hinders further investigation hence harmful. So preachers and moralists erred.",[]],"wiener-s12-001":["Inference from judgment different. mediate where still concepts that do not lie in the judgment e.g. all bodies divisible, because composite; but all bodies divisible consequently also some *consequentia immediata* because according to matter one, only form different, e.g. all human beings mortal consequently some mortal things human beings or no human being immortal Here *conclusum* and *judicium concludens* one. Also inference of the understanding to be distinguished from *ratiocinium* where *judicium* need reason because *materialiter* distinguished e.g. all human beings mortal consequently also Caius.",[]],"wiener-s12-002":["*Judicia immediata* are 1) *per judicia aequipollentia* some human beings are not7 ° virtuous consequently not all human beings are virtuous; nothing is without ground and everything has its ground are substitutions. 2) *Subalternata* also only different according to form e.g. all human beings are mortal consequently some distinguished in quantity. 3) *per judicia opposita* some human beings are learned some not different in quality; some human beings not mortal, all human beings mortal quantity and quality are a) *contradictoria* when one universally affirms or denies and the other particularly denies or affirms i.e. the proper opposition where the affirmation of the one cancels the other *affirmando unum negat alterum* and conversely. (So proofs apagogic *indirecte* only one should see whether really *contradictoria opposita*, in metaphysics one always comes up with the opposite",[]],"wiener-s12-003":["against absurdities). Many prove because opposite leads to absurdity and triumph but opponent can often do the same. b) *Subcontrarie opposita* some human beings are learned, some not Both perhaps true. Quality different, quantity one. Here one cannot infer *indirecte*. Here both can be true not both be false thus correct the inference if the one is false the other true not conversely. c) *contrarie opposita* both universal only quality different: all human beings learned no human being learned, both can be false not be true are the opposite of the preceding.",[]],"wiener-s12-004":["4) *per conversionem judiciorum* all human beings mortal consequently some mortals human beings. Quantity must be lost, for then it is called *conversio per accidens* if quantity remains it is called *simpliciter talis* e.g. no human being is without enemies etc. universal affirmative only convert *per accidens* but 2 negative also *stricte talis* – all particulars affirmative or negative can be converted *simpliciter* some human beings are learned etc.",[]],"wiener-s12-005":["5) *consequentia immediata per contrapositionem* where quality also changed all human beings are mortal something mortal is not human being. All affirmatives can be contraposed all h. b. are mortal, nothing that is not mortal is a human being. Thus with *immediatis* change concerns only form b is *quoad qualitatem* c *quoad relationem* – *contraoppositio* shows merely modality of judgments the necessity of an affirmative judgment E.g. all human beings are mortal if true necessarily what is not mortal not human being.",[]],"wiener-s13-001":["Here is *nota intermedia* Caius mortal because human being from this *Consequentia mediata* Syllogism is the truth of a judgment through the subsumption of its condition under another judgment. Necessary 1) rule *major* (*maxima*) 2) *minor* is the *Subsumtio* of the cognition under the condition of the rule, the predicate of the subsumption belongs to the rule (to subsume i.e. to cognize that something belongs under the condition of a rule) 3) predicate of the rule of the subsumption. 2 *Prämissa* [ gap in text ]. Matter premises, form conclusion (matter of a categorical 2 concepts, of a hypothetical and disjunctive, syllogisms from 2 judgments). Division is quantity for *major* always universal, quality does nothing here, not into problematic, assertoric and apodictic for *Conclusio* is always apodictic. Division 1) categorical, 2) hypothetical (where *major* expresses relation of the ground to the sequence) 3) disjunctive, where *major* expresses the members of the division. Ground of the division *relatio majoris* – *autor* however calls only categorical regular inferences but < breaks off >",[]]};
